(window.QUIZ_CHUNKS=window.QUIZ_CHUNKS||{})["e3a.3052f51c64.js"]={"25":[{"id":"sdl25_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Type 2 (Proximal) RTA & Fanconi Syndrome","batch":1,"stem":"Which defect underlies type 2 renal tubular acidosis?","choices":{"A":"Failure of alpha-intercalated cells to secrete H+ into the collecting duct lumen","B":"Aldosterone deficiency that impairs distal Na+ reabsorption and K+ secretion","C":"Loss of nephron mass that limits the total quantity of NH4+ that can be excreted","D":"Overproduction of ketoacids that exceeds the maximal renal capacity to excrete H+","E":"A lowered proximal reabsorptive threshold that lets filtered HCO3- escape"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In type 2 RTA the proximal tubule fails to reabsorb filtered HCO3- normally, which lowers the renal threshold for bicarbonate and produces renal HCO3- wasting with hyperchloremic, normal-anion-gap acidosis. Failed alpha-intercalated cell H+ secretion describes type 1, aldosterone deficiency describes type 4, reduced nephron mass describes chronic kidney disease, and ketoacid overproduction describes a high-anion-gap acidosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: RTA types are defined by where in the nephron acid-base handling fails; type 2 is the proximal HCO3- reabsorption defect. COMLEX: type 2 RTA is the RTA most often tied to a generalized proximal tubular disorder.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 -> Obj 1 — proximal HCO3- reclamation mechanics define what fails in this disorder","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Type 2 (Proximal) RTA & Fanconi Syndrome","batch":1,"stem":"Why is the metabolic acidosis of untreated type 2 RTA typically self-limiting?","choices":{"A":"Filtered HCO3- is reclaimed normally once plasma HCO3- falls below the lowered threshold","B":"Acidemia progressively raises proximal reabsorptive capacity until the normal threshold is restored","C":"Hyperventilation clears the acid load so the kidney no longer needs to excrete any H+","D":"Rising plasma K+ stimulates NH3 synthesis until enough new HCO3- is generated to normalize pH","E":"Alpha-intercalated cells upregulate H+ secretion to regenerate the HCO3- that was wasted"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Because the reabsorptive threshold is lowered, HCO3- is wasted only while plasma HCO3- remains above that threshold. As plasma HCO3- falls below it, the filtered load is once again reabsorbed completely, wasting stops, and plasma HCO3- stabilizes at a reduced steady-state level. The remaining options describe mechanisms that either do not occur or would not stop the bicarbonate wasting.","boardPrep":"Step 1: in steady state the type 2 patient has a stable, mildly to moderately low HCO3- and can acidify urine below 5.5, unlike the type 1 patient.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 -> Obj 1 — proximal HCO3- reclamation mechanics define what fails in this disorder","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Type 2 (Proximal) RTA & Fanconi Syndrome","batch":1,"stem":"Type 2 RTA is most classically associated with which generalized proximal tubular reabsorption disorder?","choices":{"A":"Liddle syndrome","B":"Fanconi syndrome","C":"Bartter syndrome","D":"Gitelman syndrome","E":"Apparent mineralocorticoid excess"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Fanconi syndrome is a generalized reabsorptive defect of the proximal tubule that wastes HCO3-, phosphate, glucose, and amino acids together, so proximal RTA is a defining feature. Liddle, Bartter, and Gitelman syndromes affect distal or loop transport and present with hypokalemic alkalosis rather than proximal RTA. Apparent mineralocorticoid excess raises mineralocorticoid receptor activity in the distal nephron and causes hypertension, hypokalemia and metabolic alkalosis, not proximal wasting.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Fanconi syndrome equals proximal RTA plus phosphaturia, glucosuria, and aminoaciduria; hypophosphatemic rickets can follow in children. COMLEX: recall the generalized PCT reabsorption defect when a vignette lists several wasted solutes.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 -> Obj 1 — proximal HCO3- reclamation mechanics define what fails in this disorder","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Type 1 (Distal) RTA & Alpha-Intercalated Cell Pump Failure","batch":1,"stem":"Which defect underlies type 1, or distal, renal tubular acidosis?","choices":{"A":"A lowered proximal reabsorptive threshold, so filtered HCO3- is wasted into the urine","B":"Aldosterone resistance with hyperkalemia that suppresses glutaminase activity","C":"Failure of alpha-intercalated cell H+ secretion, so no new HCO3- is generated","D":"Loss of nephron mass with reduced total ammonium production","E":"Excess plasma organic anions that raise the anion gap"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In type 1 RTA the alpha-intercalated cells cannot secrete H+ normally, so titratable acid and NH4+ excretion fall, no new HCO3- is generated, and the urine cannot be acidified appropriately. A lowered proximal threshold describes type 2, aldosterone resistance with hyperkalemia describes type 4, and the remaining options describe chronic kidney disease and high-anion-gap acidosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: distal RTA means no new HCO3- is made, so acidosis persists and the urine stays too alkaline. COMLEX: think autoimmune disease, obstruction, and amphotericin B as causes.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 -> Obj 2 — alpha-intercalated cell H+ secretion is the step that fails in this disorder","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"A lowered proximal threshold d"}},{"id":"sdl25_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Type 1 (Distal) RTA & Alpha-Intercalated Cell Pump Failure","batch":1,"stem":"What urine pH is characteristic of type 1 RTA despite systemic acidosis?","choices":{"A":"Persistently above 5.5, because distal H+ secretion cannot lower it further","B":"Persistently below 4.4, since distal H+ secretion is maximally stimulated","C":"Variable, rising above 5.5 after bicarbonate loading of the plasma","D":"Persistently near 7.4, because filtered HCO3- escapes reabsorption","E":"Persistently between 5.0 and 5.4, because ammonium buffering is intact"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The hallmark of type 1 RTA is an inability to acidify the urine below about pH 5.5 even in the face of systemic acidemia, because the alpha-intercalated cell H+ pump has failed. A minimum urine pH near 4.4 is the normal physiologic limit, and a variable pattern that depends on plasma HCO3- is the type 2 pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: a urine pH persistently above 5.5 in a patient with acidosis and a normal anion gap points straight to distal RTA. COMLEX: this test is inexpensive and often the first clue.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 -> Obj 2 — alpha-intercalated cell H+ secretion is the step that fails in this disorder","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"A minimum urine pH near 4.4 is"}},{"id":"sdl25_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Type 1 (Distal) RTA & Alpha-Intercalated Cell Pump Failure","batch":1,"stem":"Which laboratory pattern is characteristic of type 1 RTA?","choices":{"A":"Hyperkalemia with low plasma renin and low aldosterone levels","B":"Hypokalemia with normal anion gap metabolic acidosis","C":"Hypokalemia with a markedly increased plasma anion gap","D":"Normal serum K+ with glucosuria and phosphaturia","E":"Hypernatremia with dilute urine and marked polyuria"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Type 1 RTA produces hyperchloremic metabolic acidosis with a normal anion gap, and hypokalemia is a recognized accompaniment. Hyperkalemia with low aldosterone describes type 4, and glucosuria with phosphaturia describes the generalized proximal wasting of Fanconi syndrome. An increased anion gap points to accumulated unmeasured anions, which RTAs do not cause. Hypernatremia with persistently dilute urine and polyuria is the pattern of diabetes insipidus, not an acid-base disorder.","boardPrep":"Step 1: all RTAs share a normal anion gap; potassium classically separates type 4 (high) from types 1 and 2 (low), and urine pH then separates types 1 and 2. COMLEX: recall hypokalemia, hypocitraturia, and calcium phosphate stones as the distal RTA complications.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 -> Obj 2 — alpha-intercalated cell H+ secretion is the step that fails in this disorder","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Type 4 (Hyperkalemic) RTA & Glutaminase Inhibition","batch":1,"stem":"Which combination defines type 4 RTA?","choices":{"A":"Aldosterone deficiency or resistance, with hyperkalemia and a normal anion gap","B":"Alpha-intercalated pump failure, with hypokalemia and a urine pH above 5.5","C":"Proximal HCO3- wasting, with hypokalemia and glucosuria from generalized tubular dysfunction","D":"Excess mineralocorticoid activity, with hypokalemia and metabolic alkalosis","E":"Loss of nephron mass, with hyperkalemia and an elevated plasma anion gap"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Type 4 RTA is the hyperkalemic form, caused by hypoaldosteronism or aldosterone resistance, and hyperkalemia suppresses renal NH3 synthesis and NH4+ excretion so the acidosis has a normal anion gap. The remaining options describe type 1, type 2, primary mineralocorticoid excess, and advanced chronic kidney disease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: type 4 is the classic hyperkalemic RTA; recall low aldosterone or aldosterone resistance as the trigger. COMLEX: think diabetic nephropathy, adrenal insufficiency, and RAAS blockade.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 -> Obj 2 — aldosterone and plasma K+ govern principal cell K+ secretion, the pathway disrupted in this disorder","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"The remaining options describe","C":"The remaining options describe","E":"The remaining options describe"}},{"id":"sdl25_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Type 4 (Hyperkalemic) RTA & Glutaminase Inhibition","batch":1,"stem":"How does hyperkalemia impair renal H+ excretion in type 4 RTA?","choices":{"A":"K+ entry blocks luminal NHE3, which stops proximal HCO3- reabsorption directly","B":"Elevated plasma K+ raises aldosterone secretion, which suppresses collecting duct H+ ATPase","C":"Elevated plasma K+ inhibits carbonic anhydrase, which prevents the formation of titratable acid","D":"K+ entry stimulates phosphate reabsorption, which removes the urinary buffer for titratable acid","E":"K+ entry raises proximal cell pH, which inhibits glutaminase and lowers NH4+ synthesis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In hyperkalemia, K+ enters renal cells and H+ leaves, so intracellular and intramitochondrial pH rise. The higher pH inhibits glutaminase, so less NH3 is synthesized from glutamine and less H+ can be excreted as NH4+. The other options describe transporters or enzymes that hyperkalemia does not directly inhibit. Aldosterone stimulates rather than suppresses the collecting duct H+ ATPase, and it is deficient or blocked in type 4 RTA, and the stem's mechanism does not involve phosphate reabsorption.","boardPrep":"Step 1: hyperkalemia causes intracellular alkalinization of proximal cells, whereas hypokalemia does the reverse and stimulates NH3 synthesis. COMLEX: this explains why treating hyperkalemia can improve the acidosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 -> Obj 2 — aldosterone and plasma K+ govern principal cell K+ secretion, the pathway disrupted in this disorder","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The other options describe tra","C":"The other options describe tra"}},{"id":"sdl25_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Type 4 (Hyperkalemic) RTA & Glutaminase Inhibition","batch":1,"stem":"Which medication classically causes type 4 renal tubular acidosis?","choices":{"A":"Furosemide","B":"Hydrochlorothiazide","C":"Acetazolamide","D":"Spironolactone","E":"Amphotericin B"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Spironolactone blocks the mineralocorticoid receptor, producing aldosterone resistance with hyperkalemia and type 4 RTA. Acetazolamide causes a proximal, type 2 pattern, amphotericin B is a cause of type 1, and loop and thiazide diuretics waste K+ and tend to cause alkalosis rather than type 4 RTA.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the SHARDS mnemonic lists spironolactone among the causes of normal-anion-gap acidosis; it is the potassium-sparing cause. COMLEX: review medication lists in any patient with unexplained hyperkalemia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 -> Obj 2 — aldosterone and plasma K+ govern principal cell K+ secretion, the pathway disrupted in this disorder","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Acetazolamide causes a proxima","B":"Acetazolamide causes a proxima"}},{"id":"sdl25_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — RTA Diagnostic Algorithm: Urine pH, Serum K+, & Urinary Anion Gap","batch":1,"stem":"A patient with normal anion gap metabolic acidosis has a positive urinary anion gap. What does this suggest?","choices":{"A":"Brisk renal NH4+ excretion, pointing to diarrheal HCO3- loss","B":"A high plasma anion gap, pointing to accumulated organic acids","C":"Impaired renal NH4+ excretion, pointing to a renal tubular cause","D":"A low proximal HCO3- threshold, pointing to Fanconi syndrome","E":"Excess unmeasured urine anions, pointing to ketoacid excretion"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Urinary NH4+ is excreted with Cl-, so a low urine Cl- relative to Na+ plus K+ produces a positive gap and signals that NH4+ excretion is low. This identifies a renal defect in acid excretion, as in type 1 or type 4 RTA. Brisk NH4+ excretion produces a negative gap and points to an extrarenal source such as diarrhea. The urinary gap tracks urinary NH4+, not plasma unmeasured anions, and this patient's plasma gap is normal; and a positive gap appears in type 1, type 2 and type 4 RTA alike, so on its own it cannot point to a lowered proximal threshold.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the urinary anion gap is an indirect readout of urinary NH4+; positive means little NH4+ and a renal problem. COMLEX: use it to separate diarrhea from RTA when the history is unclear.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 -> Obj 1 — the normal-anion-gap framework is the starting point of this algorithm","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"The urinary gap tracks urinary"}},{"id":"sdl25_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — RTA Diagnostic Algorithm: Urine pH, Serum K+, & Urinary Anion Gap","batch":1,"stem":"How is the urinary anion gap calculated?","choices":{"A":"Urine Na+ plus urine K+ plus urine Cl-","B":"Urine K+ minus urine Na+ minus urine Cl-","C":"Urine Na+ minus urine Cl- minus urine HCO3-","D":"Urine Na+ plus urine K+ minus urine Cl-","E":"Urine Na+ plus urine K+ minus urine HCO3-"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The urinary anion gap equals urine Na+ plus urine K+ minus urine Cl-. NH4+ is the main unmeasured urinary cation and is excreted with Cl-, so brisk NH4+ excretion makes the gap negative. Using a sum of all three ions, altered signs, or HCO3- in place of Cl- does not produce this estimate.","boardPrep":"Step 1: memorize the formula as cations minus chloride; unlike the plasma anion gap, K+ is included and HCO3- is not. COMLEX: know both the formula and the interpretation of a positive versus negative result.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 -> Obj 1 — the normal-anion-gap framework is the starting point of this algorithm","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Using a sum of all three ions,","B":"Using a sum of all three ions,","E":"Using a sum of all three ions,"}},{"id":"sdl25_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — RTA Diagnostic Algorithm: Urine pH, Serum K+, & Urinary Anion Gap","batch":1,"stem":"Which pairing of serum K+ and urine pH best fits type 4 RTA?","choices":{"A":"Low serum K+ with urine pH above 5.5","B":"High serum K+ with urine pH below 5.5","C":"Low serum K+ with urine pH that varies with plasma HCO3-","D":"High serum K+ with urine pH above 5.5 and a negative urinary anion gap","E":"Normal serum K+ with urine pH fixed at 7.0"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In type 4 RTA hyperkalemia suppresses NH4+ synthesis, but H+ secretion is largely preserved, so the urine can still be acidified below 5.5 and the urinary anion gap is positive. Low K+ with urine above 5.5 fits type 1, and low K+ with variable urine pH fits type 2. A fixed urine pH of 7.0, or hyperkalemia with a negative urinary anion gap, does not fit a recognized RTA pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the algorithm leans mainly on two bedside numbers, serum K+ and urine pH, to sort types 1, 2, and 4. COMLEX: hyperkalemia in a normal-anion-gap acidosis should trigger the aldosterone workup.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 -> Obj 1 — the normal-anion-gap framework is the starting point of this algorithm","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Low K+ with urine above 5.5 fi"}},{"id":"sdl25_q13","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Type 2 (Proximal) RTA & Fanconi Syndrome","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old man with multiple myeloma has fatigue. Labs: Na+ 140 mEq/L (reference range: 136–146 mEq/L), K+ 3.1 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L), Cl- 112 mEq/L (reference range: 95–105 mEq/L), HCO3- 16 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L), serum glucose 92 mg/dL (reference range: 70–100 mg/dL). Urinalysis shows glucosuria, phosphaturia, aminoaciduria, and a urine pH of 5.2. Which of the following best explains his acid-base disturbance?","choices":{"A":"Generalized proximal tubular reabsorptive failure that wastes filtered HCO3-","B":"Failure of alpha-intercalated cells to secrete H+ against a steep luminal gradient","C":"Accumulation of unmeasured organic anions from excess fixed acid production","D":"Gastrointestinal loss of HCO3- from chronic secretory diarrhea","E":"Loss of nephron mass that limits total ammonium excretion"},"correct":"A","explanation":"His anion gap is 140 - 128 = 12 mEq/L, which is normal, so this is a hyperchloremic acidosis. Glucosuria with normal serum glucose, phosphaturia, and aminoaciduria show a generalized proximal reabsorptive defect, which is Fanconi syndrome, and HCO3- wasting from the same defect produces type 2 RTA. An acidic urine pH argues against failed distal H+ secretion, and there is no organic acid load or diarrhea. Loss of nephron mass limits NH4+ excretion but does not cause glucosuria, phosphaturia and aminoaciduria, and chronic kidney disease tends to raise the anion gap.","boardPrep":"Step 1: multiple myeloma light chains are a classic acquired cause of Fanconi syndrome; wasted glucose, phosphate, amino acids, and HCO3- together mean proximal tubule failure. COMLEX: normal serum glucose with glucosuria is the vignette clue.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 1 — the proximal HCO3- reclamation machinery is what fails in this generalized defect","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"An acidic urine pH argues agai","C":"An acidic urine pH argues agai","D":"An acidic urine pH argues agai"}},{"id":"sdl25_q14","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Type 2 (Proximal) RTA & Fanconi Syndrome","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old man with untreated type 2 RTA has a plasma HCO3- of 14 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L) and a morning urine pH of 5.0. He receives an intravenous bicarbonate infusion that raises his plasma HCO3- into the normal range. Which urinary change is most likely afterward?","choices":{"A":"Urine stays acidic, with pH below 5.5, as the proximal tubule reclaims the added HCO3- load","B":"Urine turns alkaline, with pH above 5.5, as filtered HCO3- exceeds reabsorptive capacity","C":"Urine pH stays fixed above 5.5 before and after loading, from a distal acidification defect","D":"Urine HCO3- excretion stays negligible because the collecting duct reclaims the excess load","E":"Urine turns markedly acidic, with pH below 4.4, as distal H+ secretion rises in compensation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"At a plasma HCO3- of 14 mEq/L the filtered load is at or below the lowered threshold, so HCO3- is fully reabsorbed and the urine can be acidified. When plasma HCO3- is raised toward normal, the filtered load exceeds the reduced proximal reabsorptive capacity, HCO3- spills into the urine, and the urine pH rises above 5.5. His baseline urine pH of 5.0 excludes a fixed distal defect.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the variable urine pH, acidic when plasma HCO3- is low and alkaline when the filtered load exceeds threshold, is the signature of type 2 RTA. COMLEX: this explains why bicarbonate therapy is difficult in proximal RTA.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 1 — the filtered load saturation of proximal HCO3- reabsorption explains the pH shift","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"His baseline urine pH of 5.0 e"}},{"id":"sdl25_q15","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Type 2 (Proximal) RTA & Fanconi Syndrome","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old woman taking acetazolamide for glaucoma develops fatigue. Labs show Na+ 139 mEq/L (reference range: 136–146 mEq/L), Cl- 111 mEq/L (reference range: 95–105 mEq/L), HCO3- 17 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L), K+ 3.4 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L), and urine pH of 5.1. Which of the following best explains her acidosis?","choices":{"A":"Inhibited alpha-intercalated cell H+ ATPase that blocks titratable acid excretion","B":"Inhibited mineralocorticoid receptors that lower distal K+ and H+ secretion","C":"Inhibited proximal carbonic anhydrase that reduces HCO3- reabsorption","D":"Inhibited thick ascending limb NKCC2 that causes volume contraction with alkalosis","E":"Inhibited mitochondrial glutaminase that blunts proximal NH4+ synthesis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Acetazolamide inhibits brush border and intracellular carbonic anhydrase, which impairs conversion of luminal H2CO3 to CO2 and water and therefore blocks proximal HCO3- reabsorption, mimicking type 2 RTA with a normal-anion-gap, hypokalemic acidosis. Her acidic urine pH fits the steady-state proximal pattern rather than a distal acidification defect. The other options describe drug or disease effects that would give hyperkalemia, alkalosis, or a persistently alkaline urine.","boardPrep":"Step 1: \"acid\"azolamide causes acidosis; carbonic anhydrase inhibition equals a drug-induced proximal RTA. COMLEX: watch for this in glaucoma and altitude-sickness patients.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 1 — luminal carbonic anhydrase is the enzymatic step this drug inhibits","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Her acidic urine pH fits the s","B":"The other options describe dru","D":"The other options describe dru"}},{"id":"sdl25_q16","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Type 1 (Distal) RTA & Alpha-Intercalated Cell Pump Failure","batch":2,"stem":"A 29-year-old woman with systemic lupus erythematosus has muscle weakness. Labs: Na+ 138 mEq/L (reference range: 136–146 mEq/L), K+ 2.9 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L), Cl- 112 mEq/L (reference range: 95–105 mEq/L), HCO3- 14 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L). Urine pH is 6.6 despite the acidemia. Which of the following best explains her acidosis?","choices":{"A":"Proximal HCO3- wasting that exceeds a lowered reabsorptive threshold","B":"Failed alpha-intercalated H+ ATPase that prevents urinary acidification","C":"Hypoaldosteronism with hyperkalemic suppression of ammonium synthesis","D":"Accumulation of lactate from tissue hypoperfusion that raises the plasma anion gap","E":"Gastrointestinal HCO3- loss from chronic diarrhea with volume depletion"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Her anion gap is 138 - 126 = 12 mEq/L, which is normal, and the persistent urine pH of 6.6 despite an HCO3- of 14 mEq/L shows she cannot acidify her urine. With hypokalemia, this is type 1 RTA, and autoimmune disease such as lupus is a recognized cause. Hyperkalemia is absent, so type 4 is excluded, and the normal gap rules out lactate.","boardPrep":"Step 1: distal RTA causes include autoimmune disease, amphotericin B, obstruction, and analgesic nephropathy. COMLEX: a urine pH above 5.5 in a patient with acidosis is the vignette clue.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 2 — this is the alpha-intercalated cell pump the disease targets","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Hyperkalemia is absent, so typ","D":"Hyperkalemia is absent, so typ"}},{"id":"sdl25_q17","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Type 1 (Distal) RTA & Alpha-Intercalated Cell Pump Failure","batch":2,"stem":"A 41-year-old man with chronic distal RTA develops recurrent flank pain, and imaging shows medullary calcifications. Which combination of urinary abnormalities best explains his risk of calcium phosphate deposition?","choices":{"A":"Acidic urine with low citrate excretion","B":"Acidic urine with high citrate excretion","C":"Alkaline urine with high citrate excretion","D":"Alkaline urine with low citrate excretion","E":"Acidic urine with normal citrate excretion"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In type 1 RTA the urine stays persistently alkaline, and acidosis lowers urinary citrate, a natural inhibitor of calcium crystal formation. Calcium phosphate is insoluble at high urine pH, so the combination of alkaline urine and hypocitraturia promotes calcium phosphate stones and nephrocalcinosis. The other combinations either describe acidic urine, which is not the pattern of distal RTA since failure to acidify defines it, or preserve citrate.","boardPrep":"Step 1: distal RTA equals calcium phosphate stones, nephrocalcinosis, and hypokalemia. COMLEX: alkali therapy corrects acidosis and restores citrate excretion.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 2 — persistent failure of urinary acidification at the alpha-intercalated cell drives this stone risk","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q18","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Type 1 (Distal) RTA & Alpha-Intercalated Cell Pump Failure","batch":2,"stem":"A 25-year-old woman receiving amphotericin B develops normal anion gap metabolic acidosis with hypokalemia. Which pattern of urinary H+ excretion best describes her disorder?","choices":{"A":"Reduced excretion of H+ as NH4+ with normal titratable acid, and urine pH below 5.5","B":"Reduced excretion of H+ as titratable acid with an adaptive NH4+ rise, and urine pH below 4.4","C":"Normal excretion of both urinary buffers, with a lowered proximal HCO3- threshold","D":"Increased excretion of both urinary buffers, with urine pH near 4.4","E":"Reduced excretion of H+ as both titratable acid and NH4+, with urine pH above 5.5"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Amphotericin B is a recognized cause of type 1 RTA. Because alpha-intercalated cell H+ secretion fails, both titratable acid and NH4+ excretion fall, no new HCO3- is generated, and the urine pH stays above 5.5. A lowered proximal HCO3- threshold describes type 2 rather than the classic amphotericin B lesion, an NH4+-only defect with urine pH below 5.5 is type 4, and normal or increased excretion of both buffers would be the appropriate renal response to acidosis, not a cause of it.","boardPrep":"Step 1: in type 1 RTA both urinary buffer routes fail, whereas in type 4 the defect is confined to ammonium. COMLEX: amphotericin B, lithium, and autoimmune disease are recurrent distal RTA triggers.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 3 — the titratable acid versus ammonium comparison in acidosis frames this pattern","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q19","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Type 4 (Hyperkalemic) RTA & Glutaminase Inhibition","batch":2,"stem":"A 63-year-old man with long-standing type 2 diabetes and mild CKD has Na+ 138 mEq/L (reference range: 136–146 mEq/L), K+ 5.9 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L), Cl- 110 mEq/L (reference range: 95–105 mEq/L), and HCO3- 17 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L). Renin and aldosterone are both low, and urine pH is 5.1. Which change in his renal NH4+ excretion is most likely, and why?","choices":{"A":"Decreased, because hyperkalemia raises proximal cell pH and inhibits glutaminase","B":"Decreased, because failed H+ secretion in alpha-intercalated cells blocks distal buffering","C":"Increased, because chronic acidosis induces glutaminase in proximal cells","D":"Increased, because hyperkalemia lowers proximal cell pH and stimulates NH3 synthesis","E":"Unchanged, because NH4+ excretion depends on titratable acid formation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The pattern of hyperkalemia, low aldosterone, and a normal anion gap of 138 - 127 = 11 mEq/L with an acidic urine identifies type 4 RTA. Hyperkalemia moves K+ into proximal cells and H+ out, raising cell pH and inhibiting glutaminase, so NH3 synthesis and NH4+ excretion fall. Urine pH remains 5.1, so the distal pump is intact.","boardPrep":"Step 1: hyporeninemic hypoaldosteronism in diabetic kidney disease is the most common clinical setting of type 4 RTA. COMLEX: pair hyperkalemia with mild acidosis in a diabetic patient.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 1 — plasma K+ regulates proximal NH3 synthesis through intracellular pH","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Urine pH remains 5.1, so the d"}},{"id":"sdl25_q20","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Type 4 (Hyperkalemic) RTA & Glutaminase Inhibition","batch":2,"stem":"A 64-year-old man with diabetic nephropathy and type 4 RTA has a serum HCO3- of 17 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L) and a urine pH of 5.0. Which of the following best explains why his acidosis persists despite the acidic urine?","choices":{"A":"Distal H+ secretion is absent, but proximal HCO3- reabsorption remains preserved","B":"Distal H+ secretion is intact, but bicarbonaturia from a lowered threshold offsets it","C":"Distal H+ secretion is intact, but low NH4+ buffering limits total H+ excretion","D":"Distal H+ secretion is excessive, but respiratory compensation has failed","E":"Titratable acid excretion is absent, but hyperkalemia markedly raises NH4+ excretion"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Urine pH reflects free H+ concentration, not the total quantity of H+ excreted. In type 4 RTA the alpha-intercalated cell H+ pump works, so urine can be acidified below 5.5, but suppressed NH3 synthesis leaves little NH4+ to buffer the H+ and total acid excretion falls. Hyperkalemia lowers rather than raises NH4+ excretion, and bicarbonaturia belongs to type 2 RTA. Excessive distal H+ secretion would correct the acidosis rather than sustain it, and the problem lies in renal acid excretion, not respiratory compensation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: distinguish urine pH from total acid excretion, since a low pH can coexist with a poor net acid load. COMLEX: this distinction is a standard trap in RTA vignettes.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 3 — urine pH reflects free H+ while buffer availability governs total excretion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"In type 4 RTA the alpha-interc","B":"Hyperkalemia lowers rather tha"}},{"id":"sdl25_q21","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Type 4 (Hyperkalemic) RTA & Glutaminase Inhibition","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old man with type 4 RTA from diabetic nephropathy is started on a low-potassium diet and a loop diuretic. Over two weeks his serum K+ falls from 5.8 to 4.2 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L). Which change in renal ammonium metabolism is expected?","choices":{"A":"Falling NH3 synthesis, since lower K+ removes the stimulus for glutaminase induction","B":"Unchanged NH3 synthesis, since glutaminase activity is set by aldosterone levels","C":"Rising NH3 synthesis, since loop diuretics directly induce mitochondrial glutaminase","D":"Rising NH3 synthesis, as correcting hyperkalemia reverses proximal cell alkalinization","E":"Falling NH3 synthesis, since volume loss raises proximal HCO3- reabsorption"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Correcting hyperkalemia lets H+ re-enter proximal cells as K+ leaves, so intracellular and intramitochondrial pH fall back toward normal and glutaminase inhibition is lifted, restoring NH3 and NH4+ production and improving acid excretion. Glutaminase responds to intracellular pH, which is shaped by plasma K+ and by acidosis, not directly by loop diuretics or aldosterone.","boardPrep":"Step 1: treating hyperkalemia often improves type 4 acidosis because ammonium synthesis recovers. COMLEX: loop diuretics and dietary K+ restriction are standard first steps.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 1 — hypokalemia stimulates and hyperkalemia inhibits the glutaminase pathway","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Glutaminase responds to intrac","C":"Glutaminase responds to intrac"}},{"id":"sdl25_q22","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — RTA Diagnostic Algorithm: Urine pH, Serum K+, & Urinary Anion Gap","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman has fatigue. Labs: Na+ 137 mEq/L (reference range: 136–146 mEq/L), K+ 2.8 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L), Cl- 112 mEq/L (reference range: 95–105 mEq/L), HCO3- 13 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L). Urine pH is 6.5, and urine Na+ 40, K+ 30, and Cl- 35 mEq/L (reference range: varies with intake). Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Type 1 renal tubular acidosis","B":"Type 2 renal tubular acidosis","C":"Type 4 renal tubular acidosis","D":"Diarrheal bicarbonate loss","E":"Chronic kidney disease stage 4"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Her plasma anion gap is 137 - 125 = 12 mEq/L, which is normal, and her urinary anion gap is 40 + 30 - 35 = +35 mEq/L, which is positive and indicates poor NH4+ excretion. Hypokalemia with a persistently alkaline urine pH of 6.5 despite acidemia identifies type 1 RTA. Diarrhea would give a negative urinary gap, hyperkalemia would appear in type 4, and type 2 would typically acidify the urine at this level of acidemia. Advanced chronic kidney disease tends to raise the anion gap through retained anions and to cause hyperkalemia, not a K+ of 2.8 mEq/L with a normal gap.","boardPrep":"Step 1: apply the three-step algorithm, normal anion gap, then serum K+, then urine pH and urinary anion gap. COMLEX: low K+ plus urine pH above 5.5 equals distal RTA.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 1 — a normal-anion-gap acidosis is the entry point to the RTA algorithm","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q23","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — RTA Diagnostic Algorithm: Urine pH, Serum K+, & Urinary Anion Gap","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old man has a normal anion gap metabolic acidosis with serum HCO3- of 15 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L). Urine Na+ is 20, K+ 25, and Cl- 70 mEq/L (reference range: varies with intake). Which interpretation of his urinary anion gap is most accurate?","choices":{"A":"Impaired renal NH4+ excretion, pointing to distal renal tubular acidosis","B":"Impaired renal NH4+ excretion, pointing to hypoaldosteronism with hyperkalemia","C":"Excess unmeasured plasma anions, pointing to ketoacid accumulation","D":"Aldosterone excess, pointing to primary hyperaldosteronism with alkalosis","E":"Brisk renal NH4+ excretion, pointing to an extrarenal source of HCO3- loss"},"correct":"E","explanation":"His urinary anion gap is 20 + 25 - 70 = -25 mEq/L, which is negative. A negative gap indicates that abundant NH4+ is being excreted with Cl-, so the kidney is responding to the acid load appropriately and the HCO3- loss is extrarenal, as in diarrhea. A positive gap would suggest impaired NH4+ excretion from type 1 or type 4 RTA. The urinary gap reflects urinary NH4+, not plasma unmeasured anions, and his plasma gap is normal; and aldosterone excess causes metabolic alkalosis, not this acidosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: a negative urinary gap means brisk NH4+ excretion and intact distal acidification, pointing away from type 1 or type 4 RTA; a positive gap means impaired NH4+ excretion. COMLEX: this is the standard way to tell diarrhea from distal RTA.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 1 — diarrhea and RTA are the two major normal-anion-gap causes to be separated","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"A positive gap would suggest i","B":"A positive gap would suggest i"}},{"id":"sdl25_q24","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — RTA Diagnostic Algorithm: Urine pH, Serum K+, & Urinary Anion Gap","batch":2,"stem":"A 38-year-old woman has muscle weakness and a hypokalemic, normal anion gap metabolic acidosis, and her physician is deciding between type 1 and type 2 RTA. Which finding best separates the two?","choices":{"A":"Generalized glucose and phosphate wasting in type 1 but not in type 2","B":"A urine pH above 5.5 in type 1 even when plasma HCO3- is markedly reduced","C":"A raised plasma anion gap in type 2 but a normal plasma anion gap in type 1","D":"Hyperchloremia in type 1 but a normal plasma Cl- concentration in type 2","E":"Preserved respiratory compensation in type 2 but absent compensation in type 1"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In type 1 RTA the distal H+ pump fails, so the urine remains above pH 5.5 even at a very low plasma HCO3-. In type 2 RTA, once plasma HCO3- has fallen below the lowered proximal threshold, the urine can be acidified below 5.5. Both types cause hyperchloremic normal-anion-gap acidosis with intact respiratory compensation, and glucose and phosphate wasting belongs to the proximal disorder, not the distal one.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the type 1 versus type 2 decision rests on whether the urine can be acidified at a low plasma HCO3-. COMLEX: a bicarbonate loading test can distinguish them further.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 3 — the limits of urinary acidification distinguish distal from proximal defects","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Both types cause hyperchloremi"}},{"id":"grid_sdl25_q01","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — RTA Diagnostic Algorithm: Urine pH, Serum K+, & Urinary Anion Gap","batch":4,"stem":"A 41-year-old woman with Sjögren syndrome comes to the physician because of fatigue. Over the past 2 years she has passed two calcium phosphate kidney stones. She has had no vomiting or diarrhea and takes no laxatives, diuretics, or other medications. Blood pressure is 118/74 mm Hg. Laboratory studies show a serum sodium of 139 mEq/L (reference range: 136–146 mEq/L), chloride of 114 mEq/L (reference range: 95–105 mEq/L), and bicarbonate of 15 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L); arterial pH is 7.30 (reference range: 7.35–7.45). Which of the following sets of additional findings is most likely in this patient?","grid":{"headers":["Urine pH","Serum K⁺","Urinary NH₄⁺ excretion"]},"choices":{"A":["> 5.5","↓","↓"],"B":["< 5.5","↑","↓"],"C":["< 5.5","↓","↑"],"D":["> 5.5","↑","↓"],"E":["< 5.5","↑","↑"]},"correct":"A","explanation":"A normal anion gap metabolic acidosis (anion gap 139 − 114 − 15 = 10 mEq/L) with calcium phosphate stones in a woman with Sjögren syndrome and no gastrointestinal losses is distal (type 1) renal tubular acidosis, so the pattern (urine pH > 5.5, ↓ serum K⁺, ↓ urinary NH₄⁺ excretion) is correct. α-Intercalated cells fail to secrete H⁺, so the urine stays above pH 5.5 despite systemic acidemia; without secreted H⁺, NH₃ is not trapped as NH₄⁺ in the collecting-duct lumen, so ammonium excretion is low; and K⁺ is wasted in the urine, so serum K⁺ falls. Acidemia shifts some K⁺ out of cells, but renal K⁺ wasting in distal RTA outweighs it, so serum K⁺ is low. The pattern (urine pH < 5.5, ↑ serum K⁺, ↓ urinary NH₄⁺ excretion) is type 4 RTA: hypoaldosteronism causes hyperkalemia, which raises proximal tubule cell pH and inhibits glutaminase, so ammoniagenesis falls while the urine can still be acidified. The pattern (urine pH < 5.5, ↓ serum K⁺, ↑ urinary NH₄⁺ excretion) is diarrhea: intact kidneys answer the HCO₃⁻ loss by acidifying the urine and markedly increasing ammonium excretion, while K⁺ is lost in stool. The pattern (urine pH > 5.5, ↑ serum K⁺, ↓ urinary NH₄⁺ excretion) keeps the alkaline urine and low ammonium of a distal defect but adds hyperkalemia, the pattern of hyperkalemic (voltage-dependent) distal RTA, as from obstructive uropathy; the autoimmune distal RTA of Sjögren syndrome lowers serum K⁺. The pattern (urine pH < 5.5, ↑ serum K⁺, ↑ urinary NH₄⁺ excretion) is the response of normal kidneys to an ingested mineral acid such as NH₄Cl: maximal acidification and ammonium excretion, with K⁺ shifted out of cells.","boardPrep":"Step 1: in a normal anion gap acidosis, urine pH above 5.5 with hypokalemia points to type 1 RTA and urine pH below 5.5 with hyperkalemia to type 4; type 1 also causes calcium phosphate stones. COMLEX: urinary NH₄⁺ separates a renal cause from diarrhea: it is low in types 1 and 4 RTA and high when healthy kidneys respond to acidemia.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 2 — collecting-duct diffusion trapping needs secreted H⁺ to convert NH₃ to NH₄⁺","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex3t_sdl25_q1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Type 2 (Proximal) RTA & Fanconi Syndrome","batch":3,"stem":"A 24-year-old woman with Wilson disease has bone pain and glucosuria with a normal serum glucose. Serum HCO3- is 15 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L) and phosphate 2.2 mg/dL (reference range: 3.0–4.5 mg/dL), with a normal anion gap. Her HCO3- has been stable for weeks, and her urine pH is 5.3. Which mechanism best explains why her HCO3- has stopped falling?","choices":{"A":"Respiratory compensation that restores plasma HCO3-","B":"Saturation of a normal proximal HCO3- transport capacity","C":"A filtered HCO3- load within her reduced capacity","D":"Hyperkalemia that suppresses proximal ammoniagenesis","E":"Unmeasured anions that accumulate in place of HCO3-"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Normoglycemic glucosuria with hypophosphatemia and a normal anion gap acidosis indicates generalized proximal tubule failure, which Wilson disease can cause, with a lowered renal threshold for HCO3-. Bicarbonate is wasted only while plasma HCO3- exceeds that threshold; as plasma HCO3- falls, the filtered load (GFR × plasma HCO3-) falls with it until the impaired proximal tubule can reclaim virtually all of it, so wasting stops and intact distal pumps acidify the urine to pH 5.3. Respiratory compensation lowers PaCO2 and does not restore plasma HCO3-. A normal proximal tubule reclaims filtered HCO3- up to a threshold of about 24 mEq/L (Exam 3 notes; Costanzo places saturation above 40 mEq/L), far above her value of 15, so a normal tubule would not be wasting HCO3- here. Hyperkalemic suppression of ammoniagenesis is the type 4 mechanism, whereas Fanconi syndrome brings hypokalemia. Unmeasured anions would raise the anion gap, which is normal. Sources: Exam 3 lecture notes (SDL 25, self-limiting HCO3- wasting in type 2 RTA and Fanconi syndrome); Costanzo Physiology Ch. 7 (effect of filtered load of HCO3- and the plasma anion gap).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Proximal (type 2) RTA is self-limiting, so plasma HCO3- settles near the lowered threshold and the urine becomes acidic at steady state. COMLEX: Glucosuria with a normal blood glucose plus phosphaturia points to Fanconi syndrome, with causes such as cystinosis, Wilson disease, heavy metals, myeloma light chains, and expired tetracycline.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 -> Obj 1 — proximal Na+-H+ exchange and carbonic anhydrase normally reclaim nearly all filtered HCO3-","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex3t_sdl25_q2","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Type 1 (Distal) RTA & Alpha-Intercalated Cell Pump Failure","batch":3,"stem":"A 36-year-old woman has recurrent flank pain, and CT shows clustered calcifications in both renal medullae. Serum Na+ is 141 mEq/L (reference range: 136–146 mEq/L), K+ 3.0 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L), Cl- 117 mEq/L (reference range: 95–105 mEq/L), and HCO3- 14 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L). Her urine pH is 6.8 on repeated samples. Which urinary change most directly promotes her calcium deposits?","choices":{"A":"High oxalate from ethylene glycol metabolism","B":"High cystine from a proximal amino acid transporter defect","C":"Low calcium from increased distal calcium reabsorption","D":"High uric acid from increased cell turnover","E":"Low citrate from enhanced proximal citrate reabsorption"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A normal anion gap acidosis, 141 − (14 + 117) = 10 mEq/L, with hypokalemia and a urine pH that stays above 5.5 despite acidemia indicates failed H+ secretion by alpha-intercalated cells (type 1 RTA). Chronic acidemia drives the proximal tubule to reabsorb filtered citrate maximally, and because urinary citrate normally chelates free calcium, hypocitraturia, together with bone-buffering hypercalciuria and an alkaline urine that favors HPO4 2-, precipitates calcium phosphate in the medulla. High urine oxalate from ethylene glycol produces calcium oxalate stones and is not part of distal proton-pump failure. High urine cystine reflects a hereditary transporter defect and would not explain her acid-base findings. Urinary calcium is increased, not decreased, by acidemic bone buffering. Uric acid stones form in acidic urine, the opposite of her urine pH of 6.8. Sources: Exam 3 lecture notes (SDL 25, hypocitraturia and calcium phosphate nephrocalcinosis in type 1 RTA); Costanzo Physiology Ch. 7 (type 1 renal tubular acidosis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Distal RTA forms calcium phosphate stones because alkaline urine, hypercalciuria, and hypocitraturia converge. COMLEX: Medullary nephrocalcinosis in a young adult with hypokalemic normal-gap acidosis should prompt a urine pH check during the acidemia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 -> Obj 2 — alpha-intercalated cell H+ secretion is the step that normally acidifies the urine","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Urinary calcium is increased, "}},{"id":"ex3t_sdl25_q3","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Type 1 (Distal) RTA & Alpha-Intercalated Cell Pump Failure","batch":3,"stem":"A 47-year-old woman with Sjögren syndrome has 2 weeks of muscle weakness. Serum Na+ is 138 mEq/L (reference range: 136–146 mEq/L), K+ 2.6 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L), Cl- 113 mEq/L (reference range: 95–105 mEq/L), and HCO3- 13 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L). Her urine pH is 6.9. Which mechanism contributes to her low serum potassium?","choices":{"A":"Acidemia shifting potassium into skeletal muscle","B":"Enhanced H+-K+-ATPase reabsorption of potassium","C":"Reduced potassium filtration from a falling GFR","D":"Aldosterone excess after ECF volume contraction","E":"Insulin release driving potassium into muscle cells"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A normal anion gap acidosis, 138 − (13 + 113) = 12 mEq/L, with a urine pH of 6.9 during acidemia identifies distal (type 1) RTA, and autoimmune destruction of alpha-intercalated cells in Sjögren syndrome is a lecture-listed cause. In this disorder mild ECF volume contraction activates secondary hyperaldosteronism, which accelerates K+ secretion by principal cells, while impaired H+-K+-ATPase exchange reduces K+ reabsorption; together they produce hypokalemia severe enough to cause weakness. In a mineral acidosis, H+ enters cells in exchange for K+, which raises rather than lowers serum K+. H+-K+-ATPase activity is impaired, not enhanced, in this disorder. A falling GFR would raise, not lower, serum K+. Nothing here increases insulin release. Sources: Exam 3 lecture notes (SDL 25, hypokalemia in type 1 RTA).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Sjögren syndrome and SLE are classic autoimmune causes of distal (type 1) RTA, which presents with hypokalemia and a urine pH above 5.5. COMLEX: Check K+ and urine pH in a patient with Sjögren syndrome who develops weakness or kidney stones.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 -> Obj 2 — aldosterone raises principal cell K+ secretion through ROMK","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"A falling GFR would raise, not"}},{"id":"ex3t_sdl25_q4","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Type 4 (Hyperkalemic) RTA & Glutaminase Inhibition","batch":3,"stem":"A 74-year-old man with hypertension treated with lisinopril has routine laboratory studies. Serum Na+ is 137 mEq/L (reference range: 136–146 mEq/L), K+ 6.0 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L), Cl- 111 mEq/L (reference range: 95–105 mEq/L), and HCO3- 17 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L). His urine pH is 5.1 during the acidemia. Which factor most directly limits his renal acid excretion?","choices":{"A":"Too little NH3 to trap secreted H+ as NH4+","B":"Proton pumps unable to lower urine pH below 5.5","C":"Too little filtered phosphate for titratable acid","D":"Proximal HCO3- loss at a lowered threshold","E":"Unmeasured organic anions accumulating in plasma"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Hyperkalemia with a normal anion gap acidosis, 137 − (17 + 111) = 9 mEq/L, fits type 4 RTA, and ACE inhibitors are a recognized cause of hypoaldosteronism. The high plasma K+ drives K+ into proximal tubule cells in exchange for H+, producing intracellular and intramitochondrial alkalosis that inhibits glutaminase, so little NH3 is synthesized and too little buffer reaches the collecting duct to excrete H+ as NH4+. His urine pH of 5.1 shows that the proton pumps can still lower urine pH below 5.5. Phosphate buffering is not the defect; titratable acid normally carries the smaller share of acid excretion. Proximal HCO3- wasting does not explain hyperkalemia. Organic anion accumulation would raise the anion gap, which is normal. Sources: Exam 3 lecture notes (SDL 25, hyperkalemia-induced glutaminase inhibition in type 4 RTA); Costanzo Physiology Ch. 7 (effect of plasma K+ on NH3 synthesis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: In type 4 RTA the urine pH can fall below 5.5, but acid excretion is limited because hyperkalemia suppresses NH3 synthesis. COMLEX: ACE inhibitors, ARBs, NSAIDs, heparin, and K+-sparing drugs are medication-list causes of hyperkalemic normal-gap acidosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 -> Obj 1 — proximal glutaminase converts glutamine to NH4+ and new HCO3-","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Proximal HCO3- wasting does no"}},{"id":"ex3t_sdl25_q5","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Type 4 (Hyperkalemic) RTA & Glutaminase Inhibition","batch":3,"stem":"A 71-year-old man has taken trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole for 5 days. Serum Na+ is 136 mEq/L (reference range: 136–146 mEq/L), K+ 6.1 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L), Cl- 109 mEq/L (reference range: 95–105 mEq/L), HCO3- 18 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L), creatinine 1.0 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL), and glucose 92 mg/dL (reference range: 70–100 mg/dL). Which change best explains his rise in serum potassium?","choices":{"A":"Excess aldosterone action on principal cells","B":"Reduced potassium filtration from a falling GFR","C":"Insulin deficiency limiting cellular K+ uptake","D":"Hyperosmolar water exit dragging K+ from cells","E":"Reduced ENaC sodium entry that blunts K+ secretion"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Trimethoprim blocks principal cell ENaC directly, as amiloride does, producing renal mineralocorticoid resistance, the mechanism of hyperkalemic (type 4) RTA, which matches his hyperkalemic normal anion gap acidosis, 136 − (18 + 109) = 9 mEq/L. Loss of effective aldosterone action in the late distal tubule and cortical collecting duct reduces apical ENaC Na+ entry and basolateral Na+-K+-ATPase activity in principal cells, which blunts K+ secretion and raises serum K+. Aldosterone-dependent Na+ entry is blocked here rather than enhanced, and excess aldosterone action would cause hypokalemia. His creatinine is normal, so a falling GFR does not explain the K+. His normal glucose argues against insulin deficiency and against hyperosmolar water exit from cells, the two mechanisms behind the hyperkalemia of uncontrolled diabetes. Sources: Exam 3 lecture notes (SDL 25, hypoaldosteronism and mineralocorticoid resistance in type 4 RTA); Costanzo Physiology Ch. 7 (insulin and hyperosmolarity in K+ shifts).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Trimethoprim, amiloride, and spironolactone cause hyperkalemia by blocking aldosterone-dependent Na+ entry and K+ secretion in principal cells. COMLEX: Recheck K+ in older adults started on trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole, especially alongside ACE inhibitors.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 -> Obj 3 — ENaC blockers and mineralocorticoid receptor antagonists both spare K+","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Aldosterone-dependent Na+ entr","B":"His creatinine is normal, so a"}},{"id":"ex3t_sdl25_q6","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — RTA Diagnostic Algorithm: Urine pH, Serum K+, & Urinary Anion Gap","batch":3,"stem":"A 30-year-old woman has a normal anion gap metabolic acidosis with K+ 3.2 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L) and HCO3- 15 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L). Her urine pH is 5.3, and her urinary anion gap is positive. When intravenous bicarbonate raises her plasma HCO3- to normal, her fractional excretion of HCO3- is 22% (normally less than 1%). Which process best explains her acidosis?","choices":{"A":"Intestinal HCO3- loss with intact NH4+ output","B":"Proximal HCO3- wasting from a lowered threshold","C":"Failed H+ secretion by collecting duct cells","D":"Gastric acid loss with persistent vomiting","E":"Reduced ammoniagenesis from high plasma K+"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Hypokalemia with a normal anion gap acidosis points to type 1 RTA, type 2 RTA, or diarrhea, and the course Exam 3 notes then use urine pH and the urinary anion gap. A positive urinary anion gap means little NH4+ (excreted with Cl-) is in the urine, which confirms a renal tubular acidosis; with gastrointestinal HCO3- loss the kidneys excrete large amounts of NH4Cl and the gap is negative. A urine pH below 5.5 during steady-state acidemia fits type 2 rather than type 1 RTA, because distal proton secretion is intact once plasma HCO3- falls below the lowered proximal threshold. Raising plasma HCO3- back to normal then exceeds that threshold and produces heavy bicarbonaturia, and the high fractional excretion of HCO3- confirms proximal (type 2) RTA. Failed collecting duct proton secretion (type 1 RTA) would keep urine pH above 5.5. Intestinal HCO3- loss would give a negative urinary anion gap. Vomiting causes metabolic alkalosis, not acidosis. Reduced ammoniagenesis from hyperkalemia is type 4 RTA, which has a high K+. Sources: Exam 3 lecture notes (SDL 25 RTA algorithm and summary matrix: type 2 RTA has a urine pH below 5.5 and a positive urinary anion gap); Costanzo Physiology Ch. 7 (filtered load and excretion of HCO3-). Costanzo, Guyton, the BRS reading (SDL 30) and First Aid 2025 (p611) do not give a urinary anion gap for each RTA type, so no textbook value conflicts with the notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Exam 3 notes RTA algorithm — K+ (high → type 4), then urine pH (above 5.5 → type 1; below 5.5 → type 2 or diarrhea), then urinary anion gap (positive → RTA; negative → diarrhea); heavy bicarbonaturia on HCO3- loading confirms type 2. COMLEX: Look for glucosuria, aminoaciduria, and phosphaturia (Fanconi syndrome) before ordering a bicarbonate loading test.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 -> Obj 2 — diffusion trapping in the collecting duct is what lets urinary NH4+ rise in diarrhea","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Vomiting causes metabolic alka"}}],"26":[{"id":"sdl26_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Simple vs. Mixed Disorders: The Acid-Base Map & Isohydric Lines","batch":1,"stem":"What does each isohydric line on the acid-base map represent?","choices":{"A":"The set of Pco2 and HCO3- pairs that produce the same pH","B":"The set of Pco2 and HCO3- pairs that produce the same anion gap","C":"The set of pH and Pco2 pairs that produce the same plasma HCO3-","D":"The set of pH and HCO3- pairs that produce the same renal compensation","E":"The expected range of values for one simple acid-base disorder"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Isohydric lines radiate from the origin of the acid-base map, and each line gives all combinations of arterial Pco2 and HCO3- that yield the same H+ concentration and therefore the same pH. Shaded areas, not isohydric lines, show the range of values for a simple disorder, and the map does not plot anion gap or renal compensation as separate lines.","boardPrep":"Step 1: a normal Pco2 of 40 mm Hg with HCO3- of 24 mEq/L sits on the pH 7.4 isohydric line. COMLEX: the same line also passes through Pco2 60 with HCO3- 36 and Pco2 20 with HCO3- 12, which is why compensated disorders can have a near-normal pH.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 -> Obj 4 — mixed disturbances are diagnosed against the same acid-base map framework","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Simple vs. Mixed Disorders: The Acid-Base Map & Isohydric Lines","batch":1,"stem":"At a normal arterial pH of 7.4 (reference range: 7.35–7.45), what is the ratio of HCO3- concentration to dissolved CO2 concentration?","choices":{"A":"5 to 1","B":"10 to 1","C":"20 to 1","D":"15 to 1","E":"30 to 1"},"correct":"C","explanation":"With a Pco2 of 40 mm Hg the dissolved CO2 is 0.03 x 40 = 1.2 mmol/L, and with HCO3- at 24 mmol/L the ratio is 24 to 1.2, or 20 to 1. The Henderson-Hasselbalch equation then gives pH = 6.1 + log 20 = 7.4, and every point on the pH 7.4 isohydric line preserves this same 20 to 1 ratio.","boardPrep":"Step 1: memorize 20 to 1 as the HCO3- to CO2 ratio that defines pH 7.4; disorders shift the ratio, compensation restores it.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 -> Obj 4 — mixed disturbances are diagnosed against the same acid-base map framework","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Simple vs. Mixed Disorders: The Acid-Base Map & Isohydric Lines","batch":1,"stem":"A patient's arterial blood values fall between the shaded areas of two different disorders on the acid-base map. What does this indicate?","choices":{"A":"A laboratory error because valid blood gases fall inside a shaded area","B":"A simple metabolic disorder with respiratory compensation","C":"A single simple disorder with renal compensation","D":"Two or more acid-base disorders are present at the same time","E":"Normal acid-base balance with abnormally low buffer stores"},"correct":"D","explanation":"When a patient's values fall inside a shaded area, a single simple disorder can be concluded. Values that fall outside the shaded areas, including values that fall between two areas, indicate a mixed disorder. A simple metabolic disorder with appropriate respiratory compensation would still lie within its own shaded area, and appropriate renal compensation would place the values in the chronic shaded area. Normal acid-base balance would place the values in the central normal ellipse, not between two disorder areas.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the acid-base map and the renal rules answer the same question, whether the observed compensation matches a simple disorder. COMLEX: think mixed disorder whenever the numbers do not fit any shaded area.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 -> Obj 4 — mixed disturbances are diagnosed against the same acid-base map framework","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"A simple metabolic disorder wi"}},{"id":"sdl26_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Renal Compensation Rules for Primary Respiratory Disturbances","batch":1,"stem":"According to the renal rules, how much does HCO3- rise for each 1 mm Hg rise in Pco2 in acute respiratory acidosis?","choices":{"A":"0.4 mEq/L","B":"0.1 mEq/L","C":"0.6 mEq/L","D":"1.0 mEq/L","E":"1.2 mEq/L"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In acute respiratory acidosis the renal compensation has not yet developed, so the small rise in HCO3- reflects mass action alone, about 0.1 mEq/L for every 1 mm Hg rise in Pco2. The values 0.6 and 1.2 belong to metabolic disorders, where they relate Pco2 to HCO3- in the opposite direction, and the chronic rules are larger than the acute rule.","boardPrep":"Step 1: acute respiratory rules are small because only mass action is operating; chronic rules are larger because the kidney has responded.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 -> Obj 3 — the same delta rules are applied to metabolic disorders and the delta-delta ratio","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The values 0.6 and 1.2 belong "}},{"id":"sdl26_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Renal Compensation Rules for Primary Respiratory Disturbances","batch":1,"stem":"Which renal rule applies to chronic respiratory alkalosis?","choices":{"A":"HCO3- falls 0.4 mEq/L for each 1 mm Hg fall in Pco2","B":"Pco2 falls 1.2 mm Hg for each 1 mEq/L fall in HCO3-","C":"Pco2 rises 0.6 mm Hg for each 1 mEq/L rise in HCO3-","D":"HCO3- rises 0.1 mEq/L for each 1 mm Hg rise in Pco2","E":"HCO3- falls 0.2 mEq/L for each 1 mm Hg fall in Pco2"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The renal rule for chronic respiratory alkalosis predicts a 0.4 mEq/L fall in HCO3- for every 1 mm Hg fall in Pco2 (Costanzo and BRS; the course notes give 0.4 to 0.5), larger than the acute prediction of 0.2 mEq/L because renal compensation has had time to reduce H+ excretion and new HCO3- reabsorption. The other options are the rules for metabolic acidosis, metabolic alkalosis, acute respiratory acidosis, and acute respiratory alkalosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: for every respiratory disorder the chronic rule is larger than the acute rule. COMLEX: use these rules to decide whether a patient is acute or chronic.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 -> Obj 3 — the same delta rules are applied to metabolic disorders and the delta-delta ratio","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"The other options are the rule","C":"The other options are the rule","D":"The other options are the rule","E":"The other options are the rule"}},{"id":"sdl26_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Renal Compensation Rules for Primary Respiratory Disturbances","batch":1,"stem":"Why is the compensation for a primary respiratory disorder renal rather than respiratory?","choices":{"A":"Peripheral chemoreceptors are unresponsive to changes in arterial Pco2","B":"The lungs cannot change Pco2 without a preceding change in renal HCO3- handling","C":"Intracellular buffers remove the change in Pco2 before the lungs can respond","D":"Respiration is itself the cause of the disorder and so cannot correct it","E":"The medullary respiratory center is suppressed by any change in plasma pH"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In a respiratory disorder, ventilation is the cause of the abnormal Pco2, so changing ventilation cannot compensate. The kidney instead adjusts the HCO3- concentration to bring the HCO3- to CO2 ratio, and therefore the pH, back toward normal. Metabolic disorders, in contrast, are compensated immediately by ventilation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: metabolic disorders are compensated by the lungs and respiratory disorders by the kidneys. COMLEX: use the rule of thumb that the compensating organ is generally not the organ causing the primary problem.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 -> Obj 3 — the same delta rules are applied to metabolic disorders and the delta-delta ratio","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Acute vs. Chronic Respiratory Acidosis: Buffering & Renal Compensation","batch":1,"stem":"Where does buffering of excess CO2 occur in acute respiratory acidosis?","choices":{"A":"Primarily in extracellular fluid, through the HCO3-/CO2 buffer system","B":"Primarily in the urine, through excretion of titratable acid","C":"Primarily in plasma, through binding of H+ to albumin","D":"Primarily in the lungs, through increased alveolar ventilation","E":"Primarily in intracellular fluid, especially within red blood cells"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Lipid-soluble CO2 diffuses into cells, especially red blood cells, where it is converted to H+ and HCO3-, and the H+ is buffered by hemoglobin, other proteins, and organic phosphates. The HCO3-/CO2 buffer cannot buffer a CO2 excess because CO2 is one of its own components, and urinary excretion and ventilation are not chemical buffering steps in acute respiratory acidosis. Plasma proteins such as albumin are not the main site, because the CO2 load is buffered inside cells.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the respiratory disorders are buffered in intracellular fluid, while metabolic disorders are first buffered by extracellular HCO3-. COMLEX: deoxyhemoglobin is an even more effective buffer than oxyhemoglobin.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 -> Obj 5 — intracellular buffering by hemoglobin and organic phosphates handles the CO2 load","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The HCO3-/CO2 buffer cannot bu","B":"The HCO3-/CO2 buffer cannot bu","D":"The HCO3-/CO2 buffer cannot bu"}},{"id":"sdl26_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Acute vs. Chronic Respiratory Acidosis: Buffering & Renal Compensation","batch":1,"stem":"Why is arterial pH lower in acute respiratory acidosis than in chronic respiratory acidosis at the same Pco2?","choices":{"A":"Respiratory compensation is stronger in the chronic phase","B":"Renal compensation has yet to raise HCO3- in the acute phase","C":"Intracellular buffers become exhausted in the acute phase","D":"Pco2 is higher during the acute phase by definition","E":"Hypoventilation is corrected during the chronic phase"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The difference between acute and chronic respiratory acidosis lies in renal compensation. In the acute phase HCO3- rises only by mass action, so the HCO3-/CO2 ratio is low and pH is quite low. In the chronic phase the kidney excretes more H+ and adds new HCO3-, which raises HCO3-, restores the ratio toward 20 to 1, and moves pH toward normal.","boardPrep":"Step 1: same Pco2, different HCO3-, different pH; the extra HCO3- is the renal compensation. COMLEX: an acutely worsening COPD patient can drop pH sharply from a compensated baseline.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 -> Obj 5 — intracellular buffering by hemoglobin and organic phosphates handles the CO2 load","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Acute vs. Chronic Respiratory Acidosis: Buffering & Renal Compensation","batch":1,"stem":"Which renal changes underlie compensation for chronic respiratory acidosis?","choices":{"A":"Greater excretion of H+ as titratable acid and NH4+ with new HCO3- reabsorption","B":"Increased HCO3- excretion in the urine with reduced NH3 synthesis in proximal cells","C":"Decreased alpha-intercalated cell H+ secretion with greater glutaminase activity","D":"Increased proximal HCO3- secretion with decreased NHE3 activity in the tubule","E":"Increased urinary phosphate loss with decreased titratable acid formation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Renal compensation for respiratory acidosis increases H+ excretion as titratable acid and NH4+ and increases synthesis and reabsorption of new HCO3-, which raises plasma HCO3- beyond the mass-action effect. The alternatives describe changes that would lower plasma HCO3- or that do not correspond to any known compensatory pathway. Decreased alpha-intercalated cell H+ secretion would cut, not raise, new HCO3- generation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the kidney compensates for acidemia by adding new HCO3- through titratable acid and ammonium excretion. COMLEX: the process takes several days to become complete.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 -> Obj 5 — intracellular buffering by hemoglobin and organic phosphates handles the CO2 load","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Respiratory Alkalosis: HCO3- Wasting, Albumin-Ca2+ Binding, & Tetany","batch":1,"stem":"Which arterial blood profile defines primary respiratory alkalosis?","choices":{"A":"Low pH with low HCO3- and a compensatory fall in Pco2","B":"High pH with high HCO3- and a compensatory rise in Pco2","C":"Low pH with high Pco2 and a compensatory rise in HCO3-","D":"Normal pH with low Pco2 and an unexplained rise in HCO3-","E":"High pH with low Pco2 and a compensatory fall in HCO3-"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Hyperventilation blows off CO2, so Pco2 falls first and pH rises, and HCO3- falls both by mass action and by renal compensation. The other profiles correspond to metabolic acidosis, metabolic alkalosis, respiratory acidosis, and a combination that no simple disorder produces.","boardPrep":"Step 1: in a respiratory disorder pH and Pco2 move in opposite directions, while HCO3- moves with Pco2 as compensation. COMLEX: in metabolic disorders pH and HCO3- move together, so ask which variable can explain the pH shift to find the primary disturbance.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 -> Obj 2 — primary respiratory disorders and the etiologies of hypocapnia are introduced there","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The other profiles correspond ","B":"The other profiles correspond ","C":"The other profiles correspond ","D":"The other profiles correspond "}},{"id":"sdl26_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Respiratory Alkalosis: HCO3- Wasting, Albumin-Ca2+ Binding, & Tetany","batch":1,"stem":"Why does alkalemia cause tingling, numbness, and tetany?","choices":{"A":"Albumin releases Ca2+, so ionized Ca2+ rises and prolongs membrane depolarization","B":"Less H+ binds albumin, so more Ca2+ binds and ionized Ca2+ falls","C":"Alkalemia raises PTH release, which lowers total serum Ca2+ through renal wasting","D":"Alkalemia lowers extracellular K+, which hyperpolarizes nerve and muscle membranes","E":"Alkalemia reduces intestinal Ca2+ absorption, which lowers total body Ca2+ within minutes"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Negatively charged groups on albumin bind either H+ or Ca2+. In alkalemia less H+ is bound, so more Ca2+ binds to albumin and the free ionized Ca2+ falls. Low extracellular Ca2+ makes the threshold potential of nerves and muscles more negative and increases excitability, producing tingling, numbness, and tetany.","boardPrep":"Step 1: pH changes ionized Ca2+ without changing total Ca2+; alkalemia lowers it and acidemia raises it. COMLEX: this is why anxious hyperventilating patients have perioral and acral paresthesias.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 -> Obj 2 — primary respiratory disorders and the etiologies of hypocapnia are introduced there","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Respiratory Alkalosis: HCO3- Wasting, Albumin-Ca2+ Binding, & Tetany","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following is a cause of respiratory alkalosis?","choices":{"A":"Opioid overdose","B":"Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis","C":"Salicylate poisoning","D":"Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease","E":"Guillain-Barre syndrome"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Salicylates directly stimulate the medullary respiratory center, producing hyperventilation and respiratory alkalosis, in addition to a metabolic acidosis. Opioids depress the respiratory center, ALS and Guillain-Barre syndrome weaken respiratory muscles, and COPD impairs gas exchange, so all four cause respiratory acidosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: causes of respiratory alkalosis include hysterical hyperventilation, salicylate poisoning, gram-negative sepsis, hypoxemia, and mechanical ventilation. COMLEX: salicylate toxicity is the classic mixed respiratory alkalosis with metabolic acidosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 -> Obj 2 — primary respiratory disorders and the etiologies of hypocapnia are introduced there","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Opioids depress the respirator","D":"Opioids depress the respirator"}},{"id":"sdl26_q13","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Simple vs. Mixed Disorders: The Acid-Base Map & Isohydric Lines","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old man with severe COPD has Pco2 60 mm Hg (reference range: 33–45 mm Hg) and HCO3- 36 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L). Substitution into the Henderson-Hasselbalch equation gives a pH of 7.4 (reference range: 7.35–7.45). Which statement best describes his acid-base status?","choices":{"A":"A near-normal pH despite abnormal values because HCO3- offsets the high Pco2","B":"Normal acid-base status because a normal pH excludes any acid-base disorder","C":"A laboratory error because a high Pco2 and a high HCO3- give an abnormal pH","D":"A metabolic alkalosis with respiratory compensation that normalized the pH","E":"An acute respiratory acidosis because the pH has not fallen below normal"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The ratio of 36 to 0.03 x 60 = 1.8 is 20 to 1, so the pH is 7.4, but both Pco2 and HCO3- are clearly abnormal. This is the principle underlying compensation: abnormal combinations of Pco2 and HCO3- on the same isohydric line yield a normal pH. Deciding whether such a pattern is a pure chronic respiratory acidosis or a mixed disorder requires the compensation rules, not the pH. His HCO3- of 36 is higher than a simple chronic respiratory acidosis alone would predict, so a coexisting metabolic alkalosis is possible, but the isohydric principle holds either way.","boardPrep":"Step 1: a normal pH does not exclude an acid-base disorder, since compensation can restore the ratio. COMLEX: check Pco2 and HCO3- separately before calling a blood gas normal.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 4 — the acid-base map places compensated and mixed disorders on isohydric lines","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"This is the principle underlyi","C":"This is the principle underlyi"}},{"id":"sdl26_q14","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Simple vs. Mixed Disorders: The Acid-Base Map & Isohydric Lines","batch":2,"stem":"A 31-year-old man has severe pain from an ankle fracture sustained an hour ago and is breathing rapidly. Arterial pH is 7.49 (reference range: 7.35–7.45), Pco2 is 30 mm Hg (reference range: 33–45 mm Hg), and HCO3- is 22 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L). Which pattern on the acid-base map is most consistent with his values?","choices":{"A":"The shaded area for chronic respiratory alkalosis","B":"The shaded area for simple metabolic alkalosis","C":"The shaded area for acute respiratory acidosis","D":"The shaded area for acute respiratory alkalosis","E":"A position between two shaded areas"},"correct":"D","explanation":"His high pH with low Pco2 and slightly low HCO3- identifies respiratory alkalosis. The HCO3- fall of only 2 mEq/L matches the acute rule of 0.2 mEq/L per mm Hg, since a 10 mm Hg fall in Pco2 predicts a fall of about 2 mEq/L, whereas the chronic rule predicts a fall of about 4 to 5 mEq/L. His values therefore lie in the acute respiratory alkalosis band. Because his values fit the acute rule, they fall within one shaded area rather than between two.","boardPrep":"Step 1: compare the observed HCO3- change with the acute and chronic predictions to place a respiratory disorder on the map. COMLEX: a near-normal HCO3- with a very high pH means the disorder is acute.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 4 — mixed versus simple disorders are separated by whether values fall in a shaded area","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The HCO3- fall of only 2 mEq/L","E":"Because his values fit the acu"}},{"id":"sdl26_q15","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Simple vs. Mixed Disorders: The Acid-Base Map & Isohydric Lines","batch":2,"stem":"A 63-year-old woman with obesity hypoventilation syndrome has arterial pH 7.32 (reference range: 7.35–7.45), Pco2 60 mm Hg (reference range: 33–45 mm Hg), and HCO3- 30 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L). Her values fall within a single shaded band on an acid-base map. Which primary disturbance does that band represent?","choices":{"A":"Metabolic alkalosis, because HCO3- is elevated above the normal value of 24 mEq/L","B":"Respiratory acidosis, because the elevated Pco2 explains the acidemia","C":"Metabolic acidosis, because pH is below 7.4 and HCO3- is abnormal","D":"Respiratory alkalosis, because HCO3- is elevated after prolonged hyperventilation","E":"Identifying the primary disturbance requires an anion gap measurement"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The pH of 7.32 indicates acidemia, and the disturbance that explains that direction is the elevated Pco2, so the primary disorder is respiratory acidosis. The elevated HCO3- is the compensatory response, not the primary disturbance, because a primary metabolic alkalosis would raise pH, not lower it. The anion gap is used to differentiate metabolic acidoses and is not needed here.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the primary disturbance is the variable whose change would explain the direction of the pH shift, here a high Pco2 for a low pH. COMLEX: this step must come before any compensation calculation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 2 — a primary respiratory disorder is identified when the Pco2 change explains the direction of the pH shift","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The elevated HCO3- is the comp"}},{"id":"sdl26_q16","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Renal Compensation Rules for Primary Respiratory Disturbances","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old man with a long history of COPD has arterial pH 7.29 (reference range: 7.35–7.45), Pco2 70 mm Hg (reference range: 33–45 mm Hg), and HCO3- 33 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L). Which best describes his condition?","choices":{"A":"A new episode of CO2 retention with no renal compensation yet under way","B":"A primary metabolic alkalosis with compensatory hypoventilation raising Pco2","C":"A primary respiratory alkalosis with renal compensation raising HCO3- further","D":"Long-standing CO2 retention with renal compensation already in place","E":"A primary metabolic acidosis with compensatory hypoventilation raising Pco2"},"correct":"D","explanation":"His Pco2 is 30 mm Hg above normal. The acute rule (about 0.1 mEq/L per mm Hg) predicts an HCO3- rise of about 3 mEq/L, and the chronic rule (0.3 to 0.4 mEq/L per mm Hg) predicts about 9 to 12 mEq/L. His rise of 9 mEq/L fits the chronic prediction, indicating renal compensation from long-standing CO2 retention. Metabolic alkalosis would produce alkalemia, metabolic acidosis would lower HCO3-, and respiratory alkalosis would lower Pco2.","boardPrep":"Step 1: an HCO3- rise far above the acute prediction points to chronic respiratory acidosis, as in COPD. COMLEX: a chronic lung disease history in the vignette supports the chronic pattern.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 3 — the delta rules are applied here to primary respiratory disturbances","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The acute rule (about 0.1 mEq/"}},{"id":"sdl26_q17","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Renal Compensation Rules for Primary Respiratory Disturbances","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old woman has lived and worked at a high-altitude mountain station for 3 weeks and has had 3 days of profuse watery diarrhea. Arterial pH is 7.40 (reference range: 7.35–7.45), Pco2 25 mm Hg (reference range: 33–45 mm Hg), and HCO3- 15 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L). Full renal adaptation to hyperventilation is expected to lower HCO3- by 0.4 mEq/L for each 1 mm Hg fall in Pco2. Which interpretation best fits her blood gas?","choices":{"A":"Chronic respiratory alkalosis that is fully compensated","B":"Chronic respiratory alkalosis plus a metabolic acidosis","C":"Acute respiratory alkalosis that is simple and uncomplicated","D":"Chronic respiratory alkalosis plus a metabolic alkalosis","E":"Primary metabolic acidosis with appropriate respiratory compensation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A 15 mm Hg fall in Pco2 with full renal adaptation predicts a drop of 15 × 0.4 = 6 mEq/L, to about 18 mEq/L; her measured 15 mEq/L is lower than adaptation alone explains, so a second process, a metabolic acidosis from the diarrhea, is also lowering HCO3-, which is why her pH sits at a normal 7.40. Full compensation is not the explanation, because HCO3- falls beyond the predicted value rather than matching it. An acute respiratory alkalosis would lower HCO3- by only about 0.2 × 15 = 3 mEq/L. A coexisting metabolic alkalosis would raise HCO3- above the predicted 18 mEq/L, not lower it. As a primary metabolic acidosis, a 9 mEq/L fall in HCO3- predicts a Pco2 near 29 mm Hg (1.2 mm Hg per mEq/L), and her Pco2 of 25 mm Hg is lower than that, so a respiratory alkalosis is present as well.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Respiratory alkalosis renal rules: acute, HCO3- falls 0.2 mEq/L per mm Hg; chronic, 0.4 mEq/L per mm Hg; an HCO3- below the predicted value means an added metabolic acidosis. COMLEX: A normal pH with clearly abnormal Pco2 and HCO3- should prompt a search for two primary disorders.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 3 — the same renal rules and Winters' formula flag a second disorder","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Full compensation is not the e","D":"An acute respiratory alkalosis"}},{"id":"sdl26_q18","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Renal Compensation Rules for Primary Respiratory Disturbances","batch":2,"stem":"A 27-year-old man who has spent five days climbing at high altitude has arterial pH 7.48 (reference range: 7.35–7.45), Pco2 28 mm Hg (reference range: 33–45 mm Hg), and HCO3- 20 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L). Which best explains his HCO3- value?","choices":{"A":"Immediate mass action from lost CO2, the same change expected within minutes","B":"Respiratory compensation from the medullary center after the initial hyperventilation","C":"Intracellular buffering by hemoglobin, which lowers plasma HCO3- immediately","D":"Increased urinary excretion of titratable acid, which removes plasma HCO3- directly","E":"Renal compensation, with less H+ secretion and more excretion of filtered HCO3-"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Hypoxemia at altitude stimulates peripheral chemoreceptors, causing hyperventilation and respiratory alkalosis. Over several days the kidney decreases H+ secretion and excretes part of the filtered HCO3-, so plasma HCO3- falls. His fall of 4 mEq/L is larger than the acute prediction of about 2.4 mEq/L for a 12 mm Hg fall in Pco2 and approaching the chronic range of 4.8 to 6.0 mEq/L, consistent with renal compensation under way. There is no respiratory compensation for a respiratory disorder, and excreting titratable acid would add HCO3-.","boardPrep":"Step 1: hypoxemia from altitude, pneumonia, or embolism stimulates peripheral chemoreceptors and produces respiratory alkalosis. COMLEX: time course of exposure tells you whether the kidney has had time to compensate.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 3 — the delta rules distinguish acute from chronic respiratory alkalosis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"His fall of 4 mEq/L is larger "}},{"id":"sdl26_q19","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Acute vs. Chronic Respiratory Acidosis: Buffering & Renal Compensation","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman in a severe asthma exacerbation has pH 7.21 (reference range: 7.35–7.45), Pco2 68 mm Hg (reference range: 33–45 mm Hg), and HCO3- 26 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L) after two hours of shallow breathing. Which best explains her HCO3- concentration?","choices":{"A":"Mass action from CO2 hydration before renal compensation has begun","B":"Complete renal compensation from increased new HCO3- reabsorption over several hours","C":"Respiratory compensation from medullary stimulation that has raised her ventilation","D":"A coexisting metabolic alkalosis that balances the respiratory acidosis","E":"Extracellular buffering of CO2 by plasma HCO3- that raises its concentration"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Retained CO2 is hydrated to H2CO3 and dissociates, so HCO3- rises slightly by mass action; the H+ formed is buffered mainly inside red cells by hemoglobin, and HCO3- does not buffer CO2 because CO2 is part of its own buffer pair. The kidney has not had time to add new HCO3-, so pH is quite low. There is no respiratory compensation for a respiratory disorder, complete renal compensation takes several days, and a coexisting metabolic alkalosis would push HCO3- much higher.","boardPrep":"Step 1: in acute respiratory acidosis HCO3- rises only slightly, and pH tracks the Pco2. COMLEX: a lower-than-expected pH in an acutely worsening asthmatic signals impending respiratory failure.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 5 — CO2 buffering occurs within cells rather than by extracellular HCO3-","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"There is no respiratory compen"}},{"id":"sdl26_q20","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Acute vs. Chronic Respiratory Acidosis: Buffering & Renal Compensation","batch":2,"stem":"A 64-year-old man with chronic hypercapnia shows increased urinary NH4+ excretion and enhanced bicarbonate generation. Which effect of his elevated Pco2 on renal cells most directly supports this compensation?","choices":{"A":"Carbonic anhydrase inhibition that lowers filtered HCO3- reclamation","B":"Luminal alkalinization that favors NH3 trapping in the collecting duct","C":"Greater intracellular CO2 supplies more H+ for secretion and new HCO3- synthesis","D":"Glutaminase suppression that lowers proximal NH4+ synthesis and its excretion","E":"Phosphate depletion in tubular cells that increases titratable acid excretion"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Increased Pco2 raises the CO2 supply inside proximal and alpha-intercalated cells, which combines with water to generate more H+ for secretion and more HCO3- for reabsorption into blood, so more H+ is excreted as titratable acid and NH4+ and more new HCO3- is added. The other options describe effects that would either reduce HCO3- generation or that do not occur in hypercapnia. Hypercapnia acidifies rather than alkalinizes the tubular fluid, and NH3 is trapped as NH4+ because the lumen is acidic.","boardPrep":"Step 1: arterial Pco2 is a direct determinant of renal H+ secretion, which is why chronic hypercapnia is compensated by the kidney. COMLEX: this is the mechanistic basis for the higher chronic rule.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 1 — arterial Pco2 modifies proximal HCO3- reclamation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q21","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Acute vs. Chronic Respiratory Acidosis: Buffering & Renal Compensation","batch":2,"stem":"Two men have the same arterial Pco2 of 70 mm Hg (reference range: 33–45 mm Hg). Patient X, a 59-year-old, became hypercapnic over 30 minutes after an opioid overdose; patient Y, a 67-year-old with severe COPD, has been hypercapnic for several weeks. Which comparison of their blood gases is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Patient Y has the lower pH because prolonged CO2 retention exhausts intracellular buffers","B":"Patient X has the higher HCO3- because acute CO2 hydration is the larger effect","C":"Both patients have the same HCO3- and pH because their Pco2 values are equal","D":"Patient Y has a higher HCO3- and a pH closer to normal than patient X","E":"Patient X has the pH closer to normal because immediate buffering is complete"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Patient X is in the acute phase with almost no renal compensation, so HCO3- is only slightly elevated and pH is quite low. Patient Y has had weeks for the kidney to raise HCO3- through greater H+ excretion and new HCO3- generation, which restores the HCO3- to CO2 ratio partway, so pH is closer to normal. Equal Pco2 does not mean equal HCO3-.","boardPrep":"Step 1: chronic respiratory acidosis has a higher HCO3- and a less severe acidemia than acute respiratory acidosis at the same Pco2. COMLEX: this is why a chronic CO2 retainer tolerates a Pco2 that would be dangerous in an acute setting.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 3 — the difference between acute and chronic rules is the renal compensation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q22","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Respiratory Alkalosis: HCO3- Wasting, Albumin-Ca2+ Binding, & Tetany","batch":2,"stem":"A 38-year-old man has a panic attack in the emergency department and develops light-headedness, numbness of his fingers, and carpopedal spasm. Arterial pH is 7.53 (reference range: 7.35–7.45), Pco2 is 26 mm Hg (reference range: 33–45 mm Hg), and HCO3- is 21 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L). Serum total calcium is 9.4 mg/dL (reference range: 8.4–10.2 mg/dL). Which mechanism best explains his numbness and carpopedal spasm?","choices":{"A":"Decreased ionized Ca2+ from greater albumin binding that raises excitability","B":"Decreased Pco2 that causes cerebral vasoconstriction and cerebral ischemia","C":"Decreased total body Ca2+ from renal calcium wasting after alkalemia","D":"Increased HCO3- excretion that causes hypokalemia and generalized weakness","E":"Decreased HCO3- that lowers plasma osmolality and causes neuronal swelling"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Alkalemia reduces H+ binding to albumin, so more Ca2+ is bound, ionized Ca2+ falls, the threshold potential of nerves becomes more negative, and excitability rises, producing numbness and carpopedal spasm. His light-headedness is explained separately by cerebral arteriolar vasoconstriction from the low Pco2. Total body Ca2+ is unchanged. The course attributes this tingling and carpopedal spasm to the fall in ionized Ca2+, not to K+ loss or neuronal swelling, and a 3 mEq/L fall in bicarbonate barely changes plasma osmolality, which is set mainly by Na+ and its anions.","boardPrep":"Step 1: distinguish the two consequences of hypocapnia, cerebral vasoconstriction for light-headedness and low ionized Ca2+ for paresthesias. COMLEX: exam-anxiety hyperventilation is a classic presentation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 1 — the kidney regulates total plasma Ca2+, while alkalemia shifts only the ionized fraction","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"The course attributes this tin"}},{"id":"sdl26_q23","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Respiratory Alkalosis: HCO3- Wasting, Albumin-Ca2+ Binding, & Tetany","batch":2,"stem":"A 19-year-old man is breathing rapidly and has perioral tingling after a minor car accident, and pulse oximetry is 99% on room air. Arterial pH is 7.55 (reference range: 7.35–7.45), Pco2 is 25 mm Hg (reference range: 33–45 mm Hg), and HCO3- is 21 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L). He is coached to breathe slowly into a paper bag, and his symptoms resolve within minutes. Which mechanism best explains his improvement?","choices":{"A":"Rebreathing raises plasma HCO3- through renal compensation within minutes","B":"Rebreathing increases H+ excretion, which raises albumin-bound Ca2+","C":"Rebreathing raises Pco2 to lower pH and restore ionized Ca2+","D":"Rebreathing supplies oxygen that corrects the hypoxemia driving his ventilation","E":"Rebreathing lowers Pco2, which relieves the cerebral vasoconstriction"},"correct":"C","explanation":"By rebreathing his own CO2 he raises Pco2 to normal, which restores the HCO3- to CO2 ratio and lowers the pH. Falling pH increases H+ binding to albumin, so less Ca2+ is bound and ionized Ca2+ rises, relieving the excitability. Renal compensation cannot act within minutes, his ventilation was driven by anxiety and not hypoxemia, and rebreathing raises rather than lowers Pco2.","boardPrep":"Step 1: treating the cause of hyperventilation, by restoring normal Pco2, resolves both the alkalemia and the low ionized Ca2+. COMLEX: reassurance and coached slow breathing or brief rebreathing are the textbook approach to uncomplicated anxiety hyperventilation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 1 — the kidney regulates total plasma Ca2+; normalizing pH restores the ionized fraction","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"Renal compensation cannot act "}},{"id":"sdl26_q24","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Respiratory Alkalosis: HCO3- Wasting, Albumin-Ca2+ Binding, & Tetany","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman with chronic hyperventilation from a brainstem lesion has a persistently low Pco2 and a low plasma HCO3- beyond what mass action predicts. Which mechanism explains the additional HCO3- loss?","choices":{"A":"A rising GFR filters more HCO3- than the proximal tubule can reabsorb","B":"Less CO2 in renal cells reduces H+ secretion and new HCO3- generation","C":"Aldosterone suppression blocks alpha-intercalated cell H+ secretion and wastes HCO3- directly","D":"Increased proximal NHE3 activity raises H+ secretion and dilutes plasma HCO3-","E":"Stimulated glutaminase increases NH4+ excretion, which removes plasma HCO3-"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In respiratory alkalosis the low Pco2 leaves less CO2 available in renal cells to generate H+ for secretion, so H+ excretion as titratable acid and NH4+ falls and less new HCO3- is synthesized and reabsorbed. This decreased HCO3- reabsorption lowers plasma HCO3- beyond the mass-action effect and is the renal compensation. Stimulated glutaminase would add HCO3-, and none of the other listed mechanisms is the compensatory pathway.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the kidney compensates for alkalemia by wasting HCO3-, which is why chronic respiratory alkalosis has a larger HCO3- fall than the acute form. COMLEX: CNS disease, salicylates, and pregnancy are chronic hyperventilation causes.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 1 — arterial Pco2 governs proximal HCO3- reclamation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"grid_sdl26_q01","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Respiratory Alkalosis: HCO3- Wasting, Albumin-Ca2+ Binding, & Tetany","batch":4,"stem":"A 23-year-old woman is brought to the emergency department 20 minutes after the sudden onset, while she sat in a classroom, of a pounding heartbeat, chest tightness, and an intense fear that she was dying. She now reports numbness and tingling around her mouth and in both hands, and her hands are flexed at the wrists and metacarpophalangeal joints. She takes no medications. Respirations are 32/min and deep, pulse is 112/min, and oxygen saturation is 99% on room air. The lungs are clear to auscultation. An ECG shows sinus tachycardia. Which of the following sets of arterial blood findings, compared with normal, is most likely in this patient?","grid":{"headers":["Arterial pH","Pco₂","HCO₃⁻","Ionized Ca²⁺"]},"choices":{"A":["↑","↑","↑","↓"],"B":["↓","↑","↑","↑"],"C":["↓","↓","↓","↑"],"D":["↑","↓","↓","↓"],"E":["↑","↓","↓","↑"]},"correct":"D","explanation":"Sudden fear, palpitations, and rapid deep breathing with clear lungs and normal oxygen saturation are a panic attack with hyperventilation, which causes acute respiratory alkalosis, so the pattern (↑ arterial pH, ↓ Pco₂, ↓ HCO₃⁻, ↓ ionized Ca²⁺) is correct. Blowing off CO₂ lowers Pco₂ and raises pH; by mass action HCO₃⁻ falls slightly in the acute phase (about 2 mEq/L for each 10 mm Hg fall in Pco₂). With less H⁺ bound to albumin, more Ca²⁺ binds to it, so ionized Ca²⁺ falls even though total Ca²⁺ is unchanged, causing perioral tingling and carpopedal spasm. The pattern (↑ arterial pH, ↑ Pco₂, ↑ HCO₃⁻, ↓ ionized Ca²⁺) is metabolic alkalosis with respiratory compensation, as after vomiting; it also lowers ionized Ca²⁺, but compensatory hypoventilation raises Pco₂. The pattern (↓ arterial pH, ↑ Pco₂, ↑ HCO₃⁻, ↑ ionized Ca²⁺) is respiratory acidosis from hypoventilation. The pattern (↓ arterial pH, ↓ Pco₂, ↓ HCO₃⁻, ↑ ionized Ca²⁺) is metabolic acidosis with respiratory compensation, in which Kussmaul breathing also lowers Pco₂ but the pH is low and ionized Ca²⁺ rises. The pattern (↑ arterial pH, ↓ Pco₂, ↓ HCO₃⁻, ↑ ionized Ca²⁺) has the acid-base pattern of respiratory alkalosis but the wrong Ca²⁺ change: alkalemia increases Ca²⁺ binding to albumin and lowers the ionized fraction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: alkalemia lowers ionized Ca²⁺ (more Ca²⁺ bound to albumin) and acidemia raises it, with total Ca²⁺ unchanged; hyperventilation tetany is the classic example. COMLEX: in respiratory alkalosis HCO₃⁻ falls about 2 mEq/L per 10 mm Hg fall in Pco₂ when acute and 4–5 mEq/L when chronic, after renal HCO₃⁻ wasting.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 3 — metabolic alkalemia shifts K⁺ into cells, but a respiratory alkalosis like this one does not cause a significant shift, because CO₂ diffuses freely and H⁺ is buffered inside the cell","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex3t_sdl26_q1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Simple vs. Mixed Disorders: The Acid-Base Map & Isohydric Lines","batch":3,"stem":"A 45-year-old man is found unresponsive beside an empty bottle of oxycodone. He is cyanotic with a respiratory rate of 4/min. Arterial pH is 7.10 (reference range: 7.35–7.45), PaCO2 60 mm Hg (reference range: 33–45 mm Hg), and HCO3- 18 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L). Which of the following best describes his acid-base status?","choices":{"A":"Acute respiratory acidosis with expected buffering","B":"Chronic respiratory acidosis with renal compensation","C":"Metabolic acidosis with expected respiratory response","D":"Combined respiratory and metabolic acidosis","E":"Respiratory acidosis plus a metabolic alkalosis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The ratio 18/(0.03 × 60) = 18/1.8 = 10 gives pH = 6.1 + log 10 = 7.10, so the values are internally consistent. A PaCO2 20 mm Hg above normal should raise HCO3- to 24 + (0.1 × 20) = 26 mEq/L if acute or 24 + (0.3 × 20) = 30 mEq/L if chronic (up to 32 with the 0.4 upper value in the Exam 3 notes), yet his HCO3- has fallen to 18 mEq/L; a second process is consuming HCO3-, such as lactic acid accumulating during hypoxia, so his values fall outside every shaded band of the acid-base map. Acute respiratory acidosis with expected buffering predicts an HCO3- near 26 mEq/L, not 18. Chronic respiratory acidosis predicts an even higher HCO3- of about 30 mEq/L. Simple metabolic acidosis with an HCO3- of 18 mEq/L predicts compensatory hyperventilation to a PaCO2 of 40 − (1.2 × 6) = 32.8 mm Hg, not 60 mm Hg. An added metabolic alkalosis would push HCO3- above 26 mEq/L rather than below normal. Sources: Costanzo Physiology Ch. 7 (acid-base map, renal rules, and causes of respiratory acidosis); Exam 3 lecture notes (SDL 26, simple versus mixed disorders).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Compensation moves the second variable in the same direction as the first, so a high PaCO2 with a low HCO3- means two acidoses. COMLEX: In opioid overdose, a low HCO3- alongside hypercapnia signals a concurrent metabolic acidosis that needs its own evaluation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 -> Obj 4 — cardiorespiratory arrest is a classic source of combined respiratory and metabolic acidosis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"An added metabolic alkalosis w"}},{"id":"ex3t_sdl26_q2","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Simple vs. Mixed Disorders: The Acid-Base Map & Isohydric Lines","batch":3,"stem":"A 19-year-old woman is brought to the emergency department 3 hours after swallowing a large number of aspirin tablets. She is breathing rapidly and deeply. Arterial pH is 7.40 (reference range: 7.35–7.45), PaCO2 20 mm Hg (reference range: 33–45 mm Hg), and HCO3- 12 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L). Which interpretation best fits her blood gas?","choices":{"A":"Normal acid-base status needing no further workup","B":"Metabolic acidosis with complete respiratory compensation","C":"Two primary disorders with opposing effects on pH","D":"Respiratory alkalosis with complete renal compensation","E":"Values that fail the Henderson-Hasselbalch check"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The ratio 12/(0.03 × 20) = 12/0.6 = 20 gives pH = 6.1 + log 20 = 7.40, so the values are internally consistent and lie on the normal 7.40 isohydric line despite a markedly abnormal PaCO2 and HCO3-. Salicylate stimulates the medullary respiratory center, causing respiratory alkalosis, and also causes an increased anion gap metabolic acidosis; their opposing effects on pH cancel. A normal pH reached with a PaCO2 of 20 mm Hg and an HCO3- of 12 mEq/L reflects two abnormal processes, not normal status. Simple metabolic acidosis with an HCO3- of 12 mEq/L predicts a PaCO2 of 40 − (1.2 × 12) = 25.6 mm Hg, higher than her 20 mm Hg. Respiratory alkalosis of 3 hours predicts an HCO3- of 24 − (0.2 × 20) = 20 mEq/L, and even chronic compensation predicts 24 − (0.4 × 20) = 16 mEq/L (14 mEq/L with the 0.5 upper value in the Exam 3 notes), both above her 12 mEq/L. The Henderson-Hasselbalch check reproduces the measured pH exactly. Sources: Costanzo Physiology Ch. 7 (causes of respiratory alkalosis and metabolic acidosis; renal rules); Exam 3 lecture notes (SDL 26, isohydric lines on the acid-base map).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Salicylate poisoning classically causes a mixed respiratory alkalosis and increased anion gap metabolic acidosis, which can leave pH near 7.40. COMLEX: A normal pH does not exclude an acid-base disorder, so compare PaCO2 and HCO3- with the predicted compensation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 -> Obj 3 — the Henderson-Hasselbalch ratio confirms that abnormal values can still give a normal pH","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"A normal pH reached with a PaC"}},{"id":"ex3t_sdl26_q3","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Renal Compensation Rules for Primary Respiratory Disturbances","batch":3,"stem":"A 34-year-old man with a severe traumatic brain injury has been mechanically ventilated for 6 days, with settings that have held his PaCO2 steady at 30 mm Hg (reference range: 33–45 mm Hg). He has no other acid-base disturbance. If his kidneys have responded appropriately for this duration, his plasma HCO3- (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L) is expected to be closest to which value?","choices":{"A":"About 22 mEq/L","B":"About 16 mEq/L","C":"About 26 mEq/L","D":"About 28 mEq/L","E":"About 20 mEq/L"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Six days of sustained hypocapnia is chronic respiratory alkalosis, for which the renal rule predicts a 0.4 mEq/L fall in HCO3- per 1 mm Hg fall in PaCO2: 24 − (0.4 × 10) = 20 mEq/L, and a steeper 0.5 mEq/L per mm Hg estimate gives 19 mEq/L, still closest to 20. The decreased CO2 supply to renal cells reduces H+ secretion, filtered HCO3- reabsorption, and new HCO3- synthesis. About 22 mEq/L is the acute prediction, 24 − (0.2 × 10), which applies before renal compensation has had several days to develop. About 16 mEq/L is a larger fall than either rule predicts and would suggest an added metabolic acidosis. About 26 and 28 mEq/L move HCO3- upward, the direction of compensation for respiratory acidosis. Sources: Costanzo Physiology Ch. 7 (renal rules for respiratory alkalosis; effect of PCO2 on HCO3- reabsorption); Exam 3 lecture notes (SDL 26, acute and chronic delta rules).","boardPrep":"Step 1: The respiratory rules (Costanzo) are acute 0.1 and chronic 0.3 for acidosis and acute 0.2 and chronic 0.4 for alkalosis, in mEq/L of HCO3- per mm Hg of PaCO2; the Exam 3 notes give chronic ranges of 0.3 to 0.4 and 0.4 to 0.5. COMLEX: Mechanical ventilation is a cause of respiratory alkalosis, so check the PaCO2 target before blaming a low HCO3- on a metabolic process.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 -> Obj 3 — the acute and chronic respiratory delta rules quantify expected compensation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"About 22 mEq/L is the acute pr","D":"About 26 and 28 mEq/L move HCO"}},{"id":"ex3t_sdl26_q4","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Renal Compensation Rules for Primary Respiratory Disturbances","batch":3,"stem":"A 62-year-old woman has had several days of epigastric pain with repeated vomiting. Ninety minutes after receiving intravenous morphine, she is somnolent with shallow respirations. Arterial pH is 7.28 (reference range: 7.35–7.45), PaCO2 70 mm Hg (reference range: 33–45 mm Hg), and HCO3- 32 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L). Which best explains her HCO3- value?","choices":{"A":"Renal compensation for chronic hypercapnia","B":"A preexisting metabolic alkalosis","C":"Acute mass action effect of the CO2 load","D":"A superimposed metabolic acidosis","E":"Normal bicarbonate handling by the kidney"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Ninety minutes of opioid-induced hypoventilation is acute respiratory acidosis, for which HCO3- rises 0.1 mEq/L per mm Hg: 24 + (0.1 × 30) = 27 mEq/L. Her HCO3- of 32 mEq/L exceeds that by 5 mEq/L, so a metabolic alkalosis, generated by loss of gastric H+ with vomiting, was already present; the ratio 32/(0.03 × 70) = 15.2 gives pH 7.28. Renal compensation is the trap: the chronic rule (0.3 to 0.4 mEq/L per mm Hg) predicts about 33 to 36 mEq/L, but renal compensation takes 3 to 5 days and has not developed within 90 minutes. The acute mass action effect accounts for about 3 mEq/L of the rise, not 8. A superimposed metabolic acidosis would lower HCO3- below 27 mEq/L. Normal renal handling would leave HCO3- near the 27 mEq/L acute prediction rather than at 32. Sources: Costanzo Physiology Ch. 7 (renal rules for respiratory acidosis; metabolic alkalosis from vomiting); Exam 3 lecture notes (SDL 26, acute and chronic delta rules).","boardPrep":"Step 1: The same PaCO2 predicts very different HCO3- values in acute and chronic respiratory acidosis, so establish the time course before applying the rules. COMLEX: A patient who has been vomiting can carry a metabolic alkalosis into opioid-induced hypercapnia, and the HCO3- value is what reveals it.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 -> Obj 2 — vomiting generates a metabolic alkalosis that volume contraction maintains","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex3t_sdl26_q5","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Acute vs. Chronic Respiratory Acidosis: Buffering & Renal Compensation","batch":3,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman develops laryngospasm shortly after extubation. Fifteen minutes later, arterial pH is 7.26 (reference range: 7.35–7.45), PaCO2 60 mm Hg (reference range: 33–45 mm Hg), and HCO3- 26 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L). Where is most of the H+ generated from her retained CO2 being buffered at this point?","choices":{"A":"Hemoglobin and other intracellular proteins","B":"Extracellular bicarbonate in the plasma","C":"Urinary phosphate as titratable acid","D":"Urinary ammonia trapped as ammonium","E":"Extracellular inorganic phosphate buffer"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Fifteen minutes of airway obstruction is acute respiratory acidosis, and her HCO3- of 26 mEq/L matches the acute rule, 24 + (0.1 × 20) = 26 mEq/L. Buffering of excess CO2 occurs in the intracellular fluid, especially red blood cells: lipid-soluble CO2 enters the cells, carbonic anhydrase converts it to H+ and HCO3-, and the H+ is bound by intracellular proteins such as deoxyhemoglobin and by organic phosphates, which is why HCO3- rises only slightly and pH stays low. Extracellular bicarbonate does not buffer CO2-derived H+, because CO2 and HCO3- belong to the same equilibrium system. Excretion of H+ as titratable acid and as NH4+ is part of renal compensation, which begins over hours and takes days to reach its maximum. Extracellular inorganic phosphate is present at only 1–2 mmol/L, and CO2 loads are buffered intracellularly. Sources: Costanzo Physiology Ch. 7 (buffering in respiratory acidosis and the phosphate buffer); Exam 3 lecture notes (SDL 26, immediate chemical buffering versus delayed renal compensation).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Acute respiratory acidosis is buffered intracellularly, mainly by hemoglobin, so HCO3- rises only about 1 mEq/L per 10 mm Hg of PaCO2. COMLEX: Upper airway obstruction, such as laryngospasm or aspiration, produces an acute respiratory acidosis with little HCO3- change.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 -> Obj 5 — deoxyhemoglobin is the main intracellular buffer for H+ formed from CO2","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Excretion of H+ as titratable "}},{"id":"ex3t_sdl26_q6","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Respiratory Alkalosis: HCO3- Wasting, Albumin-Ca2+ Binding, & Tetany","batch":3,"stem":"A 44-year-old woman develops sudden dyspnea and pleuritic chest pain 5 days after hip surgery, and CT angiography shows a pulmonary embolism. She is not taking aspirin and has no fever. Arterial pH is 7.50 (reference range: 7.35–7.45), PaCO2 30 mm Hg (reference range: 33–45 mm Hg), PaO2 60 mm Hg (reference range: 75–105 mm Hg), and HCO3- 23 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L). Which mechanism best explains her hyperventilation?","choices":{"A":"Medullary chemoreceptors sensing a high PaCO2","B":"Lactic acidosis lowering arterial pH first","C":"Direct medullary stimulation by a toxin","D":"Renal HCO3- loss lowering the blood pH","E":"Hypoxemia driving peripheral chemoreceptors"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Costanzo lists hypoxemia, as in high altitude, pneumonia, or pulmonary embolism, among the causes of respiratory alkalosis, because hypoxemia stimulates the peripheral chemoreceptors and drives hyperventilation; her low PaO2, low PaCO2, and high pH with a near-normal HCO3- fit an acute respiratory alkalosis from that mechanism. Her PaCO2 is low, so a high-PaCO2 stimulus to the medullary chemoreceptors is absent. Her pH is high and HCO3- is near normal, so no metabolic acidosis is driving the breathing. Direct stimulation of the medullary respiratory center is the mechanism of salicylate poisoning, gram-negative septicemia, hysterical hyperventilation, and neurologic disorders, none of which fits her. Renal HCO3- loss would lower pH, the opposite of her alkalemia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Causes of respiratory alkalosis: medullary stimulation (hysterical hyperventilation, gram-negative sepsis, salicylates, CNS tumor or stroke), hypoxemia via peripheral chemoreceptors (high altitude, pneumonia, pulmonary embolism), and mechanical ventilation. COMLEX: Unexplained hyperventilation with hypoxemia after surgery should raise concern for pulmonary embolism.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 2 — renal compensation rules for primary respiratory disturbances","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Her pH is high and HCO3- is ne"}}],"27":[{"id":"sdl27_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Seminiferous Tubule vs. Interstitial Compartments","batch":1,"stem":"What proportion of the adult testis is composed of seminiferous tubules?","choices":{"A":"About 20 percent of testicular volume","B":"About 80 percent of testicular volume","C":"About 50 percent of testicular volume","D":"About 65 percent of testicular volume","E":"About 95 percent of testicular volume"},"correct":"B","explanation":"About 80 percent of the adult testis is seminiferous tubules, the site of sperm production, and the remaining 20 percent is connective tissue interspersed with Leydig cells that secrete testosterone. The other percentages do not match this compartment ratio.","boardPrep":"Step 1: memorize the 80 to 20 split, seminiferous tubules to interstitium, since it anchors questions about which testicular function a lesion disrupts. COMLEX: the interstitial compartment is the smaller of the two but holds the endocrine cells.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 -> Obj 1 — Leydig cell testosterone synthesis is the interstitial function developed in detail there","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Seminiferous Tubule vs. Interstitial Compartments","batch":1,"stem":"Which testicular cell type synthesizes testosterone?","choices":{"A":"Sertoli cell","B":"Spermatogonium","C":"Granulosa cell","D":"Primary spermatocyte","E":"Leydig cell"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Leydig cells in the interstitial connective tissue synthesize and secrete testosterone in response to LH. Sertoli cells support developing sperm and secrete antimullerian hormone, androgen-binding protein, and inhibin, spermatogonia and spermatocytes are germ cells, and granulosa cells are ovarian.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Leydig cells make testosterone and Sertoli cells make sperm-supporting products; LH acts on the former and FSH on the latter. COMLEX: link the two cell types to the two testicular compartments.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 -> Obj 1 — Leydig cell testosterone synthesis is the interstitial function developed in detail there","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Sertoli cells support developi"}},{"id":"sdl27_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Seminiferous Tubule vs. Interstitial Compartments","batch":1,"stem":"Which cell population serves as the stem cells of the seminiferous epithelium?","choices":{"A":"Spermatogonia","B":"Sertoli cells","C":"Round spermatids","D":"Leydig cells","E":"Mature spermatozoa"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Spermatogonia are the stem cells of the seminiferous epithelium and divide mitotically to generate the spermatocytes destined to become mature sperm. Sertoli cells are supporting cells, spermatids and spermatozoa are later products, and Leydig cells lie outside the tubules.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the lining of the seminiferous tubules holds three cell types, spermatogonia as stem cells, spermatocytes in transit, and Sertoli cells as support. COMLEX: cryptorchidism and heat injure this epithelium first.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 -> Obj 1 — Leydig cell testosterone synthesis is the interstitial function developed in detail there","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Sertoli cells are supporting c","E":"Sertoli cells are supporting c"}},{"id":"sdl27_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Blood-Testis Barrier & Sertoli Cell Functions","batch":1,"stem":"What structure forms the blood-testis barrier?","choices":{"A":"Gap junctions between adjacent Leydig cells","B":"Desmosomes anchoring spermatogonia to the basement membrane","C":"The fibrous tunica albuginea surrounding the entire testis","D":"The fenestrated endothelium of interstitial capillaries","E":"Tight junctions between adjacent Sertoli cells"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Sertoli cells form tight junctions with one another along the seminiferous epithelium, creating the blood-testis barrier that isolates developing sperm from the bloodstream. It admits allowable substances such as testosterone while excluding noxious substances that could damage the germ cells. Spermatogonia sit in the basal compartment below the barrier, and the interstitial capillaries lie outside the tubules, so neither forms the barrier.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the blood-testis barrier is a Sertoli cell to Sertoli cell junction, not a vascular or capsular structure. COMLEX: it divides the tubule into a basal compartment and an adluminal compartment where meiosis and spermiogenesis proceed.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 -> Obj 3 — Sertoli cell inhibin B feedback builds on the Sertoli cell functions reviewed here","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl27_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Blood-Testis Barrier & Sertoli Cell Functions","batch":1,"stem":"Which role does the blood-testis barrier serve for developing germ cells?","choices":{"A":"It shields haploid germ cells from immune recognition","B":"It carries testosterone out of the tubule and into the plasma","C":"It lets circulating antibodies reach meiotic germ cells freely","D":"It concentrates FSH within the adluminal compartment","E":"It converts testosterone to dihydrotestosterone for germ cells"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Haploid germ cells appear after puberty and carry antigens the immune system has not learned to tolerate, so the barrier confers immune privilege by sequestering them from circulating immune cells and antibodies. The barrier is not a route for exporting testosterone, does not admit antibodies freely, and does not concentrate FSH or convert testosterone to dihydrotestosterone.","boardPrep":"Step 1: immune privilege of the testis explains why antisperm antibodies follow testicular trauma or infection. COMLEX: breach of the barrier is the mechanism to remember for autoimmune infertility.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 -> Obj 3 — Sertoli cell inhibin B feedback builds on the Sertoli cell functions reviewed here","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"The barrier is not a route for","C":"The barrier is not a route for"}},{"id":"sdl27_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Blood-Testis Barrier & Sertoli Cell Functions","batch":1,"stem":"What is the function of androgen-binding protein in the seminiferous tubule?","choices":{"A":"It carries testosterone through plasma to distant target tissues","B":"It converts testosterone to estradiol in the seminiferous tubule","C":"It inhibits FSH secretion from the anterior pituitary gland","D":"It keeps local testosterone levels high near developing sperm cells","E":"It suppresses antimullerian hormone release from fetal testes"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Sertoli cells secrete androgen-binding protein into the tubule lumen near developing sperm, where it binds testosterone and helps maintain a high local concentration, reinforcing FSH action on spermatogenesis. Transport in plasma is the role of sex hormone-binding globulin and albumin, and inhibin, not androgen-binding protein, feeds back on FSH.","boardPrep":"Step 1: androgen-binding protein acts locally in the tubule, whereas SHBG acts in the circulation. COMLEX: high intratubular testosterone is required for spermatogenesis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 -> Obj 3 — Sertoli cell inhibin B feedback builds on the Sertoli cell functions reviewed here","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Transport in plasma is the rol","C":"Transport in plasma is the rol"}},{"id":"sdl27_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Germ Cell Progression: Mitosis, Meiosis, & Spermiogenesis","batch":1,"stem":"Which cell is produced when a primary spermatocyte completes meiosis I?","choices":{"A":"Type A spermatogonial cell","B":"Secondary spermatocyte","C":"Round spermatid","D":"Primary spermatocyte","E":"Mature spermatozoon"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Meiosis I is the reductional division that converts a diploid primary spermatocyte into two haploid secondary spermatocytes. Meiosis II then divides each secondary spermatocyte into spermatids, which mature into spermatozoa by spermiogenesis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: primary spermatocyte to secondary spermatocyte marks meiosis I, and secondary spermatocyte to spermatid marks meiosis II. COMLEX: know which step halves the chromosome number.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 40 -> Obj 2 — cryptorchidism causes progressive seminiferous tubule and germ cell atrophy","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Meiosis II then divides each s","E":"Meiosis II then divides each s"}},{"id":"sdl27_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Germ Cell Progression: Mitosis, Meiosis, & Spermiogenesis","batch":1,"stem":"Which description best fits meiosis I in spermatogenesis?","choices":{"A":"An equational division that separates sister chromatids and keeps the diploid number","B":"A mitotic division that maintains ploidy while increasing spermatogonial numbers","C":"A reductional division that halves the chromosome number from diploid to haploid","D":"A remodeling step that converts round spermatids into flagellated sperm","E":"A division that begins after the acrosome has formed on the spermatid"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Meiosis I separates homologous chromosomes, so the chromosome number falls from diploid to haploid, whereas meiosis II is the equational division that separates sister chromatids. Mitotic spermatogonial divisions maintain ploidy, and remodeling of spermatids is spermiogenesis, not a meiotic division.","boardPrep":"Step 1: meiosis I is reductional and meiosis II is equational, which explains why aneuploidy from nondisjunction arises at either step. COMLEX: relate the ploidy transitions to genetic counseling questions.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 40 -> Obj 2 — cryptorchidism causes progressive seminiferous tubule and germ cell atrophy","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Mitotic spermatogonial divisio"}},{"id":"sdl27_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Germ Cell Progression: Mitosis, Meiosis, & Spermiogenesis","batch":1,"stem":"What occurs during spermiogenesis?","choices":{"A":"Spermatogonia divide mitotically to generate spermatocytes","B":"Primary spermatocytes undergo reduction division into haploid cells","C":"Secondary spermatocytes replicate DNA to restore the diploid number","D":"Spermatids lose cytoplasm and develop flagella to become mature sperm","E":"Sertoli cells transform into Leydig cells that secrete testosterone"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Spermiogenesis is the third phase of spermatogenesis, in which haploid spermatids are transformed into mature sperm through loss of cytoplasm and development of flagella, without further division. Mitosis of spermatogonia is the first phase and meiosis of spermatocytes is the second.","boardPrep":"Step 1: spermatogenesis has three phases, mitosis, meiosis, and spermiogenesis, and only the last involves no cell division. COMLEX: one full cycle takes about 64 days (Costanzo; Guyton gives about 74 days and First Aid 2025 about 2 months).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 40 -> Obj 2 — cryptorchidism causes progressive seminiferous tubule and germ cell atrophy","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Mitosis of spermatogonia is th","B":"Mitosis of spermatogonia is th","C":"Mitosis of spermatogonia is th"}},{"id":"sdl27_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Accessory Gland Secretions & Sperm Capacitation","batch":1,"stem":"Which accessory structure contributes a fluid rich in fructose, citrate, prostaglandins, and fibrinogen to the ejaculate?","choices":{"A":"Seminal vesicles","B":"Prostate gland","C":"Epididymal head","D":"Bulbourethral glands","E":"Rete testis tubules"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The seminal vesicles empty into the ejaculatory ducts and contribute a nutritive fluid rich in fructose, citrate, prostaglandins, and fibrinogen. The prostate adds a milky fluid rich in citrate, calcium, and enzymes, and the epididymis and rete testis store and transport sperm rather than adding this fluid. The bulbourethral glands add only a small mucous pre-ejaculate.","boardPrep":"Step 1: fructose in semen comes mainly from the seminal vesicles, and its absence suggests ejaculatory duct obstruction. COMLEX: the accessory glands together account for about 90 percent of semen volume.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 41 -> Obj 1 — prostatic secretions and prostatic disease follow from the gland physiology reviewed here","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl27_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Accessory Gland Secretions & Sperm Capacitation","batch":1,"stem":"Which description fits prostatic fluid?","choices":{"A":"An acidic viscous fluid containing fructose and prostaglandins","B":"A neutral watery fluid containing androgen-binding protein and inhibin","C":"A slightly alkaline milky fluid containing PSA and zinc","D":"A dense sperm-rich fluid stored for months and containing spermatozoa","E":"A clear mucous fluid containing fibrinogen and seminal coagulum proteins"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Prostatic secretion is a milky aqueous solution rich in citrate, calcium, and enzymes, with PSA and zinc, and it is slightly alkaline, which increases sperm motility and neutralizes acidic secretions. Fructose and prostaglandins come from the seminal vesicles, androgen-binding protein and inhibin from Sertoli cells, and stored sperm from the epididymis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PSA is a prostatic serine protease, and zinc is concentrated in prostatic secretion. COMLEX: know which gland contributes each component to interpret semen analysis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 41 -> Obj 1 — prostatic secretions and prostatic disease follow from the gland physiology reviewed here","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"D":"Fructose and prostaglandins co"}},{"id":"sdl27_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Accessory Gland Secretions & Sperm Capacitation","batch":1,"stem":"Which process occurs during sperm capacitation in the female reproductive tract?","choices":{"A":"Addition of prostatic enzymes to the sperm surface before ejaculation","B":"Removal of seminal inhibitory factors and membrane cholesterol","C":"Fusion of the acrosomal membrane with the oocyte plasma membrane","D":"Completion of meiosis II by the oocyte in the fallopian tube","E":"Conversion of testosterone to dihydrotestosterone within the epididymis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Capacitation takes 4 to 6 hours in the female tract (Costanzo; Guyton gives 1 to 10 hours) and washes inhibitory factors from the sperm, withdraws membrane cholesterol, redistributes surface proteins, and allows Ca2+ influx that increases whiplike motility. It also permits the acrosomal reaction. Completion of meiosis II is an oocyte event after fertilization. Testosterone is converted to dihydrotestosterone by 5α-reductase in androgen target tissues; that is not part of capacitation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: freshly ejaculated sperm cannot fertilize until capacitation has occurred. COMLEX: the acrosomal reaction is enabled by capacitation and releases enzymes that let sperm penetrate the ovum coverings.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 41 -> Obj 1 — prostatic secretions and prostatic disease follow from the gland physiology reviewed here","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl27_q13","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Seminiferous Tubule vs. Interstitial Compartments","batch":2,"stem":"A 28-year-old man presents with infertility. Semen analysis shows a severely reduced sperm count, while serum testosterone is normal and libido is intact. Which pattern of testicular involvement is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Impaired seminiferous tubules with preserved Leydig cell function","B":"Impaired interstitial cells with continued seminiferous tubule sperm production","C":"Equal impairment of both compartments from a shared blood supply","D":"Impaired interstitial cells with loss of androgen-binding protein secretion","E":"Impaired accessory glands with normal seminiferous tubule function"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Sperm are produced in the seminiferous tubules, which make up about 80 percent of the testis, whereas testosterone is produced by Leydig cells in the interstitium. Normal testosterone and libido with a low sperm count indicate that the tubules are selectively affected while the Leydig cells work normally. Accessory gland disease alters semen volume and composition, not sperm production.","boardPrep":"Step 1: separate the two testicular functions, sperm production in the tubules and testosterone in the interstitium, because lesions can affect them independently. COMLEX: normal androgen effects with infertility point to a tubule problem.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 1 — Leydig cell steroidogenesis explains why testosterone remains normal in tubule disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Normal testosterone and libido","D":"Normal testosterone and libido"}},{"id":"sdl27_q14","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Seminiferous Tubule vs. Interstitial Compartments","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old man with bilateral intra-abdominal undescended testes has azoospermia. Which mechanism best explains the failure of sperm production?","choices":{"A":"Leydig cells make too little testosterone in the abdominal environment","B":"The blood-testis barrier fails to form when the testis is not in the scrotum","C":"Sertoli cells stop secreting androgen-binding protein once the testis is intra-abdominal","D":"Core temperature exceeds the lower scrotal temperature that spermatogenesis needs","E":"Abdominal blood flow is too low to nourish the developing germ cells"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The scrotum is maintained 1 to 2 degrees C below body temperature by a countercurrent arrangement of testicular arteries and veins, and this lower temperature is essential for normal spermatogenesis. Undescended testes remain at core temperature, so germ cell maturation fails and azoospermia results. The other options describe cellular failures that are not established as the cause. Leydig cell testosterone production is unaffected by temperature, and although Sertoli cells are temperature sensitive, the failure is attributed to the heat itself, not a specific loss of androgen-binding protein.","boardPrep":"Step 1: temperature sensitivity of spermatogenesis explains why cryptorchidism, varicocele, and fever impair sperm production. COMLEX: orchiopexy is done early to protect germ cell function.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 40 Obj 2 — cryptorchidism produces progressive seminiferous tubule atrophy and heightened cancer risk","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q15","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Seminiferous Tubule vs. Interstitial Compartments","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old man receives an experimental chemotherapy agent that selectively destroys Leydig cells but spares the seminiferous tubule epithelium. Which hormonal change is expected first?","choices":{"A":"Falling antimullerian hormone from loss of Sertoli cell secretion","B":"Falling testosterone with a rise in LH from loss of negative feedback","C":"Rising inhibin B from expansion of Sertoli cell activity","D":"Falling FSH from greater Sertoli inhibin B feedback on the pituitary","E":"Rising testosterone from compensatory Sertoli cell steroidogenesis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Leydig cells synthesize testosterone, and when testosterone falls there is less negative feedback on the hypothalamus and anterior pituitary, so GnRH and LH secretion increase. Sertoli cells are spared, so antimullerian hormone and inhibin are unaffected, and Sertoli cells do not synthesize testosterone.","boardPrep":"Step 1: testosterone loss raises LH, and Leydig failure therefore produces high LH with low testosterone. COMLEX: this pattern helps separate primary testicular failure from pituitary disease.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 3 — testosterone negative feedback on hypothalamic GnRH and pituitary LH","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Sertoli cells are spared, so a","D":"Sertoli cells are spared, so a","E":"Sertoli cells are spared, so a"}},{"id":"sdl27_q16","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Blood-Testis Barrier & Sertoli Cell Functions","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old man develops antisperm antibodies after severe testicular trauma. Which mechanism best explains this finding?","choices":{"A":"Leydig cell destruction removed testosterone-mediated tolerance of germ cell antigens","B":"Loss of androgen-binding protein allowed circulating antibodies into the interstitium","C":"Failure of FSH signaling in Sertoli cells permitted recognition of spermatogonia","D":"Damaged Sertoli tight junctions exposed haploid germ cell antigens to immune cells","E":"Elevated inhibin B suppressed regulatory immune cells within the seminiferous tubule"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Haploid germ cells appear after puberty and are hidden from the immune system behind Sertoli cell tight junctions, which form the blood-testis barrier. Trauma that disrupts these junctions exposes their antigens to immune cells and provokes antisperm antibody formation. Androgen-binding protein, FSH signaling, Leydig cell function, and inhibin are not the primary determinants of this barrier.","boardPrep":"Step 1: sequestered antigens behind the blood-testis barrier explain immune infertility after trauma, infection, or vasectomy. COMLEX: the same principle applies to sympathetic ophthalmia in the eye.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 40 Obj 2 — testicular injury and its consequences for the seminiferous epithelium","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Androgen-binding protein, FSH ","E":"Androgen-binding protein, FSH "}},{"id":"sdl27_q17","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Blood-Testis Barrier & Sertoli Cell Functions","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old man with infertility has selective Sertoli cell damage and intact Leydig cell function. Which hormone panel is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Elevated LH with low testosterone and a normal FSH level","B":"Low FSH with elevated inhibin B and a normal LH level","C":"Elevated FSH with normal LH and normal testosterone","D":"Low FSH and low LH with low testosterone levels","E":"Elevated FSH and LH with elevated testosterone levels"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Sertoli cells secrete inhibin, which selectively inhibits FSH secretion from the anterior pituitary. With Sertoli cell damage, inhibin falls and FSH rises, whereas Leydig cells continue to make normal testosterone, so LH and testosterone remain normal. Low inhibin, not high inhibin, accompanies Sertoli cell failure.","boardPrep":"Step 1: inhibin from Sertoli cells is the FSH-selective feedback signal, and a high isolated FSH marks tubule damage. COMLEX: use FSH as a marker of spermatogenic status in male infertility.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 3 — inhibin B selectively inhibits pituitary FSH","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Low inhibin, not high inhibin,"}},{"id":"sdl27_q18","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Blood-Testis Barrier & Sertoli Cell Functions","batch":2,"stem":"In an animal study, a toxin selectively blocks one secretory function of Sertoli cells. Spermatids still mature into spermatozoa and the tight junctions between Sertoli cells remain intact, but the spermatozoa accumulate within the lumen of the seminiferous tubules instead of moving on to the epididymis. Loss of which Sertoli cell function best explains this finding?","choices":{"A":"Secretion of aqueous fluid that carries sperm along the tubules","B":"Secretion of androgen-binding protein near the developing sperm","C":"Secretion of inhibin that feeds back on pituitary FSH release","D":"Supply of nutrients to germ cells that are isolated from blood","E":"Secretion of anti-Mullerian hormone that regresses Mullerian ducts"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Costanzo lists four Sertoli cell functions that support spermatogenesis: supplying nutrients to the isolated germ cells, forming the tight junctions of the blood-testis barrier, secreting an aqueous fluid into the tubule lumen that helps transport sperm through the tubules into the epididymis, and secreting androgen-binding protein to keep local testosterone high; loss of the fluid explains sperm that form normally but do not move on. Loss of androgen-binding protein would impair sperm production rather than sperm transport. Inhibin regulates pituitary FSH secretion and has no role in moving sperm along the tubule. Loss of nutrient supply would prevent normal maturation of the germ cells, which is preserved. Anti-Mullerian hormone acts in the fetus to regress the paramesonephric ducts.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Sertoli cells: nutrients, blood-testis barrier (tight junctions), luminal fluid that carries sperm to the epididymis, and androgen-binding protein; they also secrete inhibin (FSH feedback) and, in the fetus, AMH. COMLEX: Sertoli cell injury shows up as a low sperm count with a selective FSH rise.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 3 — Sertoli cell inhibin selectively restrains FSH secretion","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl27_q19","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Germ Cell Progression: Mitosis, Meiosis, & Spermiogenesis","batch":2,"stem":"A 23-year-old man with normal fertility has a testicular biopsy for a research study. One of his primary spermatocytes is followed through both meiotic divisions and spermiogenesis. How many mature spermatozoa result, and what is their ploidy?","choices":{"A":"Two haploid spermatozoa","B":"Four haploid spermatozoa","C":"Four diploid spermatozoa","D":"Two diploid spermatozoa","E":"One haploid spermatozoon and three polar bodies"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Meiosis I produces two haploid secondary spermatocytes, and meiosis II divides each into two spermatids, giving four haploid spermatids that mature into four haploid spermatozoa by spermiogenesis. Polar bodies are a feature of oogenesis, not spermatogenesis, because the male divisions are symmetric.","boardPrep":"Step 1: one primary spermatocyte gives four sperm, whereas one primary oocyte gives one ovum. COMLEX: all four products are functional, unlike in oogenesis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 4 — primary testicular failure disrupts the germ cell line that these meiotic divisions produce","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q20","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Germ Cell Progression: Mitosis, Meiosis, & Spermiogenesis","batch":2,"stem":"A 25-year-old man has a mutation causing spermatogenic arrest at the primary spermatocyte stage. Which cell types are absent from his seminiferous tubules?","choices":{"A":"Spermatogonia, primary spermatocytes, and Sertoli cells","B":"Primary spermatocytes, spermatogonia, and secondary spermatocytes","C":"Spermatogonia, spermatids, and primary spermatocytes","D":"Secondary spermatocytes and Sertoli cells","E":"Secondary spermatocytes, spermatids, and spermatozoa"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Arrest at the primary spermatocyte stage means the cells cannot complete meiosis I, so all later stages, secondary spermatocytes, spermatids, and spermatozoa, are absent. Spermatogonia and primary spermatocytes are present, Sertoli cells are supporting cells that persist, and Leydig cells lie in the interstitium outside the tubules.","boardPrep":"Step 1: order the stages of spermatogenesis, spermatogonium, primary spermatocyte, secondary spermatocyte, spermatid, spermatozoon. COMLEX: a testicular biopsy showing maturation arrest identifies the stage of failure.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 4 — primary testicular failure alters the hormonal panel through loss of feedback","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Spermatogonia and primary sper","C":"Spermatogonia and primary sper"}},{"id":"sdl27_q21","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Germ Cell Progression: Mitosis, Meiosis, & Spermiogenesis","batch":2,"stem":"A 25-year-old man in a fertility study asks how many sperm his testes make. About 2 million of his spermatogonia begin spermatogenesis each day, and each gives rise to 64 spermatozoa. Approximately how many sperm does he produce daily?","choices":{"A":"128 million","B":"64 million","C":"256 million","D":"32 million","E":"512 million"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Multiplying 2 million spermatogonia by 64 spermatozoa each gives about 128 million sperm produced daily. Spermatogenesis is continuous from puberty to senescence, and a full cycle takes about 64 days (Costanzo; Guyton gives about 74 days and First Aid 2025 about 2 months), with the spermatogenic wave ensuring continuous output.","boardPrep":"Step 1: sperm production is continuous and about 64 days per cycle, in contrast to oogenesis, where the pool of oocytes only declines. COMLEX: these numbers explain why semen parameters reflect events about two months earlier.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 3 — Sertoli cell inhibin B feedback reflects sperm output","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q22","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Accessory Gland Secretions & Sperm Capacitation","batch":2,"stem":"A 32-year-old infertile man has a low ejaculate volume with no fructose and no sperm. His testes are normal in size, and FSH, LH, and testosterone are normal. Which is the most likely site of the lesion?","choices":{"A":"Seminiferous tubules with total germ cell arrest","B":"Prostate gland with loss of secretory epithelium","C":"Ejaculatory ducts that drain the seminal vesicles","D":"Bilateral obstruction of the epididymal tubules","E":"Leydig cells with loss of LH receptor signaling"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Fructose is contributed by the seminal vesicles, so its absence together with low volume points to obstruction of the ejaculatory ducts that deliver seminal vesicle fluid, which also blocks sperm. Germ cell arrest would raise FSH and leave volume and fructose normal, and epididymal obstruction would leave volume and fructose normal. Prostatic atrophy alters citrate, PSA, and zinc, and Leydig cell resistance affects testosterone.","boardPrep":"Step 1: absent fructose with low volume and normal testes localizes the lesion to the ejaculatory ducts or seminal vesicles. COMLEX: this is a classic obstructive azoospermia workup.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 41 Obj 1 — accessory gland disease and semen composition","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Prostatic atrophy alters citra"}},{"id":"sdl27_q23","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Accessory Gland Secretions & Sperm Capacitation","batch":2,"stem":"A 29-year-old man undergoing a fertility evaluation asks how the prostaglandins in his seminal fluid help sperm reach the oocyte. Which contribution of seminal vesicle prostaglandins best assists fertilization?","choices":{"A":"Triggering capacitation by removing cholesterol from the sperm membrane","B":"Providing fructose as the principal energy source for ejaculated sperm","C":"Neutralizing the acidic secretions of the vagina and vas deferens","D":"Liquefying the semen coagulum through proteolytic activity","E":"Easing cervical mucus penetration and aiding tubal peristalsis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Seminal fluid prostaglandins react with cervical mucus to make it more penetrable by sperm and induce peristaltic contractions of the uterus and fallopian tubes that propel sperm up the tract. Fructose is the nutritive component, prostatic alkalinity neutralizes acid, and capacitation is a process within the female tract, not a prostaglandin action. Liquefaction of the coagulum is by prostatic proteolytic enzymes such as PSA.","boardPrep":"Step 1: prostaglandins from the seminal vesicles aid sperm transport, and fructose feeds the sperm. COMLEX: distinguish the nutrient, alkalinizing, and transport roles of accessory fluids.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 41 Obj 1 — prostatic secretions and their disorders (prostatitis, BPH) alter the seminal fluid","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Fructose is the nutritive comp","B":"Fructose is the nutritive comp","C":"Fructose is the nutritive comp"}},{"id":"sdl27_q24","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Accessory Gland Secretions & Sperm Capacitation","batch":2,"stem":"A 33-year-old woman is undergoing in vitro fertilization. Her partner's freshly ejaculated sperm are placed directly with her mature oocytes without any laboratory preparation, and none of the oocytes are fertilized. Which explains this result?","choices":{"A":"Sperm have not yet completed meiosis II inside the female tract","B":"Sperm have not yet been capacitated by time in the female tract","C":"The acrosome was not shed in the epididymis before ejaculation","D":"Prostatic fluid has not yet converted testosterone to dihydrotestosterone","E":"Sertoli cells have not yet completed spermiogenesis after ejaculation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Ejaculated sperm cannot immediately fertilize an ovum. They must reside in the female reproductive tract for 4 to 6 hours (Costanzo; Guyton gives 1 to 10 hours) for capacitation, which removes inhibitory seminal factors and cholesterol, redistributes surface proteins, and enables the acrosomal reaction that lets sperm penetrate the ovum coverings. Meiosis, acrosome loss, and spermiogenesis are not the missing steps. Conversion of testosterone to dihydrotestosterone happens in androgen target tissues by 5α-reductase and plays no part in fertilization.","boardPrep":"Step 1: capacitation precedes the acrosomal reaction, and in vitro fertilization protocols must reproduce it. COMLEX: this is the practical reason sperm are incubated before insemination.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 2 — Sertoli cell support and androgen-binding protein produce the sperm that capacitation readies for fertilization","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"C":"Meiosis, acrosome loss, and sp"}},{"id":"ex3t_sdl27_q1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Seminiferous Tubule vs. Interstitial Compartments","batch":3,"stem":"A 7-year-old boy has 6 months of rapid growth, new pubic hair, and penile enlargement. A firm nontender right scrotal mass is palpable, and the opposite gonad is prepubertal in size. Serum testosterone is 620 ng/dL (reference range before puberty: <20 ng/dL), and LH is <0.1 mIU/mL (reference range before puberty: <0.3 mIU/mL). Which cells are the most likely source of the excess androgen?","choices":{"A":"Sertoli cells of the tubules","B":"Spermatogonia of the basal layer","C":"Leydig cells of the testis","D":"Primary spermatocytes in meiosis","E":"Efferent ductule epithelial cells"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Gonadotropin-independent virilization, shown by an adult-level testosterone with LH at a prepubertal level, plus a unilateral gonadal mass points to an autonomous androgen source within the gonad, and Leydig cells are the interstitial cells that synthesize testosterone and can form tumors that secrete large amounts. Sertoli cells line the tubules and secrete antimüllerian hormone, androgen-binding protein, and inhibin rather than testosterone. Spermatogonia are basal stem cells of the germ line and make no steroid. Primary spermatocytes are meiotic germ cells and make no steroid. Efferent ductule epithelium conveys sperm and fluid out of the gonad and is not steroidogenic. Sources: Exam 3 lecture notes (SDL 27, interstitial compartment and Leydig cell androgen synthesis); Guyton and Hall Physiology (interstitial Leydig cell tumors secreting testosterone).","boardPrep":"Step 1: A high testosterone with low LH and FSH signals a gonadotropin-independent androgen source, whereas a high testosterone with high LH points to central puberty or receptor resistance. COMLEX: Sudden childhood virilization with a unilateral gonadal mass should prompt a search for an interstitial cell tumor, and early epiphyseal fusion can shorten adult height.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 -> Obj 1 — these interstitial cells carry the LH receptor that drives testosterone synthesis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex3t_sdl27_q2","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Seminiferous Tubule vs. Interstitial Compartments","batch":3,"stem":"A 13-year-old boy is seen for a well visit. Over the past year his voice has deepened, axillary hair has appeared, and both gonads have enlarged substantially. Morning LH is 4.1 mIU/mL (reference range: 1.7–8.6 mIU/mL) and testosterone is 410 ng/dL (reference range: 300–1000 ng/dL). Which tissue accounts for most of the increase in gonadal size?","choices":{"A":"Expansion of the interstitial Leydig cells","B":"Growth of the seminiferous tubules","C":"Thickening of the fibrous tunica albuginea","D":"Enlargement of the coiled epididymal duct","E":"Dilation of the pampiniform venous plexus"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Gonadal growth at puberty is mostly growth of the seminiferous tubules, which make up about 80% of adult gonadal volume and house the spermatogenic epithelium that activates at spermarche. Leydig cells do proliferate and drive the rise in testosterone, but the interstitial compartment is only about 20% of the volume. The tunica albuginea is the fibrous capsule and does not explain the volume change. The epididymal duct lies outside the gonad, where sperm mature and are stored. The pampiniform plexus is the venous network that cools arterial blood and is not part of the parenchyma. Sources: Exam 3 lecture notes (SDL 27, seminiferous tubule compartment as about 80% of gonadal mass); Costanzo Physiology Ch. 10 (pubertal growth of the testis from the seminiferous tubules).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Gonadal volume is a bedside readout of the tubular compartment, so small gonads signal tubular loss even when testosterone is normal. COMLEX: Gonadal enlargement is an early sign of male puberty, and pubertal testosterone with prepubertal gonadal size should prompt a search for an outside androgen source.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 -> Obj 4 — testicular atrophy in anabolic steroid abuse","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex3t_sdl27_q3","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Blood-Testis Barrier & Sertoli Cell Functions","batch":3,"stem":"A 30-year-old man develops infertility several months after blunt scrotal trauma. Semen testing detects antibodies bound to his own sperm. Which structure normally prevents this immune response?","choices":{"A":"Fenestrated capillaries of the interstitium","B":"Lamina propria under the germinal epithelium","C":"Basement membrane of the seminiferous tubule","D":"Tight junctions joining adjacent Sertoli cells","E":"Smooth muscle of the peritubular myoid layer"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Adjacent Sertoli cells are joined by tight junctions that form the blood-testis barrier, which keeps lymphocytes, immunoglobulins, and complement away from the meiotic and haploid germ cells in the adluminal compartment, so trauma that breaches this barrier exposes germ cell antigens and triggers antisperm antibodies. Fenestrated interstitial capillaries deliver blood to the Leydig cells and do not seal off the germ cells. The lamina propria is loose connective tissue outside the barrier. The tubule basement membrane supports the epithelium but does not create the selective permeability. The peritubular myoid layer contracts to move fluid and sperm and does not isolate germ cells immunologically. Sources: Exam 3 lecture notes (SDL 27, blood-testis barrier and immune privilege); Costanzo Physiology Ch. 10 (Sertoli cell tight junctions and the blood-testis barrier).","boardPrep":"Step 1: The blood-testis barrier shields haploid germ cell antigens that arise after immune tolerance is set, so a breach can provoke antisperm antibodies. COMLEX: Antisperm antibodies after testicular injury can cause autoimmune orchitis and infertility.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 -> Obj 3 — Sertoli cell products in testicular feedback","isHighYield":false},{"id":"ex3t_sdl27_q4","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Germ Cell Progression: Mitosis, Meiosis, & Spermiogenesis","batch":3,"stem":"In a testicular biopsy processed for cytogenetics, one germ cell population lies in the adluminal compartment. Each cell has 46 chromosomes, each with two sister chromatids, a 4C DNA content, and homologous chromosomes aligned as tetrads that are exchanging segments. Which cells are these?","choices":{"A":"Secondary spermatocytes after reductional division","B":"Committed type B spermatogonia before meiosis","C":"Primary spermatocytes in meiotic prophase","D":"Round haploid spermatids after equational division","E":"Self-renewing type A spermatogonial stem cells"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Primary spermatocytes replicate their DNA before meiosis, giving 46 two-chromatid chromosomes and 4C DNA, and in prophase I they pair homologs into tetrads that cross over, matching every feature listed. Secondary spermatocytes follow meiosis I with 23 duplicated chromosomes and 2C DNA and show no homologous pairing. Type B spermatogonia are mitotic progenitors in the basal compartment that do not form tetrads. Round spermatids are haploid products of meiosis II with 23 single-chromatid chromosomes and 1C DNA. Type A spermatogonia are basal stem cells that divide by mitosis and do not pair homologs. Sources: Exam 3 lecture notes (SDL 27, meiotic phase ploidy and DNA transitions); Costanzo Physiology Ch. 10 (meiotic divisions of the spermatocytes).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Track ploidy with DNA content: primary spermatocyte 2n/4C, secondary spermatocyte 1n/2C, spermatid 1n/1C, and recombination occurs only in prophase I. COMLEX: Primary spermatocytes are the first germ cells to enter the adluminal compartment, where the blood-testis barrier shields meiotic cells.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 -> Obj 2 — basal and adluminal compartments of the seminiferous epithelium","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Type B spermatogonia are mitot"}},{"id":"ex3t_sdl27_q5","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Germ Cell Progression: Mitosis, Meiosis, & Spermiogenesis","batch":3,"stem":"A 33-year-old man has azoospermia with a normal ejaculate volume. Biopsy shows basal stem cells, abundant cells in meiotic prophase, and abundant round cells near the lumen that each carry 23 single-chromatid chromosomes, but no cells with condensed elongated nuclei, acrosomal caps, or tails. Which step of germ cell development is failing?","choices":{"A":"Reductional separation of homologous chromosomes","B":"Equational separation of the sister chromatids","C":"Mitotic proliferation of diploid stem cells","D":"Release of mature sperm from the Sertoli cells","E":"Remodeling of spermatids into mature sperm"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Round haploid cells with 23 single-chromatid chromosomes are spermatids, so both meiotic divisions are complete, yet no cells have condensed elongated heads, acrosomes, or flagella; the failing step is spermiogenesis, the structural remodeling of spermatids into mature sperm. Separation of homologs in meiosis I is intact because haploid descendants are present. Separation of sister chromatids in meiosis II is intact because the round cells already have single-chromatid chromosomes. Mitotic proliferation is intact because basal stem cells and their meiotic progeny are abundant. Release of finished sperm is not the first failure, because mature sperm do not form. Sources: Exam 3 lecture notes (SDL 27, spermiogenesis as acrosome, nuclear condensation, and flagellar assembly); Costanzo Physiology Ch. 10 (transformation of spermatids into sperm).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Spermiogenesis involves no cell division; it is structural remodeling with a Golgi-derived acrosome and a mitochondria-wrapped flagellum. COMLEX: The last cell type present on biopsy marks the stage just before the failed step, localizing a spermatogenic arrest.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 -> Obj 4 — azoospermia in anabolic steroid abuse","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex3t_sdl27_q6","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Accessory Gland Secretions & Sperm Capacitation","batch":3,"stem":"In an in vitro study, freshly ejaculated sperm are placed directly with a mature oocyte and fail to fertilize it, whereas sperm first incubated in fluid from the uterus and fallopian tubes fertilize it readily. Which change in the incubated sperm best explains their new fertilizing ability?","choices":{"A":"Loss of membrane cholesterol and inhibitory coat","B":"Acquisition of forward motility in the epididymis","C":"Lysis of the seminal coagulum by prostatic enzymes","D":"Neutralization of vaginal acid by alkaline secretions","E":"Uptake of fructose supplied by the seminal vesicles"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Ejaculated sperm must undergo capacitation in the female tract, during which inhibitory surface factors are washed away and membrane cholesterol is withdrawn, raising membrane fluidity and allowing calcium influx and the acrosome reaction, so tubal and uterine fluid confers fertilizing ability. Motility is acquired earlier, during passage through the epididymis, and does not by itself allow fertilization. Lysis of the seminal coagulum by prostatic enzymes frees sperm within semen but does not capacitate them. Neutralizing vaginal acid raises sperm motility but does not remove the membrane cholesterol that blocks the acrosome reaction. Fructose from the seminal vesicles fuels motility and is not the capacitation step. Sources: Exam 3 lecture notes (SDL 27, female-tract capacitation and cholesterol depletion); Guyton and Hall Physiology (capacitation of spermatozoa in the female genital tract).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Capacitation removes a cholesterol-stabilized inhibitory coat and primes the acrosome reaction, which is why freshly ejaculated sperm do not fertilize an ovum right away. COMLEX: In assisted reproduction, sperm are capacitated in vitro to reproduce the changes normally supplied by the female tract.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 -> Obj 1 — DHT-dependent growth of the prostate, a source of seminal fluid","isHighYield":false}],"28":[{"id":"sdl28_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Leydig Cell Steroidogenesis & the Rate-Limiting Step","batch":1,"stem":"Which enzyme catalyzes the LH-stimulated first step of testicular steroidogenesis?","choices":{"A":"17-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase","B":"5-alpha-reductase type 2","C":"Cholesterol desmolase","D":"Steroid 11-beta-hydroxylase","E":"21-beta-hydroxylase enzyme"},"correct":"C","explanation":"LH stimulates Leydig cells to synthesize testosterone by increasing the activity of cholesterol desmolase, the first step of the steroidogenic pathway, which parallels the action of ACTH in the adrenal cortex. The remaining enzymes act later in the pathway or in other tissues. 17β-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase acts late, converting androstenedione to testosterone, and 5α-reductase converts testosterone to dihydrotestosterone in target tissues.","boardPrep":"Step 1: cholesterol desmolase catalyzes the first, conventionally rate-limiting step of steroidogenesis, stimulated by LH in Leydig cells and by ACTH in the adrenal cortex. COMLEX: think of LH as the testicular equivalent of ACTH.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 -> Obj 1 — testosterone and its 5-alpha-reductase product DHT act on different target tissues","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl28_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Leydig Cell Steroidogenesis & the Rate-Limiting Step","batch":1,"stem":"What is the androgenic end product of the testes?","choices":{"A":"Dehydroepiandrosterone","B":"Androstenedione","C":"Dihydrotestosterone","D":"Testosterone","E":"Estradiol"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The testes contain 17-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase, which converts androstenedione to testosterone, so testosterone is the androgenic end product. Dehydroepiandrosterone and androstenedione are the androgenic end products of the adrenal cortex, dihydrotestosterone is formed in target tissues by 5-alpha-reductase, and estradiol is the major ovarian estrogen.","boardPrep":"Step 1: testes end with testosterone, adrenals end with DHEA and androstenedione, and target tissues generate DHT. COMLEX: know which steroid each organ ultimately secretes.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 -> Obj 1 — testosterone and its 5-alpha-reductase product DHT act on different target tissues","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl28_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Leydig Cell Steroidogenesis & the Rate-Limiting Step","batch":1,"stem":"Why can the testes not synthesize glucocorticoids or mineralocorticoids?","choices":{"A":"They lack the 21- and 11-beta-hydroxylases","B":"They lack cholesterol desmolase in Leydig cells","C":"They lack 17-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase","D":"They lack LH receptors on Leydig cells","E":"They lack aromatase for estrogen synthesis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The testicular steroidogenic pathway resembles the adrenal pathway, but the testes lack 21-hydroxylase and 11-beta-hydroxylase, so they cannot make glucocorticoids or mineralocorticoids. They do have cholesterol desmolase and 17-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase, and Leydig cells express LH receptors. The testes do express aromatase, but estrogen synthesis is not what blocks corticoid production.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the testes lack 21- and 11-beta-hydroxylase, so they make no cortisol or aldosterone. COMLEX: this is why 21-hydroxylase deficiency does not directly impair testicular testosterone synthesis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 -> Obj 1 — testosterone and its 5-alpha-reductase product DHT act on different target tissues","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl28_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — GnRH Pulsatility vs. Continuous Agonist Therapy","batch":1,"stem":"Which pattern of GnRH secretion drives normal FSH and LH release in men?","choices":{"A":"Continuous release","B":"Pulsatile release","C":"Release confined to sleep","D":"Tonic low-level release","E":"Single midcycle surge release"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Throughout the reproductive period GnRH secretion is pulsatile, and it drives a parallel pulsatile secretion of FSH and LH from the anterior pituitary. Continuous exposure down-regulates GnRH receptors and suppresses gonadotropin secretion, and a single midcycle surge belongs to the female cycle. GnRH is pulsatile throughout, not confined to sleep or purely tonic.","boardPrep":"Step 1: pulsatile GnRH stimulates gonadotropins, whereas continuous GnRH inhibits them. COMLEX: this principle underlies both puberty induction and GnRH agonist therapy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 41 -> Obj 2 — GnRH agonist androgen deprivation is applied to prostatic adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"GnRH is pulsatile throughout, "}},{"id":"sdl28_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — GnRH Pulsatility vs. Continuous Agonist Therapy","batch":1,"stem":"What is the effect of continuous exposure to a GnRH agonist on pituitary gonadotrophs?","choices":{"A":"Receptor up-regulation that sustains LH and FSH secretion","B":"Receptor sensitization that amplifies pulsatile LH release","C":"Down-regulation of receptors that suppresses LH and FSH","D":"Receptor internalization that suppresses FSH but spares LH","E":"Receptor stabilization that shifts secretion toward FSH"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Continuous high levels of GnRH down-regulate GnRH receptors on anterior pituitary gonadotrophs and inhibit both FSH and LH secretion. This principle is used to suppress LH and testosterone in prostate cancer. Pulsatile, not continuous, stimulation up-regulates receptors and sustains gonadotropin release.","boardPrep":"Step 1: continuous GnRH agonists such as leuprolide cause an initial transient stimulation followed by profound suppression of LH, FSH, and testosterone. COMLEX: know that the therapeutic effect depends on desensitization.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 41 -> Obj 2 — GnRH agonist androgen deprivation is applied to prostatic adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — GnRH Pulsatility vs. Continuous Agonist Therapy","batch":1,"stem":"Which drug exploits continuous GnRH receptor stimulation to lower testosterone in prostate cancer?","choices":{"A":"Leuprolide","B":"Finasteride","C":"Flutamide","D":"Ketoconazole","E":"Bicalutamide"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Leuprolide is a long-acting GnRH agonist that, given continuously, down-regulates pituitary GnRH receptors and lowers LH and testosterone. Finasteride inhibits 5-alpha-reductase, flutamide and bicalutamide block the androgen receptor, and ketoconazole inhibits steroid synthesis, so none of them works through GnRH receptor desensitization.","boardPrep":"Step 1: distinguish central suppression by GnRH agonists from peripheral androgen receptor blockade. COMLEX: androgen deprivation therapy is a mainstay of advanced prostate cancer treatment.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 41 -> Obj 2 — GnRH agonist androgen deprivation is applied to prostatic adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Dual Negative Feedback: Testosterone vs. Inhibin B","batch":1,"stem":"Which testicular hormone selectively inhibits FSH secretion by the anterior pituitary?","choices":{"A":"Testosterone secreted by Leydig cells","B":"Activin secreted by granulosa cells","C":"Androgen-binding protein secreted into the tubule lumen","D":"Antimullerian hormone secreted by fetal Sertoli cells","E":"Inhibin secreted by Sertoli cells"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Sertoli cells secrete inhibin, a glycoprotein of the transforming growth factor-beta superfamily, which serves as a feedback inhibitor of FSH secretion and acts as an indicator of spermatogenic activity. Testosterone inhibits GnRH and LH, activin increases FSH, and androgen-binding protein and antimullerian hormone are not pituitary feedback signals.","boardPrep":"Step 1: testosterone feeds back on GnRH and LH, whereas inhibin feeds back selectively on FSH. COMLEX: FSH tracks Sertoli cell and spermatogenic status.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 -> Obj 4 — loss of testicular feedback raises LH and FSH in primary hypogonadism","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Dual Negative Feedback: Testosterone vs. Inhibin B","batch":1,"stem":"At which levels of the axis does testosterone exert negative feedback?","choices":{"A":"On the hypothalamus with no direct pituitary effect","B":"On the anterior pituitary with no hypothalamic effect","C":"On Sertoli cells to reduce inhibin secretion","D":"On both the hypothalamus and the anterior pituitary","E":"On the posterior pituitary to reduce oxytocin release"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In the first of two feedback paths, testosterone feeds back on both the hypothalamus and the anterior lobe, where it inhibits secretion of GnRH and LH. At the hypothalamic level it decreases both the frequency and amplitude of GnRH pulses. The second path is inhibin acting on FSH. The posterior pituitary and oxytocin are not part of this loop, which acts on GnRH and LH.","boardPrep":"Step 1: testosterone has two feedback targets, hypothalamic GnRH and pituitary LH, and inhibin has one target, FSH. COMLEX: high testosterone from any source suppresses LH.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 -> Obj 4 — loss of testicular feedback raises LH and FSH in primary hypogonadism","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Dual Negative Feedback: Testosterone vs. Inhibin B","batch":1,"stem":"What does testosterone decrease at the hypothalamus?","choices":{"A":"The number of GnRH neurons in the arcuate nucleus","B":"The frequency and amplitude of GnRH pulses","C":"The half-life of GnRH in portal blood","D":"The binding of GnRH to Sertoli cell receptors","E":"The delivery of GnRH to the posterior pituitary"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Testosterone inhibits GnRH secretion by decreasing both the frequency and the amplitude of GnRH pulses. It does not reduce neuron number, alter portal blood half-life, or act through Sertoli cell GnRH receptors, and GnRH is delivered to the anterior lobe, not the posterior pituitary.","boardPrep":"Step 1: negative feedback changes pulse frequency and amplitude, not the presence of GnRH neurons. COMLEX: removal of the testes increases both GnRH pulse frequency and amplitude.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 -> Obj 4 — loss of testicular feedback raises LH and FSH in primary hypogonadism","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Primary vs. Secondary Hypogonadism & Kallmann Syndrome","batch":1,"stem":"Which hormone panel defines primary hypogonadism?","choices":{"A":"Low testosterone with elevated LH and FSH","B":"Low testosterone with low LH and FSH","C":"Normal testosterone with elevated LH and low FSH","D":"High testosterone with low LH and FSH","E":"Low testosterone with elevated LH and low FSH"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In primary hypogonadism the testes fail, so testosterone and inhibin fall and the loss of negative feedback raises LH and FSH, which is why it is called hypergonadotropic hypogonadism. Low testosterone with low LH and FSH indicates hypothalamic or pituitary failure, which is secondary hypogonadism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: check the gonadotropins to localize the lesion, high in testicular failure and low in central failure. COMLEX: primary means the gonad, secondary means the pituitary or hypothalamus.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 -> Obj 3 — negative feedback from testosterone and inhibin B sets the LH and FSH levels compared in these disorders","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Low testosterone with low LH a"}},{"id":"sdl28_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Primary vs. Secondary Hypogonadism & Kallmann Syndrome","batch":1,"stem":"Which defect causes Kallmann syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Leydig cell failure to respond to LH","B":"Absent SRY gene product in the fetal gonad","C":"Absent androgen receptors in target cells","D":"Loss of Sertoli cell inhibin secretion","E":"Failed GnRH neuron migration into the brain"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Kallmann syndrome is a form of secondary hypogonadotropic hypogonadism caused by failure of GnRH-secreting neurons to migrate from the olfactory placode to the hypothalamus, so GnRH, LH, FSH, and testosterone are all low. The other options describe Leydig cell resistance, sex determination failure, androgen insensitivity, and Sertoli cell dysfunction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Kallmann syndrome combines hypogonadotropic hypogonadism with anosmia because olfactory and GnRH neurons migrate together. COMLEX: delayed puberty with no sense of smell is the clinical clue.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 -> Obj 3 — negative feedback from testosterone and inhibin B sets the LH and FSH levels compared in these disorders","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"The other options describe Ley"}},{"id":"sdl28_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Primary vs. Secondary Hypogonadism & Kallmann Syndrome","batch":1,"stem":"Which additional feature is characteristic of Kallmann syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Bilateral early cataracts","B":"Absent sense of smell","C":"Macroglossia with hypotonia","D":"Galactorrhea with visual field loss","E":"Hypertension with hypokalemia"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Because GnRH neurons and olfactory neurons migrate together during development, Kallmann syndrome features anosmia or hyposmia along with delayed or absent puberty. Cataracts, macroglossia, galactorrhea with visual field loss, and hypertension with hypokalemia point to unrelated disorders.","boardPrep":"Step 1: pair hypogonadotropic hypogonadism with anosmia to recognize Kallmann syndrome. COMLEX: test smell in any adolescent with absent puberty and low gonadotropins.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 -> Obj 3 — negative feedback from testosterone and inhibin B sets the LH and FSH levels compared in these disorders","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q13","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Leydig Cell Steroidogenesis & the Rate-Limiting Step","batch":2,"stem":"A newborn with an XX genotype has ambiguous genitalia, an enlarged clitoris, and ovaries because of a congenital lack of adrenal 21-beta-hydroxylase. Which mechanism explains the virilization?","choices":{"A":"Excess LH drives ovarian testosterone synthesis through 17-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase","B":"Precursors accumulate behind the block and are shunted into adrenal androgen production","C":"Testes secrete testosterone because the enzyme block is absent in gonadal tissue","D":"Loss of aromatase activity raises adrenal cortisol and secondarily raises androgens","E":"Enhanced 5-alpha-reductase in the adrenal cortex converts cortisol to androgens"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Without 21-beta-hydroxylase, steroid precursors accumulate behind the block and are directed toward adrenal androgens, dehydroepiandrosterone and androstenedione, which masculinize the external genitalia in utero. The child has ovaries and no testes, and low cortisol raises ACTH, causing adrenal hyperplasia. The other options invoke enzymes or organs that are not responsible.","boardPrep":"Step 1: 21-hydroxylase deficiency shunts precursors to androgens, causing virilization in XX infants and adrenal hyperplasia from high ACTH. COMLEX: compare with the testes, which normally lack 21-hydroxylase.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 1 — external genital differentiation depends on androgen action and DHT","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"C":"The child has ovaries and no t"}},{"id":"sdl28_q14","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Leydig Cell Steroidogenesis & the Rate-Limiting Step","batch":2,"stem":"Two 55-year-old men each have a total serum testosterone of 450 ng/dL (reference range: 300–1000 ng/dL). One of them has a markedly raised estrogen level, which has doubled his concentration of sex hormone-binding globulin (SHBG). Compared with the other man, which statement about him is most accurate?","choices":{"A":"His albumin-bound testosterone becomes the main active fraction","B":"His androgen effects are greater, as SHBG delivers testosterone","C":"His LH falls, because SHBG binding increases hypothalamic feedback","D":"His DHT is higher, because SHBG stimulates 5-alpha-reductase","E":"His free, active testosterone is lower than the other's"},"correct":"E","explanation":"About 98% of circulating testosterone is bound to plasma proteins, roughly 45% to SHBG and the rest to albumin, and only the small free fraction is biologically active; SHBG acts as a reservoir, and its synthesis is stimulated by estrogens and inhibited by androgens, so doubling SHBG binds more hormone and leaves less free testosterone at the same total level. Albumin-bound testosterone is bound, not free, so it does not become the active fraction. SHBG holds testosterone in the circulation rather than delivering extra hormone to cells, so androgen effects are not greater. Bound testosterone is inactive, so more binding cannot strengthen feedback on the hypothalamus. SHBG does not act on 5-alpha-reductase, the target-tissue enzyme that forms DHT.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Testosterone in plasma is about 98% protein-bound (SHBG about 45%, the rest albumin); only free hormone acts; estrogens raise and androgens lower SHBG synthesis. COMLEX: When total testosterone is borderline, consider SHBG, because a high SHBG lowers the active free hormone.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 3 — free testosterone enters target cells and binds the intracellular androgen receptor","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl28_q15","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Leydig Cell Steroidogenesis & the Rate-Limiting Step","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old man has a bilateral orchiectomy for testicular cancer and declines hormone replacement. His adrenal cortex continues to secrete androgens, yet he develops signs of marked androgen deficiency. Which biochemical difference best explains this?","choices":{"A":"Little adrenal conversion of cholesterol to pregnenolone","B":"Little adrenal conversion of progesterone to deoxycorticosterone","C":"Little adrenal conversion of androstenedione to testosterone","D":"Little adrenal conversion of cholesterol to androstenedione","E":"Little adrenal conversion of pregnenolone to progesterone"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The adrenal cortex has little 17-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase activity, so its androgenic end products are dehydroepiandrosterone and androstenedione, whereas the testes, which have this enzyme, produce testosterone. The adrenal cortex does make pregnenolone, progesterone, deoxycorticosterone, and androstenedione.","boardPrep":"Step 1: 17-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase is the testicular step that converts androstenedione to testosterone. COMLEX: adrenal androgens are weak precursors that need peripheral conversion.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 1 — testosterone, not the weak adrenal androgens, acts directly on muscle and voice and is converted to DHT in target tissues","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"The adrenal cortex does make p","D":"The adrenal cortex does make p"}},{"id":"sdl28_q16","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — GnRH Pulsatility vs. Continuous Agonist Therapy","batch":2,"stem":"A 15-year-old boy has delayed puberty from isolated GnRH deficiency. Which regimen most likely initiates puberty and establishes reproductive function?","choices":{"A":"A depot GnRH analog given at three-month intervals","B":"A continuous high-dose GnRH infusion over several weeks","C":"A daily oral FSH tablet given at bedtime","D":"Intermittent GnRH analog pulses that mimic normal release","E":"A GnRH receptor blocker given before each sleep period"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Pulsatility of the hypothalamic-pituitary axis is required for normal reproductive function, so intermittent pulses of a GnRH analog replicate the normal secretory pattern and initiate puberty. If a long-acting GnRH analog is given, puberty is not initiated because continuous exposure down-regulates receptors. A receptor blocker would suppress gonadotropins.","boardPrep":"Step 1: pulsatile GnRH stimulates the axis and continuous GnRH shuts it down. COMLEX: therapy for GnRH deficiency must be delivered as a pulsatile pattern.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 46 Obj 6 — drug effects on the HPG axis depend on how the axis is stimulated or suppressed","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"If a long-acting GnRH analog i","B":"If a long-acting GnRH analog i"}},{"id":"sdl28_q17","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — GnRH Pulsatility vs. Continuous Agonist Therapy","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old man with metastatic prostate cancer receives depot leuprolide for several months. Which steady-state hormone profile is expected?","choices":{"A":"Low LH and testosterone from GnRH receptor down-regulation","B":"High LH and low testosterone from primary testicular failure","C":"High LH and high testosterone from pituitary receptor sensitization","D":"Low LH and high testosterone from increased Leydig cell sensitivity","E":"Normal LH and low testosterone from direct Leydig cell blockade"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Continuous GnRH agonist exposure down-regulates pituitary GnRH receptors, so LH and FSH secretion fall and Leydig cells receive little stimulation, lowering testosterone. This profile is central, not testicular, so LH is low rather than high. An early transient rise in LH and testosterone can occur before down-regulation is established.","boardPrep":"Step 1: agonist therapy produces a central, secondary hypogonadal pattern, with low LH and low testosterone. COMLEX: the initial flare is the reason antiandrogens are sometimes given at the start.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 41 Obj 2 — androgen deprivation therapy in prostatic adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q18","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — GnRH Pulsatility vs. Continuous Agonist Therapy","batch":2,"stem":"A 12-year-old boy is entering puberty as pulsatile GnRH secretion begins. How does this pulsatile GnRH change the responsiveness of his anterior pituitary?","choices":{"A":"GnRH down-regulates its own receptors, which lowers FSH and LH release","B":"GnRH up-regulates its own receptors and raises FSH and LH output per dose","C":"GnRH inhibits LH synthesis so FSH becomes the dominant gonadotropin","D":"GnRH inhibits melatonin release, which directly raises testosterone","E":"GnRH converts receptors to a tonic mode with constant FSH release"},"correct":"B","explanation":"At puberty pulsatile GnRH up-regulates its own receptors on anterior pituitary gonadotrophs, so a given concentration of GnRH produces greater FSH and LH secretion. The relative secretion also shifts so that LH exceeds FSH, the opposite of the childhood pattern. Down-regulation occurs with continuous, not pulsatile, exposure.","boardPrep":"Step 1: pulsatile GnRH up-regulates its receptors and continuous GnRH down-regulates them. COMLEX: during childhood FSH exceeds LH, and after puberty LH exceeds FSH.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 46 Obj 6 — recreational THC suppresses GnRH, LH, and FSH secretion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"The relative secretion also sh"}},{"id":"sdl28_q19","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Dual Negative Feedback: Testosterone vs. Inhibin B","batch":2,"stem":"Researchers isolate, from cultured Sertoli cells, the hormone whose fall explains the isolated rise in FSH seen in men with seminiferous tubule damage. The hormone acts directly on the anterior pituitary. To which molecular class does this hormone belong?","choices":{"A":"A glycoprotein of the TGF-beta superfamily","B":"A steroid acting on nuclear receptors","C":"A small peptide acting via guanylyl cyclase","D":"A catecholamine acting on beta receptors","E":"An eicosanoid acting on nearby cells"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The hormone is inhibin, which Costanzo describes as a glycoprotein and a member of the transforming growth factor-beta superfamily, and Guyton describes as a glycoprotein, like LH and FSH, isolated from cultured Sertoli cells, with a strong direct effect on the anterior pituitary to inhibit FSH secretion. A steroid acting on nuclear receptors describes testosterone, the feedback signal for LH. A small peptide acting through guanylyl cyclase describes the natriuretic peptides. A catecholamine acting on beta receptors is not a Sertoli cell product. Eicosanoids such as prostaglandins are local mediators, not the Sertoli feedback hormone.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Two feedback paths: testosterone (a steroid) inhibits GnRH and LH, and inhibin (a TGF-beta superfamily glycoprotein from Sertoli cells) selectively inhibits FSH, acting as an indicator of spermatogenic activity. COMLEX: Low inhibin B with a high FSH points to seminiferous tubule failure.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — Sertoli cell functions within the seminiferous tubule","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q20","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Dual Negative Feedback: Testosterone vs. Inhibin B","batch":2,"stem":"A 31-year-old man completed chemotherapy for lymphoma 18 months ago. On serial visits his sperm count has been rising steadily, and his FSH has fallen from 20 to 9 mIU/mL (reference range: 1.5–12.4 mIU/mL), while his LH and testosterone have stayed normal and unchanged. Which mechanism best explains the falling FSH?","choices":{"A":"Rising testosterone feedback on hypothalamic GnRH release","B":"Rising Sertoli cell inhibin as spermatogenesis recovers","C":"Down-regulation of gonadotroph GnRH receptors by recovery","D":"Recovery of Leydig cell responsiveness to circulating LH","E":"Slower GnRH pulses from recovery of the hypothalamus itself"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Inhibin from Sertoli cells serves as an indicator of the spermatogenic activity of the testes: when the seminiferous tubules fail to produce sperm, FSH rises markedly, and as spermatogenesis recovers, rising inhibin selectively lowers FSH again, without changing LH. Rising testosterone feedback would also lower LH, and his testosterone is unchanged. Down-regulation of GnRH receptors follows continuous GnRH exposure and would lower LH as well as FSH. Leydig cell responsiveness to LH governs testosterone, which has not changed. A change in GnRH pulse pattern would alter LH too, whereas his fall in FSH is selective.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Spermatogenic failure → low inhibin → selective FSH rise; recovery → inhibin rises → FSH falls; LH tracks testosterone feedback. COMLEX: Serial FSH can follow recovery of the germinal epithelium after gonadotoxic chemotherapy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 3 — germ cell progression that resumes as spermatogenesis recovers","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"A change in GnRH pulse pattern"}},{"id":"sdl28_q21","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Dual Negative Feedback: Testosterone vs. Inhibin B","batch":2,"stem":"A 29-year-old man received testicular irradiation 1 year ago. He is now azoospermic. His testosterone and LH are within the reference ranges, and his FSH is elevated. Which statement best explains why his testosterone and LH have stayed normal?","choices":{"A":"Sertoli cell inhibin suppresses LH along with FSH","B":"Adrenal androgens replace the lost testicular output","C":"Germ cells are the main testosterone source and survived","D":"LH secretion is independent of testosterone feedback","E":"Leydig cells survive radiation and sustain LH feedback"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Guyton notes that when the germinal epithelium is destroyed by x-ray treatment or excessive heat, the Leydig cells, which are less easily destroyed, often continue to produce testosterone; testosterone feedback on the hypothalamus and pituitary therefore keeps LH normal, while loss of spermatogenesis and inhibin lets FSH rise. Inhibin selectively inhibits FSH, not LH. Adrenal androgens normally provide less than 5% of total masculinizing activity in men and cannot replace testicular testosterone. Testosterone comes from Leydig cells, not germ cells, and his germ cells have been lost. LH secretion is controlled by testosterone negative feedback, which is why it stays normal while testosterone is normal.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Two compartments, two loops: Leydig cells and testosterone regulate LH; Sertoli cells and inhibin regulate FSH; radiation or heat damage to the germinal epithelium raises FSH selectively. COMLEX: Azoospermia with normal testosterone and LH after radiation reflects tubular, not Leydig, injury.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 1 — the seminiferous tubule and interstitial compartments are damaged differently","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Inhibin selectively inhibits F"}},{"id":"sdl28_q22","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Primary vs. Secondary Hypogonadism & Kallmann Syndrome","batch":2,"stem":"A 15-year-old boy has no signs of puberty. His sense of smell is normal, and his testosterone, LH, and FSH are all low. Serum LH is measured before and at several times after an intravenous bolus of GnRH; it rises to a peak of 9 mIU/mL. Which interpretation is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"An early pubertal response favoring constitutional delay","B":"A failed LH response that establishes Kallmann syndrome","C":"Primary testicular failure with loss of negative feedback","D":"Destruction of pituitary gonadotrophs by a sellar mass","E":"Androgen receptor resistance with raised testosterone"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Katzung describes a single GnRH bolus as a way to separate constitutional delay from hypogonadotropic hypogonadism in a hypogonadotropic adolescent: an LH rise with a peak above 5–8 mIU/mL suggests early pubertal status, whereas an impaired LH response suggests pituitary or hypothalamic disease, although it does not rule out constitutional delay. His peak of 9 mIU/mL is a robust response, not a failed one, and his smell is normal. Primary testicular failure would raise basal LH and FSH through loss of feedback, but his are low. Gonadotroph destruction by a sellar mass would blunt the LH response to GnRH. Androgen receptor resistance would give a high, not low, testosterone.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hypogonadism: primary (low testosterone, high LH and FSH) versus secondary or hypothalamic (all low, as in Kallmann syndrome with anosmia); a GnRH bolus with LH peak above 5–8 mIU/mL suggests early pubertal status. COMLEX: In a boy with delayed puberty and low gonadotropins, test LH responsiveness before labeling the cause.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 2 — pulsatile GnRH drives LH and FSH release","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"His peak of 9 mIU/mL is a robu"}},{"id":"sdl28_q23","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Primary vs. Secondary Hypogonadism & Kallmann Syndrome","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old man with decreased libido, headaches, and bitemporal hemianopia has a large nonfunctioning pituitary macroadenoma. Which hormone panel is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Low testosterone with high LH and FSH","B":"High testosterone with high LH and FSH","C":"Low testosterone with low LH and FSH","D":"High testosterone with low LH and FSH","E":"Normal testosterone with high LH and low FSH"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A large pituitary mass compresses and destroys gonadotrophs, decreasing LH and FSH and therefore Leydig cell testosterone, producing secondary hypogonadotropic hypogonadism. High gonadotropins would imply testicular failure, and high testosterone would suppress LH and FSH but would not cause the libido loss. A gonadotroph mass does not raise LH in isolation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: secondary hypogonadism shows low testosterone with low LH and FSH, occasionally inappropriately normal, and a pituitary source should be imaged. COMLEX: visual field loss points to a pituitary mass effect.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 46 Obj 6 — central suppression of the axis lowers both gonadotropins and testosterone","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"A gonadotroph mass does not ra"}},{"id":"sdl28_q24","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Primary vs. Secondary Hypogonadism & Kallmann Syndrome","batch":2,"stem":"A 16-year-old with a female phenotype and breast development has primary amenorrhea and scant pubic hair. Imaging shows no uterus, testes are found in the abdomen, and the karyotype is 46,XY. Which hormone panel is expected?","choices":{"A":"Low testosterone with low LH and FSH","B":"Low testosterone with high LH and FSH","C":"Elevated estradiol with suppressed LH","D":"Mild testosterone excess with elevated LH","E":"Elevated testosterone with suppressed LH"},"correct":"D","explanation":"This is androgen insensitivity syndrome, in which androgen receptors are absent or defective. Because feedback through androgen receptors is impaired, LH is elevated and testosterone is mildly elevated, and the testes convert excess testosterone to estradiol, producing breast development. The panel differs from primary or secondary hypogonadism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: XY with a female phenotype, testes, no uterus, and high LH with mildly elevated testosterone identifies androgen insensitivity. COMLEX: the testes are removed because of tumor risk, followed by estrogen replacement.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 3 — androgen receptor signaling is required for testosterone action and feedback","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The panel differs from primary","B":"The panel differs from primary"}},{"id":"grid_sdl28_q01","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Primary vs. Secondary Hypogonadism & Kallmann Syndrome","batch":4,"stem":"A 46-year-old man comes to the physician because of 8 months of decreased libido, erectile dysfunction, and headaches. He now shaves less often than he used to. He takes no medications or supplements. Visual field testing shows loss of the temporal half of the visual field in each eye. Both testes are soft. MRI of the head shows a 2.6-cm sellar mass compressing the optic chiasm. Which of the following sets of serum hormone findings is most likely in this patient?","grid":{"headers":["Serum testosterone","LH","FSH"]},"choices":{"A":["↓","↑","↑"],"B":["↓","↓","↓"],"C":["↑","↓","↓"],"D":["↔","↔","↔"],"E":["↔","↔","↑"]},"correct":"B","explanation":"A sellar mass compressing the optic chiasm (bitemporal hemianopia) in a man with new hypogonadal symptoms suppresses LH and FSH, whether by damaging the gonadotrophs, by compressing the pituitary stalk, or, if it is a prolactinoma, by prolactin excess that inhibits GnRH, causing secondary (hypogonadotropic) hypogonadism, so the pattern (↓ serum testosterone, ↓ LH, ↓ FSH) is correct. Without LH the Leydig cells make little testosterone, and without FSH Sertoli-cell support of spermatogenesis fails; the testes are normal but unstimulated. The pattern (↓ serum testosterone, ↑ LH, ↑ FSH) is primary hypogonadism (e.g., Klinefelter syndrome or orchitis), in which loss of testosterone and inhibin feedback raises both gonadotropins. The pattern (↑ serum testosterone, ↓ LH, ↓ FSH) follows exogenous testosterone, which suppresses GnRH, LH, and FSH by negative feedback. The pattern (↔ serum testosterone, ↔ LH, ↔ FSH) would mean the mass has spared the gonadotrophs; decreased libido, erectile dysfunction, and less frequent shaving reflect low testosterone. The pattern (↔ serum testosterone, ↔ LH, ↑ FSH) is isolated Sertoli cell damage, in which loss of inhibin B raises FSH alone while Leydig function and LH stay normal.","boardPrep":"Step 1: low testosterone with high LH and FSH = primary (testicular) hypogonadism; low testosterone with low or inappropriately normal LH and FSH = secondary (pituitary or hypothalamic) hypogonadism. COMLEX: Kallmann syndrome is secondary hypogonadism from failed migration of GnRH neurons, with anosmia.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 4 — exogenous androgens suppress LH and FSH and collapse intratesticular testosterone","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex3t_sdl28_q1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Leydig Cell Steroidogenesis & the Rate-Limiting Step","batch":3,"stem":"A 7-year-old boy has a testicular biopsy during repair of a hydrocele. The interstitium between the seminiferous tubules contains fibroblast-like cells but almost no mature Leydig cells, and his serum testosterone is far below the adult range. Puberty has not begun. Which statement best explains these findings?","choices":{"A":"Leydig cells were lost to an acquired testicular injury","B":"Leydig cells arise after spermatogenesis has begun","C":"Leydig cells are nearly absent until LH rises at puberty","D":"Leydig cell maturation needs FSH acting on Sertoli cells","E":"Leydig cells persist but lose their LH receptors in childhood"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Guyton notes that Leydig cells are numerous in the newborn for the first few months, almost nonexistent through childhood, when the testes secrete almost no testosterone, and reappear after puberty; injection of LH at any age, or the rise in LH at puberty, converts the fibroblast-like interstitial cells into functioning Leydig cells. Nothing suggests injury; this is the normal prepubertal testis. Leydig cells depend on LH rather than on spermatogenesis, and they often keep producing testosterone even when the germinal epithelium is destroyed by x-rays or heat. FSH acts on Sertoli cells to support spermatogenesis, while LH is the Leydig cell signal. Because LH injection converts these cells at any age, they have not lost responsiveness to LH.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Leydig cells: numerous in the newborn, nearly absent in childhood, reappearing at puberty under LH; testosterone secretion rises roughly in proportion to LH; Leydig cells resist radiation and heat better than the germinal epithelium. COMLEX: A very low testosterone in a prepubertal boy is expected and must be read against his pubertal stage.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 1 — Leydig cells fill the interstitial compartment between the seminiferous tubules","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Nothing suggests injury; this ","B":"Leydig cells depend on LH rath"}},{"id":"ex3t_sdl28_q2","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — GnRH Pulsatility vs. Continuous Agonist Therapy","batch":3,"stem":"A 70-year-old man with prostate cancer that has metastasized to his lumbar vertebrae is about to begin depot leuprolide. His oncologist adds a second drug for the first weeks of therapy to prevent a temporary worsening of his bone pain and of his risk of spinal cord compression. Which drug is most appropriate for this purpose?","choices":{"A":"Finasteride","B":"Bicalutamide","C":"Tamsulosin","D":"Anastrozole","E":"Tadalafil"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Continuous GnRH agonist therapy is biphasic: during the first 7–10 days its agonist action raises LH and serum testosterone (the flare), which can worsen bone pain, tumor growth, and neurologic symptoms from vertebral metastases, before receptor down-regulation lowers testosterone into the hypogonadal range within about 2 weeks; Katzung notes that the flare can usually be avoided by giving an androgen receptor antagonist such as flutamide, bicalutamide, or nilutamide. Finasteride blocks conversion of testosterone to DHT by 5-alpha-reductase but does not block the rising testosterone at its receptor. Tamsulosin relaxes alpha-1 smooth muscle at the bladder neck and prostate and does not affect androgen signaling. Anastrozole blocks aromatase, the conversion of androgens to estrogens. Tadalafil is a PDE5 inhibitor for erectile dysfunction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: GnRH agonists (leuprolide, goserelin): pulsatile use stimulates gonadotropins; continuous use causes an initial flare and then down-regulation with suppressed LH and testosterone; cover the flare with an androgen receptor antagonist. COMLEX: In vertebral metastases, the flare can precipitate cord compression, so the antiandrogen is started with the agonist.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 2 — continuous GnRH agonist exposure down-regulates pituitary receptors","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex3t_sdl28_q3","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Dual Negative Feedback: Testosterone vs. Inhibin B","batch":3,"stem":"In a study, healthy young men receive a continuous infusion of recombinant inhibin B for 10 days. End-of-infusion gonadotropins are compared with each man's baseline. Which gonadotropin change is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Equal suppression of FSH and LH together","B":"An isolated rise in FSH above baseline","C":"A parallel rise in FSH and LH together","D":"A selective fall in FSH with LH unchanged","E":"A selective drop in LH with FSH unchanged"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Inhibin B acts directly on the anterior pituitary as a selective inhibitor of FSH and leaves LH unchanged, so supraphysiologic inhibin lowers FSH while LH stays near baseline. Suppression of both gonadotropins, LH more than FSH, follows androgen excess, not inhibin. An isolated rise in FSH is the pattern of inhibin deficiency, the opposite of this infusion. A parallel rise in both gonadotropins follows loss of gonadal feedback, as after castration. A selective drop in LH with FSH preserved fits neither feedback loop. Sources: Exam 3 lecture notes (SDL 28, inhibin B as the selective FSH feedback signal); Costanzo Physiology Ch. 10 (Sertoli cell inhibin inhibits FSH secretion).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Inhibin B selectively restrains FSH, whereas testosterone restrains GnRH and LH, so the two gonadotropins can move independently. COMLEX: Because Sertoli cells make inhibin B in step with sperm production, it serves as a marker of spermatogenic activity.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 -> Obj 2 — Sertoli cell functions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex3t_sdl28_q4","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Dual Negative Feedback: Testosterone vs. Inhibin B","batch":3,"stem":"A 29-year-old man undergoes bilateral orchiectomy after testicular trauma and does not receive hormone replacement. Six weeks later his testosterone is 22 ng/dL (reference range: 300–1000 ng/dL), LH is 24 mIU/mL (reference range: 1.7–8.6 mIU/mL), and FSH is 38 mIU/mL (reference range: 1.5–12.4 mIU/mL). Which change in hypothalamic-pituitary output best explains his follow-up gonadotropin levels?","choices":{"A":"Faster GnRH pulses with higher LH output","B":"Slower GnRH pulses with lower LH output","C":"Steady GnRH pulses with unchanged LH output","D":"Faster GnRH pulses with blunted LH output","E":"Absent GnRH pulses with a compensatory LH rise"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Removing the gonads abolishes testosterone feedback, so the hypothalamus accelerates its GnRH pulses and the pituitary raises LH output, producing the hypergonadotropic state that follows castration. Slower pulses with lower LH would require intact or excess androgen feedback, the opposite of this setting. Steady unchanged output does not fit, because feedback has been lost. Faster pulses with blunted LH is internally inconsistent, because faster GnRH drives LH up. LH does not rise without GnRH, so an absent-pulse pattern does not fit. Sources: Exam 3 lecture notes (SDL 28, loss of testosterone feedback accelerates GnRH and LH); Costanzo Physiology Ch. 10 (castration increases frequency and amplitude of GnRH, FSH, and LH pulses).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Loss of gonadal feedback raises GnRH pulse frequency and amplitude and drives gonadotropins up. COMLEX: The same disinhibition explains the high FSH and LH that follow menopause.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 -> Obj 4 — gonadotropin levels in anabolic steroid abuse","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex3t_sdl28_q5","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Primary vs. Secondary Hypogonadism & Kallmann Syndrome","batch":3,"stem":"A 19-year-old man is evaluated for tall stature with long legs, gynecomastia, and sparse facial hair. His gonads are small and firm and his sense of smell is intact. Testosterone is 180 ng/dL (reference range: 300–1000 ng/dL), LH is 19 mIU/mL (reference range: 1.7–8.6 mIU/mL), FSH is 32 mIU/mL (reference range: 1.5–12.4 mIU/mL), and inhibin B is undetectable. Which process best accounts for this pattern?","choices":{"A":"Central failure of GnRH-secreting neurons","B":"Androgen receptor resistance in target tissues","C":"Autonomous androgen secretion by a gonadal tumor","D":"Exogenous androgen suppression of the pituitary","E":"Primary gonadal failure from tubular dysgenesis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A low testosterone with elevated LH and FSH defines primary, hypergonadotropic hypogonadism, and small firm gonads, gynecomastia, eunuchoid proportions, undetectable inhibin B, and FSH above LH fit dysgenesis of the seminiferous tubules with loss of both feedback signals. Central failure of GnRH neurons would lower LH and FSH and often impair smell. Androgen receptor resistance raises testosterone and LH rather than lowering testosterone. An autonomous androgen-secreting tumor raises testosterone and suppresses gonadotropins. Exogenous androgen suppresses LH and FSH, the opposite of this panel. Sources: Exam 3 lecture notes (SDL 28, primary hypergonadotropic hypogonadism and the 47,XXY panel); Robbins Ch. 21 (Klinefelter syndrome).","boardPrep":"Step 1: The most common cause of primary hypogonadism shows low testosterone and inhibin B with high LH and FSH, typically FSH greater than LH. COMLEX: Tall stature with long legs reflects delayed epiphyseal closure from deficient testosterone and the estrogen made from it.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 -> Obj 2 — 5-alpha-reductase deficiency, a 46,XY difference of sex development","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex3t_sdl28_q6","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Primary vs. Secondary Hypogonadism & Kallmann Syndrome","batch":3,"stem":"A 20-year-old man has never entered puberty and has never been able to smell. Testosterone, LH, and FSH are all low, and MRI shows a normal pituitary with hypoplastic olfactory bulbs. He wants to father children. Which treatment is most likely to establish sperm production?","choices":{"A":"A long-acting continuous GnRH agonist","B":"Intramuscular testosterone replacement","C":"Intermittent pulses of GnRH by pump","D":"An oral 5-alpha-reductase inhibitor","E":"A selective estrogen receptor antagonist"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Low testosterone with low LH and FSH plus anosmia and hypoplastic olfactory bulbs indicate congenital GnRH deficiency with an intact pituitary and gonads, so GnRH delivered in intermittent pulses reproduces the normal pattern, drives LH and FSH, and establishes sperm production. A long-acting continuous GnRH analog does not initiate puberty. Intramuscular testosterone virilizes but suppresses gonadotropins by negative feedback, so it does not drive sperm production. A 5-alpha-reductase inhibitor only blocks DHT formation. An estrogen receptor antagonist does not supply the missing GnRH drive in a congenital deficiency. Sources: Exam 3 lecture notes (SDL 28, Kallmann syndrome and GnRH neuron migration failure); Costanzo Physiology Ch. 10 (pulsatile GnRH initiates puberty, continuous GnRH does not).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hypogonadotropic hypogonadism with anosmia localizes to the hypothalamus, and restoring GnRH pulses can restore fertility, which testosterone replacement does not. COMLEX: Testosterone replacement treats the androgen deficiency but suppresses the axis further, so it does not restore sperm production.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 -> Obj 2 — this is the pulsatile-versus-continuous GnRH principle applied to restore the axis","isHighYield":true}],"29":[{"id":"sdl29_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Testosterone-Mediated vs. DHT-Mediated Androgen Actions","batch":1,"stem":"Which action of androgens is mediated directly by testosterone without conversion to dihydrotestosterone?","choices":{"A":"Differentiation of the prostate","B":"Male pattern baldness","C":"Differentiation of the scrotum","D":"Sebaceous gland activity","E":"Deepening of the voice"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Deepening of the voice, increased muscle mass, the pubertal growth spurt, spermatogenesis, and libido respond directly to testosterone. Differentiation of the prostate and scrotum, male hair patterns, male pattern baldness, and sebaceous gland activity require conversion of testosterone to dihydrotestosterone by 5-alpha-reductase.","boardPrep":"Step 1: sort androgen actions into two lists, testosterone-mediated and DHT-mediated, since 5-alpha-reductase disorders and finasteride are tested on this split. COMLEX: muscle, voice, and libido are testosterone effects.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 46 -> Obj 3 — finasteride and dutasteride act by blocking the conversion that defines DHT-dependent actions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Testosterone-Mediated vs. DHT-Mediated Androgen Actions","batch":1,"stem":"Which androgen-dependent process requires conversion of testosterone to dihydrotestosterone?","choices":{"A":"Increased muscle mass","B":"Spermatogenesis","C":"Growth of the prostate","D":"Deepening of the voice","E":"Closure of the epiphyseal plates"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Growth of the prostate depends on dihydrotestosterone rather than testosterone, which is why 5-alpha-reductase inhibitors treat benign prostatic hyperplasia. Muscle mass, spermatogenesis, and voice respond directly to testosterone, and epiphyseal closure is driven by estrogen aromatized from testosterone, so none of these requires DHT.","boardPrep":"Step 1: DHT drives the prostate, external genitalia, hair pattern, baldness, and sebaceous glands. COMLEX: finasteride treats BPH and male pattern hair loss for this reason.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 46 -> Obj 3 — finasteride and dutasteride act by blocking the conversion that defines DHT-dependent actions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Testosterone-Mediated vs. DHT-Mediated Androgen Actions","batch":1,"stem":"Which hormone drives differentiation of the fetal Wolffian ducts into the epididymis, vas deferens, and seminal vesicles?","choices":{"A":"DHT formed by peripheral 5-alpha-reductase in the developing ducts","B":"Antimullerian hormone secreted by Sertoli cells of the fetal testis","C":"Estradiol formed by aromatase within fetal Leydig cells of the testis","D":"Testosterone from the same-side testis acting without DHT conversion","E":"LH from the anterior pituitary stimulating the developing duct cells"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Testosterone from each testis acts on its own Wolffian duct on the same side, and it does not need to be converted to dihydrotestosterone for this action. Antimullerian hormone instead causes atrophy of the Mullerian ducts, and LH acts on Leydig cells rather than on the ducts.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the internal male tract needs testosterone, and the external genitalia need DHT. COMLEX: this explains why 5-alpha-reductase deficiency spares internal structures.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 46 -> Obj 3 — finasteride and dutasteride act by blocking the conversion that defines DHT-dependent actions","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"Antimullerian hormone instead "}},{"id":"sdl29_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — 5-Alpha-Reductase Deficiency vs. Finasteride Therapy","batch":1,"stem":"Which description best fits the newborn presentation of classic 5-alpha-reductase deficiency?","choices":{"A":"46,XX with virilized external genitalia and normal ovaries","B":"46,XY with ambiguous genitalia and normal Wolffian derivatives","C":"46,XY with female external genitalia and no Wolffian or Mullerian derivatives","D":"47,XXY with small testes and normal male external genitalia","E":"45,X with streak gonads and female external genitalia"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Affected 46,XY individuals have testes and make testosterone, so the Wolffian ducts differentiate, and antimullerian hormone suppresses the Mullerian ducts. Without DHT, however, the external genitalia are ambiguous or appear female. Androgen insensitivity would leave no Wolffian derivatives, and the other options describe different karyotypes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: 5-alpha-reductase deficiency gives ambiguous genitalia with a blind vaginal pouch, hypospadias, and male internal ducts. COMLEX: virilization at puberty occurs because high-normal testosterone can activate receptors without DHT.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 41 -> Obj 1 — DHT-dependent prostatic growth underlies benign prostatic hyperplasia","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Androgen insensitivity would l","D":"Androgen insensitivity would l","E":"Androgen insensitivity would l"}},{"id":"sdl29_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — 5-Alpha-Reductase Deficiency vs. Finasteride Therapy","batch":1,"stem":"Which hormone profile is expected in classic 5-alpha-reductase deficiency?","choices":{"A":"Normal testosterone with low DHT","B":"Low testosterone with normal DHT","C":"Low testosterone with low DHT","D":"Normal testosterone with high DHT","E":"High testosterone with high DHT"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The testes secrete testosterone normally, but tissues lacking 5-alpha-reductase cannot convert it, so testosterone is normal or high-normal and dihydrotestosterone is low. Low testosterone would reflect a defect in synthesis, and high DHT would not occur without the converting enzyme.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the ratio of testosterone to DHT is elevated in 5-alpha-reductase deficiency. COMLEX: contrast with androgen insensitivity, in which both androgens are normal or high.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 41 -> Obj 1 — DHT-dependent prostatic growth underlies benign prostatic hyperplasia","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Low testosterone would reflect","C":"Low testosterone would reflect","D":"Low testosterone would reflect"}},{"id":"sdl29_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — 5-Alpha-Reductase Deficiency vs. Finasteride Therapy","batch":1,"stem":"Which enzyme is inhibited by finasteride?","choices":{"A":"Aromatase enzyme","B":"Cholesterol desmolase","C":"17-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase","D":"21-beta-hydroxylase","E":"5-alpha-reductase"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Finasteride blocks 5-alpha-reductase, which converts testosterone to dihydrotestosterone, and is therefore used in benign prostatic hyperplasia and male pattern hair loss. Aromatase, cholesterol desmolase, 17-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase, and 21-beta-hydroxylase are not its targets.","boardPrep":"Step 1: finasteride blocks DHT formation, and its adverse effects derive from lowering DHT. COMLEX: treatment of BPH and alopecia are the two classic indications.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 41 -> Obj 1 — DHT-dependent prostatic growth underlies benign prostatic hyperplasia","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Intracellular Androgen Receptor Signaling Pathway","batch":1,"stem":"Where does the unliganded androgen receptor reside in a target cell?","choices":{"A":"In the cytoplasm bound to heat-shock proteins","B":"In the nucleus already bound to DNA androgen response elements","C":"On the plasma membrane as a receptor tyrosine kinase","D":"In the mitochondria bound to cytochrome oxidase","E":"In the extracellular fluid bound to SHBG"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Testosterone and dihydrotestosterone are lipid-soluble and diffuse into cells, where the unliganded androgen receptor is held in the cytoplasm by heat-shock proteins. Ligand binding then releases the chaperones and allows the receptor to move to the nucleus. It is an intracellular receptor, not a membrane tyrosine kinase. Mitochondria are not where the androgen receptor waits, and SHBG is a plasma protein that carries testosterone, not a receptor.","boardPrep":"Step 1: steroid receptors are intracellular, with the ligand crossing the membrane. COMLEX: distinguish these from peptide hormone receptors at the cell surface.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 -> Obj 3 — androgen receptor signaling also mediates testosterone negative feedback on the axis","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Ligand binding then releases t"}},{"id":"sdl29_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Intracellular Androgen Receptor Signaling Pathway","batch":1,"stem":"What does the ligand-bound receptor do inside the nucleus?","choices":{"A":"Binds cAMP response elements and activates protein kinase A","B":"Forms homodimers that bind androgen response elements","C":"Phosphorylates STAT proteins that return to the cell membrane","D":"Cleaves genomic DNA at splice sites to silence gene expression","E":"Binds cytoplasmic mRNA to block translation on ribosomes"},"correct":"B","explanation":"After ligand binding, the receptor dissociates from heat-shock proteins, translocates to the nucleus, forms homodimers, and binds androgen response elements in DNA, initiating transcription of new mRNAs that are translated into proteins responsible for androgen actions. The other options describe unrelated signaling mechanisms. The receptor turns on transcription of new mRNAs; it does not cut DNA to silence genes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: steroid hormone action is slow because it works through gene transcription. COMLEX: nuclear receptors act as ligand-activated transcription factors.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 -> Obj 3 — androgen receptor signaling also mediates testosterone negative feedback on the axis","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"D":"The receptor turns on transcri"}},{"id":"sdl29_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Intracellular Androgen Receptor Signaling Pathway","batch":1,"stem":"What triggers release of heat-shock proteins from the androgen receptor?","choices":{"A":"Synthesis of DHT inside the nucleus of the cell","B":"Phosphorylation of the receptor by protein kinase A","C":"Release of mitochondrial calcium into the cytoplasm","D":"Binding of testosterone or DHT to the receptor","E":"Cleavage of the receptor by lysosomal proteases"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Ligand binding, by testosterone or dihydrotestosterone, changes receptor conformation and releases the heat-shock proteins so the receptor can translocate to the nucleus. The other options are not steps in classical androgen receptor activation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ligand binding is the trigger for chaperone release in the steroid receptor pathway. COMLEX: androgen insensitivity results from receptor defects that disrupt this pathway.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 -> Obj 3 — androgen receptor signaling also mediates testosterone negative feedback on the axis","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl29_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Anabolic Steroid Abuse: HPG Suppression & Azoospermia","batch":1,"stem":"How do exogenous anabolic steroids suppress the hypothalamic-pituitary-gonadal axis?","choices":{"A":"Negative feedback that inhibits GnRH and LH secretion","B":"Direct destruction of Leydig cells by androgen receptor activation","C":"Stimulation of inhibin secretion that blocks LH release","D":"Up-regulation of GnRH receptors that exhausts pituitary LH stores","E":"Conversion of the steroids into gonadotropins by the liver"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Androgens feed back on the hypothalamus and anterior pituitary to inhibit GnRH and LH secretion, and the same feedback applies to synthetic androgens. With LH suppressed, Leydig cell stimulation and endogenous testosterone production fall. Inhibin acts selectively on FSH, not LH. Androgens do not destroy Leydig cells directly, and the liver does not convert steroids into gonadotropins.","boardPrep":"Step 1: supraphysiologic or exogenous androgen exposure suppresses the axis through negative feedback. COMLEX: this is why anabolic steroid users show low LH and FSH.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 46 -> Obj 6 — other drugs that suppress the HPG axis follow the same principle","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl29_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Anabolic Steroid Abuse: HPG Suppression & Azoospermia","batch":1,"stem":"What happens to intratesticular testosterone in a man using high-dose anabolic steroids?","choices":{"A":"It rises because anabolic steroids concentrate within the testis","B":"It falls markedly because LH stimulation of Leydig cells declines","C":"It stays unchanged because Sertoli cells compensate for lost LH","D":"It rises because FSH secretion increases in response to feedback","E":"It stays unchanged because androgen-binding protein buffers the change"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Suppressed LH removes the stimulus for Leydig cell testosterone synthesis, so intratesticular testosterone falls markedly even though serum androgen activity from the drug is high. Because spermatogenesis depends on high local testosterone reinforcing FSH action, sperm production is impaired.","boardPrep":"Step 1: serum androgens do not substitute for intratesticular testosterone. COMLEX: this is why testosterone therapy can cause infertility.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 46 -> Obj 6 — other drugs that suppress the HPG axis follow the same principle","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl29_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Anabolic Steroid Abuse: HPG Suppression & Azoospermia","batch":1,"stem":"Which testicular findings result from prolonged anabolic steroid abuse?","choices":{"A":"Testicular hypertrophy with increased sperm concentration","B":"Normal testicular size with increased sperm motility","C":"Testicular atrophy with azoospermia or severe oligospermia","D":"Testicular atrophy with high sperm concentration","E":"Testicular hypertrophy with normal intratesticular testosterone"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Prolonged suppression of LH and FSH removes trophic support to Leydig cells and seminiferous tubules, so the testes shrink and spermatogenesis is markedly reduced, producing azoospermia or severe oligospermia. The other options describe changes that do not follow from gonadotropin suppression. Suppressed gonadotropins shrink the testes rather than enlarging them or leaving them normal, and they lower intratesticular testosterone and sperm count, so normal intratesticular testosterone, increased motility, and a high sperm concentration do not fit.","boardPrep":"Step 1: chronic exogenous androgen use produces atrophy and infertility despite muscular development. COMLEX: fertility may recover after stopping the drug but can take months.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 46 -> Obj 6 — other drugs that suppress the HPG axis follow the same principle","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Suppressed gonadotropins shrin"}},{"id":"sdl29_q13","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Testosterone-Mediated vs. DHT-Mediated Androgen Actions","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man takes an agent that blocks the conversion of testosterone to dihydrotestosterone. Which outcome is expected?","choices":{"A":"Smaller prostate and slowed hair loss with preserved muscle mass","B":"Loss of muscle mass and voice deepening with normal prostate size","C":"Regression of the epididymis and vas deferens with shrinking seminal vesicles","D":"A rise in vocal pitch from loss of laryngeal growth","E":"Loss of spermatogenesis with testicular atrophy"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Prostate growth, hair pattern, and male pattern baldness depend on dihydrotestosterone, so blocking 5-alpha-reductase shrinks the prostate and slows hair loss. Muscle mass, voice, spermatogenesis, and the internal genital tract respond directly to testosterone, which remains available.","boardPrep":"Step 1: finasteride mainly affects DHT-dependent tissues. COMLEX: expect prostate and hair benefit with limited effect on muscle and fertility.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 41 Obj 1 — DHT-dependent stromal and epithelial growth underlies benign prostatic hyperplasia","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Muscle mass, voice, spermatoge","D":"Muscle mass, voice, spermatoge","E":"Muscle mass, voice, spermatoge"}},{"id":"sdl29_q14","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Testosterone-Mediated vs. DHT-Mediated Androgen Actions","batch":2,"stem":"A 14-year-old boy in puberty begins spermatogenesis, develops acne from increased sebaceous gland activity, and has enlargement of his prostate. Which statement correctly assigns the androgen responsible for each?","choices":{"A":"Spermatogenesis and sebaceous glands via testosterone; prostate via DHT","B":"Prostate via testosterone; spermatogenesis and sebaceous glands via DHT","C":"Each of the three via DHT after 5-alpha-reduction in the target tissue","D":"Each of the three via testosterone acting without DHT involvement","E":"Spermatogenesis via testosterone; sebaceous glands and prostate via DHT"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Spermatogenesis is driven by testosterone itself, maintained at high intratubular levels, whereas sebaceous gland activity and prostate growth depend on dihydrotestosterone formed by 5-alpha-reductase in those tissues. No single androgen accounts for all three.","boardPrep":"Step 1: split androgen actions by whether 5-alpha-reductase is required. COMLEX: 5-alpha-reductase inhibitors shrink the prostate because prostate growth is DHT-dependent.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 46 Obj 3 — 5-alpha-reductase inhibition affects DHT-dependent actions","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"No single androgen accounts fo","D":"No single androgen accounts fo"}},{"id":"sdl29_q15","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Testosterone-Mediated vs. DHT-Mediated Androgen Actions","batch":2,"stem":"A 46,XY newborn has ambiguous external genitalia; his mother took a 5-alpha-reductase inhibitor early in pregnancy. Ultrasonography shows a normal epididymis, vas deferens, and seminal vesicles. Which best explains the normal internal male ducts?","choices":{"A":"Wolffian duct differentiation is driven by DHT formed within the duct","B":"Antimullerian hormone directly induces Wolffian duct differentiation","C":"Maternal estrogen crosses the placenta and drives Wolffian duct growth","D":"Wolffian duct differentiation is driven by testosterone itself without DHT","E":"Wolffian ducts differentiate by default in the absence of any androgen"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Fetal Leydig cell testosterone diffuses locally and directly stimulates the Wolffian ducts to form the epididymis, vas deferens, and seminal vesicles, so they develop normally when 5-alpha-reductase is blocked. The external genitalia and prostate require DHT and are undervirilized. Antimullerian hormone causes regression of the mullerian ducts rather than Wolffian growth, and without androgen the Wolffian ducts regress.","boardPrep":"Step 1: internal male ducts are testosterone-dependent, while external genitalia and prostate are DHT-dependent. COMLEX: 5-alpha-reductase inhibitors are contraindicated in pregnancy because they undervirilize a male fetus.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 46 Obj 3 — 5-alpha-reductase inhibitors block DHT, which is why they are avoided in pregnancy with a male fetus","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"Antimullerian hormone causes r"}},{"id":"sdl29_q16","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — 5-Alpha-Reductase Deficiency vs. Finasteride Therapy","batch":2,"stem":"A 13-year-old raised as a girl has a deepening voice, increasing muscle mass, and an enlarging clitoris, with no breasts or menses. Examination shows testes, hypospadias, and a blind vaginal pouch. Karyotype is 46,XY, testosterone is high-normal, and DHT is low. Which defect is most likely?","choices":{"A":"5-alpha-reductase","B":"17-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase","C":"Androgen receptor protein","D":"21-beta-hydroxylase","E":"Aromatase enzyme"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A 46,XY individual with testes, high-normal testosterone, and low DHT has 5-alpha-reductase deficiency. Internal male ducts formed from testosterone, but external genitalia lacked DHT, and at puberty testosterone-mediated changes such as voice deepening and muscle growth appear. Androgen receptor defects would give high androgens with little or no virilization, and the other enzymes are not consistent with this profile. 17β-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase is the testicular step that makes testosterone, so its loss would lower testosterone rather than leave it high-normal, and 21β-hydroxylase is an adrenal enzyme the testes do not use.","boardPrep":"Step 1: 46,XY with virilization at puberty and low DHT is the classic 5-alpha-reductase deficiency picture. COMLEX: patients may be raised as girls and virilize at puberty.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 41 Obj 1 — DHT dependence of prostatic development","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q17","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — 5-Alpha-Reductase Deficiency vs. Finasteride Therapy","batch":2,"stem":"A 15-year-old 46,XY patient raised as a girl has 5-alpha-reductase deficiency and lacks DHT, yet at puberty her voice deepens and muscle mass increases. Which explanation is best?","choices":{"A":"An alternative pathway supplies DHT that restores the missing actions","B":"Estradiol from ovarian tissue drives the voice and muscle changes","C":"Those actions are mediated by testosterone itself and do not require DHT","D":"Excess LH directly stimulates laryngeal and skeletal muscle growth","E":"The androgen receptor becomes constitutively active without DHT"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Voice deepening and muscle growth respond to testosterone directly, so they proceed despite the enzyme deficiency, while the DHT-dependent actions fail to develop. She has no ovaries, and LH does not act on these tissues. Rising pubertal testosterone binds the androgen receptor directly; no alternative DHT source is required.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the split between testosterone-mediated and DHT-mediated actions explains the mixed virilization. COMLEX: absent body hair with deep voice suggests DHT deficiency.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 1 — LH-driven Leydig steroidogenesis is intact in 5-alpha-reductase deficiency, so pubertal testosterone rises normally","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Rising pubertal testosterone b","B":"She has no ovaries, and LH doe"}},{"id":"sdl29_q18","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — 5-Alpha-Reductase Deficiency vs. Finasteride Therapy","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old man with benign prostatic hyperplasia starts finasteride. Which changes are expected over several months?","choices":{"A":"Reduced prostate volume with an unchanged serum PSA level","B":"Reduced prostate volume with a fall in serum PSA","C":"Prostatic hypertrophy with a lower serum PSA level","D":"Reduced prostate volume with a fall in serum testosterone","E":"Unchanged prostate volume with reduced muscle mass"},"correct":"B","explanation":"By blocking conversion of testosterone to dihydrotestosterone, finasteride reduces DHT-driven prostatic growth, so prostate volume decreases and PSA, which reflects prostatic tissue, falls. Serum testosterone is not lowered, and muscle mass, which depends on testosterone, is preserved.","boardPrep":"Step 1: 5-alpha-reductase inhibitors shrink the prostate and lower PSA, so PSA interpretation must account for therapy. COMLEX: sexual adverse effects stem from lowered DHT.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 46 Obj 3 — finasteride effects on DHT, PSA, and sexual adverse effects","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Serum testosterone is not lowe"}},{"id":"sdl29_q19","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Intracellular Androgen Receptor Signaling Pathway","batch":2,"stem":"A 16-year-old with a female phenotype, breast development, primary amenorrhea, and no uterus has testes and a 46,XY karyotype. Studies of genital skin fibroblasts show no binding of testosterone or DHT. Which defect explains this?","choices":{"A":"Absent or defective androgen receptors that prevent hormone action","B":"Absent 5-alpha-reductase that prevents formation of DHT","C":"Absent 17-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase that prevents testosterone synthesis","D":"Absent antimullerian hormone that leaves Mullerian ducts intact","E":"Absent SRY gene product that prevents testis formation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Absent androgen binding in target cells indicates a defect in the androgen receptor, so testosterone and DHT cannot act and the external genitalia develop as female by default, while Wolffian ducts regress. The testes still make antimullerian hormone, so there is no uterus, and the presence of testes excludes an SRY defect.","boardPrep":"Step 1: androgen insensitivity is a receptor disorder with normal or high androgens and elevated LH. COMLEX: the testes are removed because of tumor risk, and estrogen therapy follows.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 3 — androgen receptor signaling mediates feedback inhibition of LH","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"E":"The testes still make antimull"}},{"id":"sdl29_q20","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Intracellular Androgen Receptor Signaling Pathway","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old man with metastatic prostate cancer receives an investigational drug that prevents the hormone-bound receptor from entering the nucleus of target cells. Which effect is expected?","choices":{"A":"Loss of androgen-responsive transcription with unchanged androgen levels","B":"Lower serum testosterone from inhibited Leydig cell steroid synthesis","C":"Blocked conversion of testosterone to DHT by tissue 5-alpha-reductase","D":"Blocked release of LH from the anterior pituitary gonadotroph cells","E":"Lower SHBG-bound testosterone in the circulation after drug exposure"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Nuclear entry of the hormone-bound receptor is required for it to bind androgen response elements, so blocking translocation prevents transcription of androgen-responsive genes even when testosterone and DHT are abundant. The drug acts downstream of hormone synthesis, conversion, and release.","boardPrep":"Step 1: peripheral androgen receptor antagonists block target cell action without lowering testosterone. COMLEX: contrast this with GnRH agonists, which lower testosterone centrally.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 2 — continuous GnRH agonists treat prostate cancer by exploiting its dependence on androgen receptor signaling","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"The drug acts downstream of ho","C":"The drug acts downstream of ho","D":"The drug acts downstream of ho"}},{"id":"sdl29_q21","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Intracellular Androgen Receptor Signaling Pathway","batch":2,"stem":"A 17-year-old phenotypic female with primary amenorrhea has a 46,XY karyotype, and sequencing finds a mutation in the DNA-binding domain of her androgen receptor. Which step of the signaling pathway fails?","choices":{"A":"Diffusion of testosterone across the plasma membrane","B":"Release of heat-shock proteins after ligand binding","C":"Receptor pair attachment to DNA response elements","D":"Formation of DHT from testosterone by 5-alpha-reductase","E":"Movement of the ligand-bound receptor into the nucleus"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The DNA-binding domain mediates attachment of the receptor dimer to androgen response elements, so this step fails while ligand entry, chaperone release, DHT formation, and nuclear translocation can proceed. The result is androgen insensitivity despite normal hormone levels.","boardPrep":"Step 1: different receptor mutations disrupt different steps but produce the same androgen insensitivity phenotype. COMLEX: normal or high testosterone with no androgen effect suggests a receptor problem.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 3 — testosterone feedback on GnRH and LH also needs the androgen receptor, so receptor defects raise both LH and testosterone","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl29_q22","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Anabolic Steroid Abuse: HPG Suppression & Azoospermia","batch":2,"stem":"A 27-year-old bodybuilder using testosterone and other anabolic steroids has small soft testes and azoospermia. Which hormone profile is most likely?","choices":{"A":"High LH and FSH with low intratesticular testosterone","B":"Low LH and FSH with high intratesticular testosterone","C":"High LH with low FSH and high intratesticular testosterone","D":"Low LH and FSH with low intratesticular testosterone","E":"Normal LH and FSH with normal intratesticular testosterone"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Exogenous androgens inhibit GnRH and LH through negative feedback, so LH and FSH fall, Leydig cell stimulation is lost, and intratesticular testosterone collapses, causing azoospermia and testicular atrophy. High gonadotropins would indicate primary testicular failure, which is not the mechanism here.","boardPrep":"Step 1: suppressed gonadotropins with atrophic testes suggest exogenous androgen use. COMLEX: serum testosterone may be normal or high in these patients despite the atrophy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 3 — testosterone negative feedback inhibits GnRH and LH","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"High gonadotropins would indic","C":"High gonadotropins would indic"}},{"id":"sdl29_q23","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Anabolic Steroid Abuse: HPG Suppression & Azoospermia","batch":2,"stem":"A 27-year-old bodybuilder who used anabolic steroids for years stops them and wants to restore fertility. Which treatment stimulates his testicular testosterone production directly?","choices":{"A":"Leuprolide given continuously to stimulate pituitary LH release","B":"Finasteride to raise intratesticular testosterone through DHT","C":"Testosterone gel to restore intratesticular androgen concentration","D":"Recombinant inhibin to raise FSH secretion from the pituitary","E":"Human chorionic gonadotropin acting on Leydig cell LH receptors"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Human chorionic gonadotropin belongs to the glycoprotein hormone family; its beta subunit, which confers receptor specificity, closely resembles that of LH, so it activates Leydig cell LH receptors and stimulates testosterone synthesis. Continuous GnRH agonists suppress LH, exogenous testosterone maintains feedback suppression, finasteride blocks DHT formation, and inhibin lowers FSH.","boardPrep":"Step 1: hCG is LH-like and stimulates Leydig cells directly. COMLEX: exogenous testosterone would further suppress the axis rather than restore fertility.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 1 — LH-driven Leydig cell testosterone synthesis","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Continuous GnRH agonists suppr","C":"Continuous GnRH agonists suppr"}},{"id":"sdl29_q24","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Anabolic Steroid Abuse: HPG Suppression & Azoospermia","batch":2,"stem":"A 27-year-old bodybuilder who has injected high-dose testosterone for 9 months has tender enlargement of both breasts, acne, and small testes. His hematocrit is 54% (reference range: 41–53%). Which mechanism best explains the breast enlargement?","choices":{"A":"Direct androgen receptor stimulation of breast ducts","B":"Aromatase conversion of excess androgen to estrogen","C":"Rising LH that drives testicular estradiol secretion","D":"Prolactin release from a pituitary lactotroph tumor","E":"Loss of DHT from inhibited 5-alpha-reductase activity"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In males, androgens are converted to estrogens by aromatase, primarily in adipose tissue and the testes, and First Aid lists gynecomastia among the findings that should raise suspicion of anabolic-androgenic steroid use, along with acne, aggression, erythrocytosis (as in his hematocrit), and small testes from suppression of the hypothalamic-pituitary-gonadal axis. Breast enlargement is an estrogen effect, not a direct androgen receptor effect. Exogenous testosterone suppresses LH through negative feedback, so LH is low rather than rising. Nothing points to a pituitary tumor; his findings follow from the injected androgen. He is not taking a 5-alpha-reductase inhibitor, and lower DHT would not enlarge the breasts.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Anabolic-androgenic steroid use: acne, aggression, gynecomastia (aromatization), erythrocytosis with thromboembolic risk, small testes and azoospermia from HPG suppression, and hepatic adenoma risk. COMLEX: Ask about androgen use in a muscular young man with gynecomastia, acne, or a high hematocrit.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 3 — exogenous testosterone feedback suppresses LH and FSH","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"C":"Exogenous testosterone suppres"}},{"id":"ex3t_sdl29_q1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Testosterone-Mediated vs. DHT-Mediated Androgen Actions","batch":3,"stem":"A woman at 8 weeks' gestation reports handling and splitting her husband's tablets, a drug he takes for male pattern hair loss that does not lower his serum testosterone. If her fetus is male and significant exposure occurs, which developing structure is at greatest risk of abnormal formation?","choices":{"A":"Mesonephric epididymal duct","B":"Urethral groove of the penile shaft","C":"Ejaculatory duct of the seminal tract","D":"Mesonephric ductal vas deferens","E":"Seminiferous cords of the gonad"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A drug for male pattern hair loss that does not lower testosterone is a 5-alpha-reductase inhibitor, which blocks conversion of testosterone to DHT; because fetal formation of the penis, penile urethra, and scrotum depends on DHT, the developing penile urethra is at risk and incomplete closure produces hypospadias. The epididymis, ejaculatory duct, and vas deferens are Wolffian derivatives that this drug class spares. The seminiferous cords form as part of the SRY-driven gonadal program, not under DHT. This DHT dependence is why the drug class is contraindicated in pregnant women. Sources: Exam 3 lecture notes (SDL 29, DHT-dependent external genital formation and pregnancy contraindication); Costanzo Physiology Ch. 10 (dihydrotestosterone and differentiation of the external male genitalia).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Loss of DHT during fetal life produces hypospadias, a urethral opening low on the underside of the penis, because the penile urethra forms under DHT. COMLEX: Counsel that 5-alpha-reductase inhibitors are contraindicated in pregnant women because they impair male fetal external genital development.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 -> Obj 2 — congenital 5-alpha-reductase deficiency undervirilizes the same DHT-dependent external genitalia","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex3t_sdl29_q2","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Testosterone-Mediated vs. DHT-Mediated Androgen Actions","batch":3,"stem":"In an organ-culture experiment, genital tract tissues from a 46,XY fetus are incubated with testosterone plus a drug that completely blocks conversion of testosterone to DHT. Which tissue is most likely to still develop into a male structure?","choices":{"A":"Mesonephric Wolffian duct","B":"Indifferent urogenital sinus","C":"Paired urethral folds","D":"Labioscrotal genital swellings","E":"Paramesonephric Mullerian duct"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Testosterone itself drives differentiation of the Wolffian ducts into the epididymis, vas deferens, and seminal vesicles, and this action does not require conversion to DHT, so the Wolffian duct differentiates despite the blocked enzyme. The urogenital sinus forms the prostate, the urethral folds form the penile urethra, and the genital swellings form the scrotum, and each of these virilizes only under DHT. The Mullerian duct has no male differentiation program and regresses under Sertoli cell antimullerian hormone rather than responding to testosterone. Sources: Exam 3 lecture notes (SDL 29, testosterone-driven Wolffian differentiation versus DHT-driven external virilization); Costanzo Physiology Ch. 10 (testosterone acts on the Wolffian ducts without conversion to dihydrotestosterone).","boardPrep":"Step 1: The urogenital sinus, urethral folds, and genital swellings virilize only under DHT. COMLEX: The Mullerian ducts regress because Sertoli cells secrete antimullerian hormone, not because of any androgen.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 -> Obj 2 — congenital 5-alpha-reductase deficiency","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex3t_sdl29_q3","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — 5-Alpha-Reductase Deficiency vs. Finasteride Therapy","batch":3,"stem":"A 2-week-old 46,XY infant has a small phallus with a urethral opening at its base, a normal epididymis and vas deferens on each side, and no uterus. After 3 days of hCG, testosterone rises to 240 ng/dL (reference range for this age: 60–400 ng/dL) while DHT stays at 5 ng/dL (adult male reference range: 30–85 ng/dL). Which defect best explains these findings?","choices":{"A":"Absent androgen receptors in target cells","B":"Failed testicular testosterone synthesis","C":"Deficient hypothalamic secretion of GnRH","D":"Absent adrenal 21-hydroxylase activity","E":"Deficient conversion of testosterone to DHT"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The gonads respond to hCG with a normal testosterone rise, but DHT stays low, so the lesion is loss of 5-alpha-reductase; with DHT absent the external genitalia fail to virilize, while the internal ducts form normally and antimullerian hormone removes the uterus. Absent androgen receptors would leave conversion intact, so DHT would rise with testosterone. Failed testicular testosterone synthesis would blunt the testosterone rise to hCG. Deficient GnRH would lower testosterone, which hCG here bypasses. Absent adrenal 21-hydroxylase masculinizes 46,XX fetuses through excess adrenal androgens and would not produce a selective DHT deficit. Sources: Exam 3 lecture notes (SDL 29, 5-alpha-reductase deficiency with normal testosterone and low DHT); Costanzo Physiology Ch. 10 (5-alpha-reductase deficiency case with high-normal testosterone and low dihydrotestosterone).","boardPrep":"Step 1: A 46,XY infant with a normal internal male tract, no uterus, normal testosterone, and low DHT has a 5-alpha-reductase defect, and an elevated testosterone-to-DHT ratio after hCG is the biochemical signature. COMLEX: Distinguish it from a receptor defect by the hCG response, since the ratio is high when conversion is blocked but normal when the receptor is defective.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 -> Obj 1 — Leydig cell testosterone synthesis driven by LH or hCG","isHighYield":true},{"id":"ex3t_sdl29_q4","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — 5-Alpha-Reductase Deficiency vs. Finasteride Therapy","batch":3,"stem":"A 66-year-old man with obstructive urinary symptoms begins a once-daily oral agent. After 6 months his prostate volume has decreased, serum DHT has fallen by more than 90%, testosterone and LH are unchanged, and serum PSA is about half its baseline. Which agent was most likely prescribed?","choices":{"A":"A dual-isoform 5-alpha-reductase inhibitor","B":"A selective type 2 5-alpha-reductase inhibitor","C":"A continuous long-acting GnRH receptor agonist","D":"A competitive androgen receptor blocking agent","E":"An alpha-1 adrenergic receptor blocking agent"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A fall in DHT with preserved testosterone and LH indicates inhibition of 5-alpha-reductase rather than suppression of testicular androgen output, and a reduction above 90% matches a dual type 1 and type 2 inhibitor; prostate shrinkage and a halved PSA fit this class. A selective type 2 inhibitor lowers serum DHT by about 70%, short of the measured drop. A continuous GnRH agonist lowers LH and testosterone to castrate levels, which did not happen. An androgen receptor blocker does not lower DHT. An alpha-1 blocker relaxes prostatic smooth muscle to improve flow without changing DHT or prostate volume. Sources: Exam 3 lecture notes (SDL 29, finasteride and dutasteride potency and PSA effect); Katzung Pharmacology Ch. 10 (alpha-1 antagonists such as tamsulosin in benign prostatic hyperplasia).","boardPrep":"Step 1: 5-alpha-reductase inhibitors lower DHT without lowering testosterone, whereas GnRH agonists lower both LH and testosterone. COMLEX: These drugs cut serum PSA by about half, so the measured value must be doubled when screening a treated man for prostate cancer.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 -> Obj 4 — the prostate contributes an alkaline fraction of semen","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"An alpha-1 blocker relaxes pro"}},{"id":"ex3t_sdl29_q5","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Intracellular Androgen Receptor Signaling Pathway","batch":3,"stem":"In a cell-based assay, androgen signaling is studied in prostate cells. A mutation is introduced into the zinc-finger region of the androgen receptor, while ligand capture, heat-shock protein release, and nuclear entry all remain normal. Which step of the signaling pathway is most likely to fail?","choices":{"A":"Diffusion of the free hormone across the membrane","B":"Dissociation of chaperone heat-shock proteins","C":"Binding of the dimer to DNA response elements","D":"Translocation of the complex into the nucleus","E":"Enzymatic activation of testosterone to DHT"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The androgen receptor binds DNA through zinc-finger motifs that recognize androgen response elements, so a zinc-finger mutation prevents the receptor dimer from attaching to those DNA sequences even though earlier steps are intact. Diffusion of free hormone across the membrane precedes receptor binding and is not affected. Dissociation of heat-shock proteins is stated to be normal. Nuclear translocation is stated to be normal. Enzymatic activation to DHT occurs before the receptor acts and is unrelated to the DNA-binding domain. Sources: Exam 3 lecture notes (SDL 29, androgen receptor zinc-finger binding to androgen response elements); Costanzo Physiology Ch. 10 (androgen-receptor complex initiates gene transcription in the nucleus).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Steroid receptors use zinc-finger DNA-binding domains to engage hormone response elements, so a zinc-finger mutation blocks transcription with ligand binding intact. COMLEX: A receptor that binds hormone but not DNA still produces androgen resistance despite high circulating androgen.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 -> Obj 1 — DHT binds this same receptor with higher affinity than testosterone","isHighYield":false},{"id":"ex3t_sdl29_q6","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Anabolic Steroid Abuse: HPG Suppression & Azoospermia","batch":3,"stem":"A 27-year-old bodybuilder using high-dose injectable androgens for 2 years has small soft gonads and azoospermia. His serum testosterone is 1400 ng/dL (reference range: 300–1000 ng/dL), LH is 0.2 mIU/mL (reference range: 1.7–8.6 mIU/mL), and FSH is 0.3 mIU/mL (reference range: 1.5–12.4 mIU/mL). Which mechanism best explains his azoospermia?","choices":{"A":"Direct androgen toxicity to the germinal epithelium","B":"Autoimmune destruction of the germ cell lineage","C":"Obstruction of the ejaculatory duct by the androgen","D":"Low intragonadal testosterone from suppressed LH","E":"Failed conversion of testosterone to active estrogen"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Exogenous androgens feed back on the hypothalamus and pituitary to suppress LH and FSH; without LH, Leydig cells stop making testosterone, so intragonadal testosterone collapses far below the high levels that sperm production needs, and spermatogenesis arrests despite a high serum testosterone. Direct germ cell toxicity is not the mechanism; the loss of the local androgen supply is. There is no autoimmune process here. The androgen does not obstruct the ejaculatory duct. Failed aromatization to estrogen does not explain the azoospermia, which follows from loss of LH-driven intragonadal testosterone. Sources: Exam 3 lecture notes (SDL 29, HPG suppression and collapse of intratesticular testosterone in anabolic steroid abuse).","boardPrep":"Step 1: A high serum testosterone with low LH and FSH and small soft gonads is the fingerprint of exogenous androgen use. COMLEX: Sperm production needs intragonadal testosterone far above serum levels, so suppressing LH causes infertility even when serum testosterone is high.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 -> Obj 4 — gonadotropin panels in male hypogonadism","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"E":"Failed aromatization to estrog"}}]};
